e engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"292956ef-ca6e-4f12-960d-50db46840af6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I am drafting a structured goal plan for the implementer, adversarial verifiers, and classifier.\n\nThe objective is to build a game from PLAN.md using TASK.md as the source of truth. TASK.md specifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion as the source material, a title generated by brainstorming 50 options and selecting one at random, a single HTML file named with the title and timestamp, and the riftlight pixel engine.\n\nThe plan must specify outcomes rather than architecture. It should avoid dictating file layouts, class names, or function names. Acceptance criteria will be kept small and grouped by related mechanics. Visual and interactive checks will rely on unit tests for pure logic, browser script load verification, and an entry-point launch check. Additional requirements include art, animation, audio, a dev subfolder, multiplayer via AI Hub, dev and attract modes, exit-interview.json, an About/Credits screen, and PROGRESS.md.\n\nThe objective names an established canon, so the plan must treat PLAN.md as the primary design document. It already contains the researched mechanics and original game design for Trauma Center: Second Opinion.\n\nI will search the web to confirm the defining mechanics of Trauma Center: Second Opinion. PLAN.md already contains an extensive original design and forbids copying characters or names. I am identifying which core mechanics must be preserved in the acceptance criteria.\n\nThe defining mechanics of Trauma Center: Second Opinion are now identified. These include precision surgery with multiple instruments under time pressure, a nurse giving instructions, a time-slowing ability renamed \"The Quiet\" for originality, boss pathogens, visual novel story segments between operations, an S/A/B/C ranking system, and Wii pointer controls adapted to mouse or controller input.\n\nThe acceptance criteria must be capped at three to five tightly grouped gating criteria. The objective requires completing all PLAN.md work that runs on this computer and placing every TASK.md deliverable in the folder. I am now enumerating the specific hand-in items from TASK.md to ensure they fit within this limit.\n\nThe six TASK.md deliverables are now fully enumerated: a dev subfolder, the single HTML file, PROGRESS.md, exit-interview.json, an About/Credits screen, and a chat reply. All of these can be built on this computer using stand-in replacements for physical controllers, touch input, second-player support, iOS, and TV.\n\nThe objective does not name Trauma Center directly. The source is specified only in STAGE.md and TASK.md, so the required web research is optional. PLAN.md already contains the detailed design.\n\nThe deliverable is an original game inspired by Trauma Center, not a direct clone. PLAN.md is the authoritative design document.\n\nA quick parallel search will confirm no defining mechanic from the source is omitted. The criteria must be small and satisficing, never an exhaustive conjunction.\n\nThe first gating criterion is deliverable packaging. It requires a single self-contained HTML file in the work root with the correct filename pattern, an unmodified pixel engine, verbatim hub session client code, valid exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, and an About screen. The title must be chosen via the specified random method.\n\nThe second gating criterion covers the surgical simulation core. It requires a playable action loop with eight instruments, vitals, clock, mistakes, scoring, and ranks. It also requires gifts named The Quiet and The Second Look, Code Blue, and ten operations with their defining mechanics as data-driven content. The bot must clear these at stated skill targets. Pure simulation logic is required; no DOM interaction.\n\nThe third criterion covers presentation, drama, and audio. It requires story scenes through the campaign beats, original cast and tone, gibberish voices, code-only audio with music default at ten percent, controller-first prompts that follow last input including an Xbox option, title menus, pause, settings, and results. It also requires exact touch gate text and attract mode after ten seconds.\n\nThe fourth criterion validates the shell. It requires dev mode from pause with live sliders and reset; attract mode; couch co-op for one to four players; an AI Hub shared-world session that passes harness checks; and standalone execution from file://.\n\nThe fifth criterion is launch and tests. It requires `node tests/run.mjs` to report zero failures covering tools, operations, campaign playthrough, content lint, and browser or hub checks. The built page must boot from file:// with the title and operating field drawn and music volume at 0.1.\n\nThe plan now has five criteria. Each is an independently checkable outcome rather than a holistic end-to-end gate.\n\nCriterion 5 includes a whole-game playthrough, which PLAN.md explicitly requires as a test. The verification plan will produce evidence in {SCRATCH}. Browser checks will use unit tests for pure logic and script load verification in a browser-like environment. The game boots from file:// because PLAN.md uses inline modules rather than external imports.\n\nThe launch check will use Playwright to probe the file:// entry point, assert zero page errors, verify a substantially filled render surface, confirm driven input produces visible change, and save a screenshot to {SCRATCH}. The environment has Playwright available. The AI Hub harness is part of the plan and can run here. File layout is not an acceptance criterion; PLAN.md already prescribes architecture.\n\nThe filename pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040].html` is a literal requirement from TASK.md, so it belongs in the packaging criterion. The SOURCE segment should be the exact string \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\" with its colon and spaces preserved.\n\nThe filename pattern uses two placeholders: GameTitle and SOURCE. SOURCE is the literal string \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\", which contains spaces and colons that are invalid in filenames. The implementer must slug this value when choosing the final title. The plan already states that the output name comes from the brief.\n\nThe filename and dev subfolder will use the chosen title plus the slugified source name, with filesystem-safe characters. The HTML file must sit in the work root, not only inside the dev folder.\n\nThe title must be chosen by silently brainstorming fifty candidates and selecting one with a real random number generator. The plan cannot verify the brainstorm step, but the exit interview and title screen will reveal the final choice.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The brief is a full surgical-drama build. I’ll confirm Trauma Center’s defining mechanics, then fold them into a short outcome plan."},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_d8f3a994-3468-916e-9cb7-faa05a485602_call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-4","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Trauma Center: Second Opinion gameplay mechanics Healing Touch operations vitals instruments ranking GUILT"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_d8f3a994-3468-916e-9cb7-faa05a485602_call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-4","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay","title":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Vitals","title":"https://traumacentergame.fandom.com/wiki/Vitals"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/","title":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/45940","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/45940"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/51560","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/51560"}]},{"type":"server_tool_use","id":"ws_d8f3a994-3468-916e-9cb7-faa05a485602_call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-5","name":"web_search","input":{"query":"Trauma Center Second Opinion defining mechanics Wii surgery tools time limit score rank story operations"}},{"type":"web_search_tool_result","tool_use_id":"ws_d8f3a994-3468-916e-9cb7-faa05a485602_call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-5","content":[{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay","title":"https://strategywiki.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion/Gameplay"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/","title":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion","title":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/46894","title":"https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/wii/933018-trauma-center-second-opinion/faqs/46894"},{"type":"web_search_result","url":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/","title":"https://scathingaccuracy.com/guides/trauma-center-second-opinion-xs-guide/"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Defining mechanics match PLAN.md: timed first-person surgery, distinct tools, vitals, ranks, a nurse, two different gifts, and pathogen bosses, all under original names. I’ll write the outcome contract from that."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-6","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/goal/plan-01a1aa39-3fcd-7432-ad2b-71f4bb480a24.md","content":"# Plan: Build the original surgical-drama game PLAN.md specifies and hand in every TASK.md artifact\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one playable HTML file named `{Title}-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040.html`. `{Title}` is a playful riff on the source name (not \"Trauma Center\" or \"Second Opinion\"), chosen by executing a real RNG over 50 brainstormed titles; the 50 strings and the numeric roll are saved in the dev folder. A subfolder with that same base name holds the sources. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in the exact shape TASK.md gives, with `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` `Simulation`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The HTML starts from `file://` as a single file (no bare module URLs). The kit file `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the downloaded zip, and the hub session client embedded in the page is byte-identical to the fetched `session.js`. In-game About/Credits shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback for every field and question TASK.md lists, matching the JSON. A short authoring note in the dev folder explains how to add an operation, a scene, a strain, and a character.\n\n2. **Surgical sim (the recognizable game).** A pure sim — no engine, DOM, or wall clock — creates an operation and steps seat inputs. It implements the eight tools and their ratings: drain shrinks pools and bruises dry tissue; forceps follow a path (curved hooks, magnet speed limit, tethered cores) and tear when far off the path; scalpel grades deviation and cuts healthy tissue when way off; suture zigzags slot-by-slot and raises pitch; laser burns a target and punishes stray time; scanner reveals and keeps marks; gel completes on coverage and heals nicks; syringe helps only inside the ring. Vitals drain, the clock and a fifth mistake each fail the op, difficulties change time and drain, and score, streak, rank (S/A/B/C versus par), and pristine match PLAN.md. The Quiet slows the sim to one quarter and stops vital loss while composure drains; The Second Look restores sim state from four seconds earlier and does not move the cursor. Code Blue needs two good on-beat shocks to bring vitals back and fails if time runs out. All ten story ops plus Consult Pile-Up are real content, each completable from its own start, including: eleven hooks then a twelfth, swallowed props then a tumour, three live tables and the Quiet awakening, rotated bone fragments, the Introit knock and tether, the transplant's forced rewind and colour change, Lacrimosa's weeping eyes and the Sob, Dies Irae's safe diastole versus sloppy systole plus massage, Libera Me's lying monitor, copy-hand, and two-handed pull, and a non-fighting spine pull that raises both pain and vitals. Player-facing text does not use the forbidden source names (Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki, Deftera, Triti, Tetarti, Pempti, Paraskevi, Savato). On Resident, with par equal to the full-skill bot's own score (not a lowered number, and that run is not god-mode): no input fails every op; skill 0.4 clears ops 1–3; skill 0.7 clears every op at rank B or better, with at least 20% of the clock left on non-boss ops; skill 1.0 ranks S.\n\n3. **Drama, sound, and 10-foot play.** Episodes 1–10 and the ending are straight melodrama in the original cast's voices: at most two lines, at most 60 characters, characters the engine font can draw. Surgery is a fixed first-person close-up; story uses talking-head close-ups and wider room shots. Every note, effect, and voice is synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish keyed to the line; a fresh profile sets music to 0.1. The mouse tip hits the raised tissue surface, not a flat floor. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, key and mouse icons after a key or click. Each pad is read on its own, analog triggers included, and pad buttons do not also press keyboard keys. Screens that exist and work with a pad and with a mouse: title (dark theatre, lamp, EKG, \"press to start\"), main menu, briefing chart, operation HUD (vitals, clock, composure, comm, tool wheel), results (rank stamp and a follow-up that depends on rank), pause, and settings. Touch-only shows the exact text `Controller or keyboard required` and a settings button, draws no joystick, and still runs attract mode. Ten seconds without input on the title or a menu starts a looping bot demo with a visible demo banner; any input leaves at once to the title and does not write saves or settings. Pause can open Dev Mode: live sliders for every variable PLAN.md §12 lists, a reset to those defaults, plus the cheat, jump, action, and view tools in §8.\n\n4. **Alone, on the couch, and in AI Hub.** With no hub session the campaign runs normally (`AIHub.active` is not true). Consult on one machine seats 1–4 local players (keyboard and mouse plus pads) with independent tips and sends no network traffic. In a hub session the game follows shared-world for 1–4 players: card with ready and more-time, host runs the sim, clients send input even when idle, snapshots stay within the size and rate limits in PLAN.md §7, gameplay uses the hub RNG seed rather than `Math.random`, a server copy never starts the engine, and finish standings include every present player once. The page's hub head tags state shared-world, 1–4 players, 4 minutes, WebGPU required, plus the goal and how-to strings from §7. All five harness URLs in PLAN.md §13 end with the harness pass flag true.\n\n5. **Launch shows the surgery, twice.** Two consecutive loads of the root HTML, from `file://` or from a server only if `file://` cannot run it, each show no page errors, a fill-mode drawing surface at that viewport's art size (no letterbox bars), a frame that is substantially painted (not a blank or a few pixels), music at 0.1 on a fresh profile, and a driven drain or forceps use that changes the aimed entity in live sim state. The two runs agree. A browser that fails to start is recorded as launcher failure; a readback that succeeds and is blank is a failed launch, not a skipped check.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. **gating:** Add or update tests under the dev folder that import the shipped sim (not a copy) and assert criterion 2: each tool's success and mistake, Quiet's time scale, Second Look's exact restore, Code Blue success and timeout, rank bounds, slider apply and reset, deterministic same-seed hash, codec round-trip and peak snapshot size, every op failing with no input and clearing with the skill-0.7 bot, and the Resident balance table in criterion 2. Run `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder. It must print only failures and one count line, and exit 0. Save the stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\n2. **gating:** The same command's content checks assert: no `TODO` / placeholder / stub strings, none of the forbidden names, every About field present, story lines within the length and font limits, and a bot playthrough from the first scene through the last op to credits that writes saves and unlocks the log. Those assertions are in the run captured in `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\n3. **gating:** Load the root HTML twice in headless Chromium (WebGPU software flags from PLAN.md §13, fresh profile, viewport 960×540 or 1920×1080). Each run: zero page errors, `engine` GPU error list empty, canvas art size matches fill-mode for that viewport, painted coverage is most of the frame, music volume is 0.1, and a scripted drain or forceps action at a known target changes that entity's sim state. Screenshot each run to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and write the read state to `{SCRATCH}/launch.txt`. If Chromium cannot start, save the launcher's own error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt` and treat steps 1–2 as the bar. Do not treat a blank successful screenshot as this fallback.\n4. **gating:** Browser tests, still via the one test command or a filter it documents, assert: pad-stub, keyboard, and mouse each move a menu focus (hover moves focus); prompts swap when the device changes and the Xbox setting swaps glyphs; attract starts after 10 idle seconds and a key or pad exits without touching the save; a Pixel 7 profile shows `Controller or keyboard required`, the settings control, no joystick, and attract running; pause Dev Mode changes a live slider and reset restores it; two stub pads produce two independent tips. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.txt`.\n5. **gating:** Serve the built page and the unmodified hub harness with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then run the five PLAN.md §13 harness URLs. Each must reach `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Stop that server by its PID. Save results to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`.\n6. **gating:** Hash `pixel-engine.js` against the zip and the embedded session client against the fetched file; both equal. `exit-interview.json` parses and matches criterion 1. Record hashes and `python3 -m json.tool` output in `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\n7. **evidence:** Screenshots at 1920×1080 of the title, each arena, a bust, the HUD, and the results stamp, saved under `{SCRATCH}/`. Look for a readable 10-foot layout. Also render one voice and one effect in an offline audio context and record that the buffer is finite and not silent (`{SCRATCH}/audio.txt`).\n\n## Non-goals\n- A port of *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*: no source characters, organisations, GUILT strains, Healing Touch name, star gesture, operations, script, art, music, or screen layouts.\n- FPS targets, optimization, and the optional live OR monitor and scrub-room mirror (PLAN.md's first allowed cut).\n- Physical DualSense hardware, real speakers, a second human, real iOS Safari, or a TV. Use the pad stub, offline audio, two pages, WebKit iPhone profile, and 1920×1080 shots.\n- Online play other than local couch Consult and AI Hub shared-world. No chat, accounts, or extra sockets.\n\n## Assumed scope\n- Work root: `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work` (PLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md already there). New dev subfolder, root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` only.\n- Engine kit: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip`, used unmodified. Hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`, session client unmodified.\n- Playwright 1.63 is already on this machine. `npm install playwright@1.63.0` inside the dev folder is allowed; do not run `playwright install`.\n- Do not read or write sibling agent folders. Do not kill processes you did not start.\n- Defining mechanics were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki (timed first-person surgery, vitals and clock fails, distinct tools, zigzag suture, precision ranks, nurse prompts, one doctor who slows time and another whose gift differs, defibrillator, pathogen bosses, story between ops). PLAN.md's original names and hold-to-activate gifts replace the source IP on purpose; that is in scope, a star gesture is not.\n\n## Implementation approach\nKeep the operation rules as plain data plus pure functions so Node can step them with no browser. The view, HUD, and audio only read that state. One bot driver serves attract mode, the partner hand, balance, and the campaign test. Build in PLAN.md §15 order so something is playable early. Bundle last into the single root HTML; do not minify or rewrite the hub session client. Inline the script so `file://` works. The module split in PLAN.md §11 is guidance, not a required layout.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Download the kit and hub files, create the named dev folder, boot an empty scene from `file://`, and add the test runner plus the fresh-profile music default of 0.1.\n- [ ] Implement the pure sim and Operation 1 (drain, forceps, suture, gel, syringe) with the tissue view, hand, and HUD; test those tools.\n- [ ] Add scalpel, laser, scanner, operations 2 and 3, Code Blue, The Quiet, and the bot; assert idle-fail and skill-0.7 clear.\n- [ ] Add story scenes, gibberish voices, music, results, and saves so episodes 1–3 play through.\n- [ ] Add title, menus, last-device prompts, settings, pause, Dev Mode sliders, attract mode, the touch gate, and About pages.\n- [ ] Add the remaining operations, The Second Look, all strains, and the campaign playthrough test.\n- [ ] Add couch Consult, then the hub session, card, and net code; run all five harness scenarios.\n- [ ] Tune to the balance table, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, emit the root HTML, and save a green `node tests/run.mjs` log plus the two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- The zip may be newer than engine 0.46.0, which PLAN.md's spikes used. Read `CHANGELOG.md` and `GUIDE.md`. Do not edit the engine; adapt the game if an API moved.\n- Several agents share this machine. Bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and stop only that PID. earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory runs out; keep one or two browsers open.\n- The HTML filename depends on the RNG title. Write the chosen name in `PROGRESS.md` and `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\n- Skill-1.0 rank S is defined by setting par from that bot's score. A balance run that uses god mode, or a par set below that score, does not pass criterion 2.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":67753,"output_tokens":17091,"cache_read_input_tokens":16896,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"11d64286-969b-4efa-be7d-6f2ea7dec514"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-728ff05e-562f-4474-8ae2-2f7ae0b98bfe-6","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-drama game PLAN.md specifies and hand in every TASK.md artifact\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one playable HTML file named `{Title}-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040.html`. `{Title}` is a playful riff on the source name (not \\\"Trauma Center\\\" or \\\"Second Opinion\\\"), chosen by executing a real RNG over 50 brainstormed titles; the 50 strings and the numeric roll are saved in the dev folder. A subfolder with that same base name holds the sources. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in the exact shape TASK.md gives, with `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` `Simulation`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The HTML starts from `file://` as a single file (no bare module URLs). The kit file `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the downloaded zip, and the hub session client embedded in the page is byte-identical to the fetched `session.js`. In-game About/Credits shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback for every field and question TASK.md lists, matching the JSON. A short authoring note in the dev folder explains how to add an operation, a scene, a strain, and a character.\\n\\n2. **Surgical sim (the recognizable game).** A pure sim — no engine, DOM, or wall clock — creates an operation and steps seat inputs. It implements the eight tools and their ratings: drain shrinks pools and bruises dry tissue; forceps follow a path (curved hooks, magnet speed limit, tethered cores) and tear when far off the path; scalpel grades deviation and cuts healthy tissue when way off; suture zigzags slot-by-slot and raises pitch; laser burns a target and punishes stray time; scanner reveals and keeps marks; gel completes on coverage and heals nicks; syringe helps only inside the ring. Vitals drain, the clock and a fifth mistake each fail the op, difficulties change time and drain, and score, streak, rank (S/A/B/C versus par), and pristine match PLAN.md. The Quiet slows the sim to one quarter and stops vital loss while composure drains; The Second Look restores sim state from four seconds earlier and does not move the cursor. Code Blue needs two good on-beat shocks to bring vitals back and fails if time runs out. All ten story ops plus Consult Pile-Up are real content, each completable from its own start, including: eleven hooks then a twelfth, swallowed props then a tumour, three live tables and the Quiet awakening, rotated bone fragments, the Introit knock and tether, the transplant's forced rewind and colour change, Lacrimosa's weeping eyes and the Sob, Dies Irae's safe diastole versus sloppy systole plus massage, Libera Me's lying monitor, copy-hand, and two-handed pull, and a non-fighting spine pull that raises both pain and vitals. Player-facing text does not use the forbidden source names (Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki, Deftera, Triti, Tetarti, Pempti, Paraskevi, Savato). On Resident, with par equal to the full-skill bot's own score (not a lowered number, and that run is not god-mode): no input fails every op; skill 0.4 clears ops 1–3; skill 0.7 clears every op at rank B or better, with at least 20% of the clock left on non-boss ops; skill 1.0 ranks S.\\n\\n3. **Drama, sound, and 10-foot play.** Episodes 1–10 and the ending are straight melodrama in the original cast's voices: at most two lines, at most 60 characters, characters the engine font can draw. Surgery is a fixed first-person close-up; story uses talking-head close-ups and wider room shots. Every note, effect, and voice is synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish keyed to the line; a fresh profile sets music to 0.1. The mouse tip hits the raised tissue surface, not a flat floor. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, key and mouse icons after a key or click. Each pad is read on its own, analog triggers included, and pad buttons do not also press keyboard keys. Screens that exist and work with a pad and with a mouse: title (dark theatre, lamp, EKG, \\\"press to start\\\"), main menu, briefing chart, operation HUD (vitals, clock, composure, comm, tool wheel), results (rank stamp and a follow-up that depends on rank), pause, and settings. Touch-only shows the exact text `Controller or keyboard required` and a settings button, draws no joystick, and still runs attract mode. Ten seconds without input on the title or a menu starts a looping bot demo with a visible demo banner; any input leaves at once to the title and does not write saves or settings. Pause can open Dev Mode: live sliders for every variable PLAN.md §12 lists, a reset to those defaults, plus the cheat, jump, action, and view tools in §8.\\n\\n4. **Alone, on the couch, and in AI Hub.** With no hub session the campaign runs normally (`AIHub.active` is not true). Consult on one machine seats 1–4 local players (keyboard and mouse plus pads) with independent tips and sends no network traffic. In a hub session the game follows shared-world for 1–4 players: card with ready and more-time, host runs the sim, clients send input even when idle, snapshots stay within the size and rate limits in PLAN.md §7, gameplay uses the hub RNG seed rather than `Math.random`, a server copy never starts the engine, and finish standings include every present player once. The page's hub head tags state shared-world, 1–4 players, 4 minutes, WebGPU required, plus the goal and how-to strings from §7. All five harness URLs in PLAN.md §13 end with the harness pass flag true.\\n\\n5. **Launch shows the surgery, twice.** Two consecutive loads of the root HTML, from `file://` or from a server only if `file://` cannot run it, each show no page errors, a fill-mode drawing surface at that viewport's art size (no letterbox bars), a frame that is substantially painted (not a blank or a few pixels), music at 0.1 on a fresh profile, and a driven drain or forceps use that changes the aimed entity in live sim state. The two runs agree. A browser that fails to start is recorded as launcher failure; a readback that succeeds and is blank is a failed launch, not a skipped check.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add or update tests under the dev folder that import the shipped sim (not a copy) and assert criterion 2: each tool's success and mistake, Quiet's time scale, Second Look's exact restore, Code Blue success and timeout, rank bounds, slider apply and reset, deterministic same-seed hash, codec round-trip and peak snapshot size, every op failing with no input and clearing with the skill-0.7 bot, and the Resident balance table in criterion 2. Run `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder. It must print only failures and one count line, and exit 0. Save the stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n2. **gating:** The same command's content checks assert: no `TODO` / placeholder / stub strings, none of the forbidden names, every About field present, story lines within the length and font limits, and a bot playthrough from the first scene through the last op to credits that writes saves and unlocks the log. Those assertions are in the run captured in `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n3. **gating:** Load the root HTML twice in headless Chromium (WebGPU software flags from PLAN.md §13, fresh profile, viewport 960×540 or 1920×1080). Each run: zero page errors, `engine` GPU error list empty, canvas art size matches fill-mode for that viewport, painted coverage is most of the frame, music volume is 0.1, and a scripted drain or forceps action at a known target changes that entity's sim state. Screenshot each run to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and write the read state to `{SCRATCH}/launch.txt`. If Chromium cannot start, save the launcher's own error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt` and treat steps 1–2 as the bar. Do not treat a blank successful screenshot as this fallback.\\n4. **gating:** Browser tests, still via the one test command or a filter it documents, assert: pad-stub, keyboard, and mouse each move a menu focus (hover moves focus); prompts swap when the device changes and the Xbox setting swaps glyphs; attract starts after 10 idle seconds and a key or pad exits without touching the save; a Pixel 7 profile shows `Controller or keyboard required`, the settings control, no joystick, and attract running; pause Dev Mode changes a live slider and reset restores it; two stub pads produce two independent tips. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.txt`.\\n5. **gating:** Serve the built page and the unmodified hub harness with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then run the five PLAN.md §13 harness URLs. Each must reach `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Stop that server by its PID. Save results to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`.\\n6. **gating:** Hash `pixel-engine.js` against the zip and the embedded session client against the fetched file; both equal. `exit-interview.json` parses and matches criterion 1. Record hashes and `python3 -m json.tool` output in `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n7. **evidence:** Screenshots at 1920×1080 of the title, each arena, a bust, the HUD, and the results stamp, saved under `{SCRATCH}/`. Look for a readable 10-foot layout. Also render one voice and one effect in an offline audio context and record that the buffer is finite and not silent (`{SCRATCH}/audio.txt`).\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A port of *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*: no source characters, organisations, GUILT strains, Healing Touch name, star gesture, operations, script, art, music, or screen layouts.\\n- FPS targets, optimization, and the optional live OR monitor and scrub-room mirror (PLAN.md's first allowed cut).\\n- Physical DualSense hardware, real speakers, a second human, real iOS Safari, or a TV. Use the pad stub, offline audio, two pages, WebKit iPhone profile, and 1920×1080 shots.\\n- Online play other than local couch Consult and AI Hub shared-world. No chat, accounts, or extra sockets.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work` (PLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md already there). New dev subfolder, root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` only.\\n- Engine kit: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip`, used unmodified. Hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`, session client unmodified.\\n- Playwright 1.63 is already on this machine. `npm install playwright@1.63.0` inside the dev folder is allowed; do not run `playwright install`.\\n- Do not read or write sibling agent folders. Do not kill processes you did not start.\\n- Defining mechanics were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki (timed first-person surgery, vitals and clock fails, distinct tools, zigzag suture, precision ranks, nurse prompts, one doctor who slows time and another whose gift differs, defibrillator, pathogen bosses, story between ops). PLAN.md's original names and hold-to-activate gifts replace the source IP on purpose; that is in scope, a star gesture is not.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep the operation rules as plain data plus pure functions so Node can step them with no browser. The view, HUD, and audio only read that state. One bot driver serves attract mode, the partner hand, balance, and the campaign test. Build in PLAN.md §15 order so something is playable early. Bundle last into the single root HTML; do not minify or rewrite the hub session client. Inline the script so `file://` works. The module split in PLAN.md §11 is guidance, not a required layout.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Download the kit and hub files, create the named dev folder, boot an empty scene from `file://`, and add the test runner plus the fresh-profile music default of 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement the pure sim and Operation 1 (drain, forceps, suture, gel, syringe) with the tissue view, hand, and HUD; test those tools.\\n- [ ] Add scalpel, laser, scanner, operations 2 and 3, Code Blue, The Quiet, and the bot; assert idle-fail and skill-0.7 clear.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, gibberish voices, music, results, and saves so episodes 1–3 play through.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, last-device prompts, settings, pause, Dev Mode sliders, attract mode, the touch gate, and About pages.\\n- [ ] Add the remaining operations, The Second Look, all strains, and the campaign playthrough test.\\n- [ ] Add couch Consult, then the hub session, card, and net code; run all five harness scenarios.\\n- [ ] Tune to the balance table, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, emit the root HTML, and save a green `node tests/run.mjs` log plus the two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- The zip may be newer than engine 0.46.0, which PLAN.md's spikes used. Read `CHANGELOG.md` and `GUIDE.md`. Do not edit the engine; adapt the game if an API moved.\\n- Several agents share this machine. Bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and stop only that PID. earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory runs out; keep one or two browsers open.\\n- The HTML filename depends on the RNG title. Write the chosen name in `PROGRESS.md` and `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n- Skill-1.0 rank S is defined by setting par from that bot's score. A balance run that uses god mode, or a par set below that score, does not pass criterion 2.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/goal/plan-01a1aa39-3fcd-7432-ad2b-71f4bb480a24.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/goal/plan-01a1aa39-3fcd-7432-ad2b-71f4bb480a24.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/goal/plan-01a1aa39-3fcd-7432-ad2b-71f4bb480a24.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-drama game PLAN.md specifies and hand in every TASK.md artifact\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one playable HTML file named `{Title}-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040.html`. `{Title}` is a playful riff on the source name (not \\\"Trauma Center\\\" or \\\"Second Opinion\\\"), chosen by executing a real RNG over 50 brainstormed titles; the 50 strings and the numeric roll are saved in the dev folder. A subfolder with that same base name holds the sources. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in the exact shape TASK.md gives, with `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` `Simulation`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The HTML starts from `file://` as a single file (no bare module URLs). The kit file `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the downloaded zip, and the hub session client embedded in the page is byte-identical to the fetched `session.js`. In-game About/Credits 