sue surface, not a flat floor. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, key and mouse icons after a key or click. Each pad is read on its own, analog triggers included, and pad buttons do not also press keyboard keys. Screens that exist and work with a pad and with a mouse: title (dark theatre, lamp, EKG, \\\"press to start\\\"), main menu, briefing chart, operation HUD (vitals, clock, composure, comm, tool wheel), results (rank stamp and a follow-up that depends on rank), pause, and settings. Touch-only shows the exact text `Controller or keyboard required` and a settings button, draws no joystick, and still runs attract mode. Ten seconds without input on the title or a menu starts a looping bot demo with a visible demo banner; any input leaves at once to the title and does not write saves or settings. Pause can open Dev Mode: live sliders for every variable PLAN.md §12 lists, a reset to those defaults, plus the cheat, jump, action, and view tools in §8.\\n\\n4. **Alone, on the couch, and in AI Hub.** With no hub session the campaign runs normally (`AIHub.active` is not true). Consult on one machine seats 1–4 local players (keyboard and mouse plus pads) with independent tips and sends no network traffic. In a hub session the game follows shared-world for 1–4 players: card with ready and more-time, host runs the sim, clients send input even when idle, snapshots stay within the size and rate limits in PLAN.md §7, gameplay uses the hub RNG seed rather than `Math.random`, a server copy never starts the engine, and finish standings include every present player once. The page's hub head tags state shared-world, 1–4 players, 4 minutes, WebGPU required, plus the goal and how-to strings from §7. All five harness URLs in PLAN.md §13 end with the harness pass flag true.\\n\\n5. **Launch shows the surgery, twice.** Two consecutive loads of the root HTML, from `file://` or from a server only if `file://` cannot run it, each show no page errors, a fill-mode drawing surface at that viewport's art size (no letterbox bars), a frame that is substantially painted (not a blank or a few pixels), music at 0.1 on a fresh profile, and a driven drain or forceps use that changes the aimed entity in live sim state. The two runs agree. A browser that fails to start is recorded as launcher failure; a readback that succeeds and is blank is a failed launch, not a skipped check.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add or update tests under the dev folder that import the shipped sim (not a copy) and assert criterion 2: each tool's success and mistake, Quiet's time scale, Second Look's exact restore, Code Blue success and timeout, rank bounds, slider apply and reset, deterministic same-seed hash, codec round-trip and peak snapshot size, every op failing with no input and clearing with the skill-0.7 bot, and the Resident balance table in criterion 2. Run `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder. It must print only failures and one count line, and exit 0. Save the stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n2. **gating:** The same command's content checks assert: no `TODO` / placeholder / stub strings, none of the forbidden names, every About field present, story lines within the length and font limits, and a bot playthrough from the first scene through the last op to credits that writes saves and unlocks the log. Those assertions are in the run captured in `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n20→3. **gating:** Load the root HTML twice in headless Chromium (WebGPU software flags from PLAN.md §13, fresh profile, viewport 960×540 or 1920×1080). Each run: zero page errors, `engine` GPU error list empty, canvas art size matches fill-mode for that viewport, painted coverage is most of the frame, music volume is 0.1, and a scripted drain or forceps action at a known target changes that entity's sim state. Screenshot each run to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and write the read state to `{SCRATCH}/launch.txt`. If Chromium cannot start, save the launcher's own error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt` and treat steps 1–2 as the bar. Do not treat a blank successful screenshot as this fallback.\\n4. **gating:** Browser tests, still via the one test command or a filter it documents, assert: pad-stub, keyboard, and mouse each move a menu focus (hover moves focus); prompts swap when the device changes and the Xbox setting swaps glyphs; attract starts after 10 idle seconds and a key or pad exits without touching the save; a Pixel 7 profile shows `Controller or keyboard required`, the settings control, no joystick, and attract running; pause Dev Mode changes a live slider and reset restores it; two stub pads produce two independent tips. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.txt`.\\n5. **gating:** Serve the built page and the unmodified hub harness with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then run the five PLAN.md §13 harness URLs. Each must reach `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Stop that server by its PID. Save results to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`.\\n6. **gating:** Hash `pixel-engine.js` against the zip and the embedded session client against the fetched file; both equal. `exit-interview.json` parses and matches criterion 1. Record hashes and `python3 -m json.tool` output in `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n7. **evidence:** Screenshots at 1920×1080 of the title, each arena, a bust, the HUD, and the results stamp, saved under `{SCRATCH}/`. Look for a readable 10-foot layout. Also render one voice and one effect in an offline audio context and record that the buffer is finite and not silent (`{SCRATCH}/audio.txt`).\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A port of *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*: no source characters, organisations, GUILT strains, Healing Touch name, star gesture, operations, script, art, music, or screen layouts.\\n- FPS targets, optimization, and the optional live OR monitor and scrub-room mirror (PLAN.md's first allowed cut).\\n- Physical DualSense hardware, real speakers, a second human, real iOS Safari, or a TV. Use the pad stub, offline audio, two pages, WebKit iPhone profile, and 1920×1080 shots.\\n30→- Online play other than local couch Consult and AI Hub shared-world. No chat, accounts, or extra sockets.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work` (PLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md already there). New dev subfolder, root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` only.\\n- Engine kit: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip`, used unmodified. Hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`, session client unmodified.\\n- Playwright 1.63 is already on this machine. `npm install playwright@1.63.0` inside the dev folder is allowed; do not run `playwright install`.\\n- Do not read or write sibling agent folders. Do not kill processes you did not start.\\n- Defining mechanics were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki (timed first-person surgery, vitals and clock fails, distinct tools, zigzag suture, precision ranks, nurse prompts, one doctor who slows time and another whose gift differs, defibrillator, pathogen bosses, story between ops). PLAN.md's original names and hold-to-activate gifts replace the source IP on purpose; that is in scope, a star gesture is not.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\n40→Keep the operation rules as plain data plus pure functions so Node can step them with no browser. The view, HUD, and audio only read that state. One bot driver serves attract mode, the partner hand, balance, and the campaign test. Build in PLAN.md §15 order so something is playable early. Bundle last into the single root HTML; do not minify or rewrite the hub session client. Inline the script so `file://` works. The module split in PLAN.md §11 is guidance, not a required layout.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Download the kit and hub files, create the named dev folder, boot an empty scene from `file://`, and add the test runner plus the fresh-profile music default of 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement the pure sim and Operation 1 (drain, forceps, suture, gel, syringe) with the tissue view, hand, and HUD; test those tools.\\n- [ ] Add scalpel, laser, scanner, operations 2 and 3, Code Blue, The Quiet, and the bot; assert idle-fail and skill-0.7 clear.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, gibberish voices, music, results, and saves so episodes 1–3 play through.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, last-device prompts, settings, pause, Dev Mode sliders, attract mode, the touch gate, and About pages.\\n- [ ] Add the remaining operations, The Second Look, all strains, and the campaign playthrough test.\\n- [ ] Add couch Consult, then the hub session, card, and net code; run all five harness scenarios.\\n50→- [ ] Tune to the balance table, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, emit the root HTML, and save a green `node tests/run.mjs` log plus the two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- The zip may be newer than engine 0.46.0, which PLAN.md's spikes used. Read `CHANGELOG.md` and `GUIDE.md`. Do not edit the engine; adapt the game if an API moved.\\n- Several agents share this machine. Bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and stop only that PID. earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory runs out; keep one or two browsers open.\\n- The HTML filename depends on the RNG title. Write the chosen name in `PROGRESS.md` and `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n- Skill-1.0 rank S is defined by setting par from that bot's score. A balance run that uses god mode, or a par set below that score, does not pass criterion 2.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build the original surgical-drama game PLAN.md specifies and hand in every TASK.md artifact\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-in package.** The work-folder root contains `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json`, and one playable HTML file named `{Title}-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040.html`. `{Title}` is a playful riff on the source name (not \\\"Trauma Center\\\" or \\\"Second Opinion\\\"), chosen by executing a real RNG over 50 brainstormed titles; the 50 strings and the numeric roll are saved in the dev folder. A subfolder with that same base name holds the sources. `exit-interview.json` parses as one object in the exact shape TASK.md gives, with `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.genre` `Simulation`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, and every other value filled (`unknown` only where it cannot be confirmed). The HTML starts from `file://` as a single file (no bare module URLs). The kit file `pixel-engine.js` is byte-identical to the downloaded zip, and the hub session client embedded in the page is byte-identical to the fetched `session.js`. In-game About/Credits shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback for every field and question TASK.md lists, matching the JSON. A short authoring note in the dev folder explains how to add an operation, a scene, a strain, and a character.\\n\\n2. **Surgical sim (the recognizable game).** A pure sim — no engine, DOM, or wall clock — creates an operation and steps seat inputs. It implements the eight tools and their ratings: drain shrinks pools and bruises dry tissue; forceps follow a path (curved hooks, magnet speed limit, tethered cores) and tear when far off the path; scalpel grades deviation and cuts healthy tissue when way off; suture zigzags slot-by-slot and raises pitch; laser burns a target and punishes stray time; scanner reveals and keeps marks; gel completes on coverage and heals nicks; syringe helps only inside the ring. Vitals drain, the clock and a fifth mistake each fail the op, difficulties change time and drain, and score, streak, rank (S/A/B/C versus par), and pristine match PLAN.md. The Quiet slows the sim to one quarter and stops vital loss while composure drains; The Second Look restores sim state from four seconds earlier and does not move the cursor. Code Blue needs two good on-beat shocks to bring vitals back and fails if time runs out. All ten story ops plus Consult Pile-Up are real content, each completable from its own start, including: eleven hooks then a twelfth, swallowed props then a tumour, three live tables and the Quiet awakening, rotated bone fragments, the Introit knock and tether, the transplant's forced rewind and colour change, Lacrimosa's weeping eyes and the Sob, Dies Irae's safe diastole versus sloppy systole plus massage, Libera Me's lying monitor, copy-hand, and two-handed pull, and a non-fighting spine pull that raises both pain and vitals. Player-facing text does not use the forbidden source names (Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki, Deftera, Triti, Tetarti, Pempti, Paraskevi, Savato). On Resident, with par equal to the full-skill bot's own score (not a lowered number, and that run is not god-mode): no input fails every op; skill 0.4 clears ops 1–3; skill 0.7 clears every op at rank B or better, with at least 20% of the clock left on non-boss ops; skill 1.0 ranks S.\\n\\n3. **Drama, sound, and 10-foot play.** Episodes 1–10 and the ending are straight melodrama in the original cast's voices: at most two lines, at most 60 characters, characters the engine font can draw. Surgery is a fixed first-person close-up; story uses talking-head close-ups and wider room shots. Every note, effect, and voice is synthesized in code (no audio files); voices are gibberish keyed to the line; a fresh profile sets music to 0.1. The mouse tip hits the raised tissue surface, not a flat floor. Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox glyphs when that setting is on, key and mouse icons after a key or click. Each pad is read on its own, analog triggers included, and pad buttons do not also press keyboard keys. Screens that exist and work with a pad and with a mouse: title (dark theatre, lamp, EKG, \\\"press to start\\\"), main menu, briefing chart, operation HUD (vitals, clock, composure, comm, tool wheel), results (rank stamp and a follow-up that depends on rank), pause, and settings. Touch-only shows the exact text `Controller or keyboard required` and a settings button, draws no joystick, and still runs attract mode. Ten seconds without input on the title or a menu starts a looping bot demo with a visible demo banner; any input leaves at once to the title and does not write saves or settings. Pause can open Dev Mode: live sliders for every variable PLAN.md §12 lists, a reset to those defaults, plus the cheat, jump, action, and view tools in §8.\\n\\n4. **Alone, on the couch, and in AI Hub.** With no hub session the campaign runs normally (`AIHub.active` is not true). Consult on one machine seats 1–4 local players (keyboard and mouse plus pads) with independent tips and sends no network traffic. In a hub session the game follows shared-world for 1–4 players: card with ready and more-time, host runs the sim, clients send input even when idle, snapshots stay within the size and rate limits in PLAN.md §7, gameplay uses the hub RNG seed rather than `Math.random`, a server copy never starts the engine, and finish standings include every present player once. The page's hub head tags state shared-world, 1–4 players, 4 minutes, WebGPU required, plus the goal and how-to strings from §7. All five harness URLs in PLAN.md §13 end with the harness pass flag true.\\n\\n5. **Launch shows the surgery, twice.** Two consecutive loads of the root HTML, from `file://` or from a server only if `file://` cannot run it, each show no page errors, a fill-mode drawing surface at that viewport's art size (no letterbox bars), a frame that is substantially painted (not a blank or a few pixels), music at 0.1 on a fresh profile, and a driven drain or forceps use that changes the aimed entity in live sim state. The two runs agree. A browser that fails to start is recorded as launcher failure; a readback that succeeds and is blank is a failed launch, not a skipped check.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. **gating:** Add or update tests under the dev folder that import the shipped sim (not a copy) and assert criterion 2: each tool's success and mistake, Quiet's time scale, Second Look's exact restore, Code Blue success and timeout, rank bounds, slider apply and reset, deterministic same-seed hash, codec round-trip and peak snapshot size, every op failing with no input and clearing with the skill-0.7 bot, and the Resident balance table in criterion 2. Run `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder. It must print only failures and one count line, and exit 0. Save the stdout to `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n2. **gating:** The same command's content checks assert: no `TODO` / placeholder / stub strings, none of the forbidden names, every About field present, story lines within the length and font limits, and a bot playthrough from the first scene through the last op to credits that writes saves and unlocks the log. Those assertions are in the run captured in `{SCRATCH}/tests.txt`.\\n3. **gating:** Load the root HTML twice in headless Chromium (WebGPU software flags from PLAN.md §13, fresh profile, viewport 960×540 or 1920×1080). Each run: zero page errors, `engine` GPU error list empty, canvas art size matches fill-mode for that viewport, painted coverage is most of the frame, music volume is 0.1, and a scripted drain or forceps action at a known target changes that entity's sim state. Screenshot each run to `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and write the read state to `{SCRATCH}/launch.txt`. If Chromium cannot start, save the launcher's own error to `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.txt` and treat steps 1–2 as the bar. Do not treat a blank successful screenshot as this fallback.\\n4. **gating:** Browser tests, still via the one test command or a filter it documents, assert: pad-stub, keyboard, and mouse each move a menu focus (hover moves focus); prompts swap when the device changes and the Xbox setting swaps glyphs; attract starts after 10 idle seconds and a key or pad exits without touching the save; a Pixel 7 profile shows `Controller or keyboard required`, the settings control, no joystick, and attract running; pause Dev Mode changes a live slider and reset restores it; two stub pads produce two independent tips. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/browser.txt`.\\n5. **gating:** Serve the built page and the unmodified hub harness with `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then run the five PLAN.md §13 harness URLs. Each must reach `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. Stop that server by its PID. Save results to `{SCRATCH}/hub.txt`.\\n6. **gating:** Hash `pixel-engine.js` against the zip and the embedded session client against the fetched file; both equal. `exit-interview.json` parses and matches criterion 1. Record hashes and `python3 -m json.tool` output in `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n7. **evidence:** Screenshots at 1920×1080 of the title, each arena, a bust, the HUD, and the results stamp, saved under `{SCRATCH}/`. Look for a readable 10-foot layout. Also render one voice and one effect in an offline audio context and record that the buffer is finite and not silent (`{SCRATCH}/audio.txt`).\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- A port of *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*: no source characters, organisations, GUILT strains, Healing Touch name, star gesture, operations, script, art, music, or screen layouts.\\n- FPS targets, optimization, and the optional live OR monitor and scrub-room mirror (PLAN.md's first allowed cut).\\n- Physical DualSense hardware, real speakers, a second human, real iOS Safari, or a TV. Use the pad stub, offline audio, two pages, WebKit iPhone profile, and 1920×1080 shots.\\n- Online play other than local couch Consult and AI Hub shared-world. No chat, accounts, or extra sockets.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work root: `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work` (PLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md already there). New dev subfolder, root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, `exit-interview.json` only.\\n- Engine kit: `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip`, used unmodified. Hub client and harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`, session client unmodified.\\n- Playwright 1.63 is already on this machine. `npm install playwright@1.63.0` inside the dev folder is allowed; do not run `playwright install`.\\n- Do not read or write sibling agent folders. Do not kill processes you did not start.\\n- Defining mechanics were checked against Wikipedia and StrategyWiki (timed first-person surgery, vitals and clock fails, distinct tools, zigzag suture, precision ranks, nurse prompts, one doctor who slows time and another whose gift differs, defibrillator, pathogen bosses, story between ops). PLAN.md's original names and hold-to-activate gifts replace the source IP on purpose; that is in scope, a star gesture is not.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nKeep the operation rules as plain data plus pure functions so Node can step them with no browser. The view, HUD, and audio only read that state. One bot driver serves attract mode, the partner hand, balance, and the campaign test. Build in PLAN.md §15 order so something is playable early. Bundle last into the single root HTML; do not minify or rewrite the hub session client. Inline the script so `file://` works. The module split in PLAN.md §11 is guidance, not a required layout.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Download the kit and hub files, create the named dev folder, boot an empty scene from `file://`, and add the test runner plus the fresh-profile music default of 0.1.\\n- [ ] Implement the pure sim and Operation 1 (drain, forceps, suture, gel, syringe) with the tissue view, hand, and HUD; test those tools.\\n- [ ] Add scalpel, laser, scanner, operations 2 and 3, Code Blue, The Quiet, and the bot; assert idle-fail and skill-0.7 clear.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, gibberish voices, music, results, and saves so episodes 1–3 play through.\\n- [ ] Add title, menus, last-device prompts, settings, pause, Dev Mode sliders, attract mode, the touch gate, and About pages.\\n- [ ] Add the remaining operations, The Second Look, all strains, and the campaign playthrough test.\\n- [ ] Add couch Consult, then the hub session, card, and net code; run all five harness scenarios.\\n- [ ] Tune to the balance table, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, emit the root HTML, and save a green `node tests/run.mjs` log plus the two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- The zip may be newer than engine 0.46.0, which PLAN.md's spikes used. Read `CHANGELOG.md` and `GUIDE.md`. Do not edit the engine; adapt the game if an API moved.\\n- Several agents share this machine. Bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and stop only that PID. earlyoom kills the largest browser when memory runs out; keep one or two browsers open.\\n- The HTML filename depends on the RNG title. Write the chosen name in `PROGRESS.md` and `{SCRATCH}/package.txt`.\\n- Skill-1.0 rank S is defined by setting par from that bot's score. A balance run that uses god mode, or a par set below that score, does not pass criterion 2.\\n\",\"total_lines\":56}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-0220a019-ebfa-419c-8549-1460b9faee4b-1","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-0220a019-ebfa-419c-8549-1460b9faee4b-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ListDir\",\"Content\":{\"content\":\"- /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/\\n  - PLAN.md\\n  - STAGE.md\\n  - TASK.md\",\"absolute_root_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"fcca395d-f837-4a12-a911-66fc6a35357d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The plan is a full original surgical sim with a hand-in package. I'll read PLAN.md and TASK.md next so the build matches the spec."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e087acce-02d8-4db7-9c2c-6a2f60cd4202-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/TASK.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e087acce-02d8-4db7-9c2c-6a2f60cd4202-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3713,"output_tokens":169,"cache_read_input_tokens":21376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"8007907f-1135-4576-a0fb-b178279d1ef4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e087acce-02d8-4db7-9c2c-6a2f60cd4202-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:40:40\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For 