107,32,105,116,32,105,115,32,116,104,101,32,115,99,114,101,101,110,39,115,32,99,101,110,116,114,101,32,40,116,104,101,32,99,114,111,115,115,104,97,105,114,41,46,10,50,52,54,54,58,112,101,114,115,105,115,116,101,100,32,105,110,32,108,111,99,97,108,83,116,111,114,97,103,101,46,32,65,118,97,105,108,97,98,108,101,32,97,115,32,96,99,116,120,46,97,117,100,105,111,96,46,10,50,55,56,54,58,45,32,96,109,101,97,115,117,114,101,40,116,101,120,116,58,32,115,116,114,105,110,103,44,32,115,99,97,108,101,32,61,32,49,41,58,32,123,32,119,105,100,116,104,58,32,110,117,109,98,101,114,59,32,104,101,105,103,104,116,58,32,110,117,109,98,101,114,32,125,96,58,32,83,105,122,101,32,111,102,32,96,116,101,120,116,96,32,105,110,32,97,114,116,32,112,105,120,101,108,115,32,97,116,32,96,115,99,97,108,101,96,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":4,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":594,\"content\":\"- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\"},{\"line_number\":2304,\"content\":\"- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\"},{\"line_number\":2466,\"content\":\"persisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\"},{\"line_number\":2786,\"content\":\"- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"9abed8b9-0633-4166-a2c7-8dcc3d07b1ca"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_13","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The engine is now well understood.\n\nBeginning the full game build. The simulation will be pure and complete, serving as the core for tests and the playable view. I am designing large file chunks to cover all criteria efficiently.\n\nWorking out the input format for each seat. The plan specifies x, z, tool, use, gift, steady, and drop. I am deciding whether those coordinates represent absolute tip positions or stick velocity.\n\nThe input format is now defined. Each seat receives x, z, tool, use, gift, steady, drop, mouse, rot, charge, release, massageL, massageR, and table. Mouse mode snaps the tip to absolute coordinates. Stick mode uses -1..1 axes. The bot will use mouse-style absolute positioning for reliability. Skill affects speed, path noise, and reaction delay.\n\nThe bot will compute its own next tip position by integrating speed. It passes absolute x, z with mouse mode enabled. Skill reduces the bot's internal speed limit.\n\nThe entity model is being defined. Each entity stores position, radius, health, completion state, visibility, path data, and suture slots.\n\nThe entity model is expanding to include laser heat, gel coverage, bruise timers, grab state, fragment rotation, syringe rings, and tool phase. The operation definition structure is being drafted with stages that specify spawn entities, hints, and available tools.\n\nStage definitions now include `until` conditions for clearing pools or hooks, flags, and counts. They also include `on` triggers for vitals. Strain stages will be driven by a dedicated module with `tick` and `onTool` methods. Each strain exposes a `botGoal` function that returns the bot's next input state. The focus is shifting to designing operations that a bot can actually solve.\n\nThe bot strategy is being defined as a generic sequence of concrete actions. It will time shocks during codeblue, hold gifts when vitals are low and composure is high, press gifts to rewind when forced, inject syringes when doses are available, and then target the best incomplete entity for the current stage.\n\nThe bot will prioritize entities in this order: active strain action, bleeder, pool covering something, hook or prop, scalpel incision, suture, laser, gel, dressing, then fragment. It will move toward the target, select the correct tool, and use it.\n\nOperation One, \"Eleven Hooks,\" is now being designed. It features two pools, eleven curved hooks with short pull lengths, two lacerations for suturing, one gel mask, one bandage line, and a twelfth hook. The idle timer is set to 240 seconds.\n\nThe simulation step must run in about 0.01 milliseconds per frame. At roughly 600,000 total steps across eleven operations and four skill levels, the engine needs to finish in under six seconds. Tool timing logic stays per-frame for bruise and suture rhythm.\n\nThe simulation will accept a `dt` parameter so idle steps can advance vitals and the clock faster than 60Hz without losing tool nuance. A `runUntil` helper will let tests fast-forward to failure. I will benchmark the hot loop later and optimize it if needed. Code blue triggers when vitals hit zero, which shortens the idle timeout from 240 seconds to 12 seconds.\n\nOpen pools and bleeders drain vitals at 0.80 per second, so the 85-point pool will deplete in about 106 seconds. This guarantees the code-blue timeout of 12 seconds before the 240-second clock expires. The bot must finish operations one through three before vitals die and before five mistakes are made.\n\nOperation One should take roughly 60 to 90 seconds. The bot can clear the two pools in a few seconds, then place the 12 hooks in about 24 seconds. Sutures, gel, and dressing add the rest. Vitals will drop from 85 to around 76 by the end, so a syringe may be needed.\n\nSkill 0.4 noise is being tuned so the bot stays within one-unit path tolerance. Mistakes will be limited: scalpel errors trigger at 0.8 units off path, and suture sloppy errors trigger at 0.4 units from a slot.\n\nSkill 0.4 path noise is set to 0.25 units. Skill 0.7 must finish Operation One in under 192 seconds to leave 48 seconds on the clock. Rank B requires at least half of the skill 1.0 score. A skill 1.0 run could score roughly 4700 points, so skill 0.7 risks falling below the 50% threshold if it cannot match that speed.\n\nSkill 0.7 scoring is being balanced against skill 1.0. A skill 0.7 run scoring roughly 2900 points would hit the 50% threshold and earn Rank B. If skill 1.0 finishes too fast, skill 0.7 could drop to about 45% of the skill 1.0 score and fail. The time bonus is only 5 points per second, so streaked action points dominate the total. Thirty clean actions at roughly 150 points each can easily outscore a large time bonus.\n\nSkill 0.7 must stay rank B, so action quality cannot be poor. Suture timing is targeted at 0.45 seconds. At skill 0.7 the ±0.1 jitter keeps actions CLEAN; at skill 0.4 the ±0.2 jitter may slip to GOOD. Scalpel deviation stays within the 0.3 unit limit for both skills. Forceps noise is capped under 0.4 units to prevent tears.\n\nThe Introit boss is now designed. Its core moves on a spline and knocks every six seconds. A pore appears for three seconds, and the bot must scalpel it. When the core is exposed, the bot pulls it with forceps along the tether. The pore will appear at a predictable location so the bot can react within 0.3 seconds. At skill 0.7 speed is enough to reach the pore in time. Three cycles take about thirty-six seconds plus sutures. The operation has three hundred sixty seconds, so this should be easy. At skill 0.4 the reaction delay and slower speed may cause repeated misses. Vitals drain at 0.30 per second from eighty points, which kills the bot around two hundred sixty-six seconds. Mistakes could push it over the edge.\n\nLacrimosa is designed with five eyes. The bot will laser each open eye for 1.2 seconds, drain the droplets, and apply gel to the sob line. Eyes open one at a time near the center so the bot can reach them. They do not heal if the bot stays on them within eight seconds. At half health the stalks move slowly but the bot still lasers.\n\nDies Irae is extremely tight. The heart beats at 72 BPM, so the diastole window lasts only about 0.54 seconds. The bot must wait for that brief window and perform a short action.\n\nDies Irae will use three rounds of one plate each. Each cut adds six BPM, raising the heart rate to 114 and triggering fibrillation after round three. The scalpel stroke is kept at 0.4 units so it finishes within the 0.3-second diastole window.\n\nDies Irae will use seven plates in sequence. Each cut adds six BPM, so the heart rate rises from 78 to 114. Fibrillation will be triggered exactly at 120 BPM, either by starting at 78 or by scripting it after the seventh plate.\n\nLibera Me is now being designed. The bot will scan for real cysts among eight targets and laser only the genuine ones. Mini strains will be handled in a split-screen pattern where the right half is managed by one bot and the left by another; both bots run at skill 0.7. A copy-hand ghost will replay the last three seconds of motion and cut where the player cut.\n\nLibera Me will end with a two-handed forceps pull and a scripted code blue. The copy-hand ghost must avoid recutting the same spot.\n\nOperation 10 is slow scalpel and forceps removals that raise vitals and pain. It has no attacks and a generous clock, so all skills above 0.4 can clear it.\n\nOperation 4 bone requires grabbing fragments, rotating them to a target angle within fifteen degrees, and releasing. A scripted code blue ensures the bot succeeds.\n\nOperation 6 transplant is being designed as a sequence of steps.\n\nOperation 6 transplant will use a forced rewind. The first unclamp always bursts a clot and sets a flag. The bot then presses Second Look before the second unclamp works. Kidney color is a view state flag.\n\nOperation 3 has three tables with separate vitals. The bot rotates to the table with the most urgent task. Losing any patient fails. The Quiet awakens at seventy percent progress. Each table gets a small batch of tasks: one pool and laceration, two debris forceps and gel, or one incision, pool, laceration, and polyp laser.\n\nConsult Pile-Up is being added to the balance table as a real operation. It will include waves of hooks, pools, lacerations, and polyps, plus a small introit boss at forty seconds. The bot will use quiet mode when vitals are low. Code blue is set to a twelve-second window with zero points.\n\nThe code blue monitor is being defined with a sweeping needle, a green release zone, and a periodic beat pulse. The bot must hold charge and release the syringe only when the needle is inside the zone and the beat aligns.\n\nThe code blue beat pulse will be a simple flag that is true 40% of the time. The green release zone and the beat window will be synchronized so that being inside the zone means the beat is active. This makes the testable condition straightforward: release the syringe when the needle is in the green zone and the beat flag is set.\n\nThe code blue monitor uses a triangle-wave needle sweeping every 1.2 seconds, so the green release zone itself defines the beat pulse. A successful shock restores vitals to twenty-five and resumes the operation; a missed release adds three seconds of penalty, and a timeout loses the case. Scripted code blues do not cap rank, but an unscripted vitals collapse caps rank at A. The Quiet mode will slow time to 0.25 while keeping cursor movement unscaled.\n\nQuiet mode will freeze vitals while composure drains at eighteen percent per real second. The bot needs at least twenty percent composure to activate it, and the effect ends when composure hits zero.\n\nSecond Look will store forty frames of state in a ring buffer every six frames. Pressing it restores the scene from four seconds ago if composure is at least sixty, deducts sixty points, and applies a five percent score penalty. It is disabled during code blue.\n\nA test will step five seconds, alter the scene, and rewind to confirm the snapshot matches four seconds ago while cursors remain. The scoring function assigns base points of one hundred for CLEAN, sixty for GOOD, twenty for SLOPPY, and negative one hundred fifty for MISTAKE. Consecutive CLEAN actions stack a streak multiplier that increases by ten percent per step up to double.\n\nComposure regenerates by seven points per step. The streak multiplier rises from one to two across eleven steps. A GOOD action breaks the streak, while only SLOPPY and MISTAKE do so.\n\nEnd score equals total points plus five times remaining time plus ten times vitals. Second Look applies a 5% penalty to the final score. Rank thresholds are S at 85%, A at 70%, B at 50%, and C otherwise. Pristine rank requires zero mistakes. Unscripted code blues cap rank at A, and retrying from stage caps at B. Difficulty tiers scale time and drain rates: Intern uses 1.4× time and 0.6× drain, Resident uses normal values, and Attending uses 0.8× time and 1.3× drain with no hints.\n\nThe config slider defaults are being defined. Aim assist is stored as a three-level scale: off, low, or high. The default is low.\n\nThe remaining config defaults are set: second look costs sixty composure and lasts four seconds, strain speed and health scale at one, dies irae beats at seventy-two, code blue zone width is zero point two, and coop scale is zero point two five. Live config copies the defaults and exposes applySlider and resetConfig. Snapshot encoding uses fixed-point integers for positions, uint8 kinds, and uint8 ids, with round-trip precision within zero point zero one.\n\nA peak test will spawn every operation’s entities at once and verify snapshot size. State hashing will be deterministic so identical seeds and inputs always produce the same hash. The campaign runner will walk scenes and operations with a bot, write a save object, unlock the log, and return ranks and credits.\n\nCampaign validation will run each scene’s beats, then bot every operation, save after each, and confirm the log unlocks. Content linting forbids TODOs, placeholders, stubs, and forbidden names; story lines must be two lines or fewer at sixty characters with valid font charset; a bot playthrough is required. Forbidden names include Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki, Deftera, Triti, Tetarti, Pempti, Paraskevi, and Savato. Player-facing text must not contain “Trauma Center”.\n\nThe game title \"The Second Opinion Clinic\" is permitted. The source work \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\" may appear in the exit interview and About Source Information, but never in player-facing UI text.\n\nStory lines are limited to eight to sixteen lines per scene. Each beat is at most two lines of sixty characters. The engine uppercases all displayed text. Allowed characters are letters, digits, and the symbols . , : ; ! ? - + % / ( ) [ ] ' \" # & * = < >.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1248c470-51fd-43d6-8d68-ab0c5b1b5615-24","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pixel-engine:audio|AUDIO_KEY|audioKey|localStorage","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/pixel-engine.js","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1248c470-51fd-43d6-8d68-ab0c5b1b5615-25","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"rayAt\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":5}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1248c470-51fd-43d6-8d68-ab0c5b1b5615-26","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.