nd\":\"node -e '\\nconst fs = require(\\\"fs\\\");\\nconst p = \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/pixel-engine.js\\\";\\nconst s = fs.readFileSync(p, \\\"utf8\\\");\\nlet i = 0, n = 0;\\nwhile ((i = s.indexOf(\\\"pixel-engine:\\\", i)) !== -1 && n < 20) {\\n  console.log(\\\"---\\\", n, \\\"at\\\", i);\\n  console.log(s.slice(Math.max(0, i-120), i+180).replace(/\\\\n/g, \\\" \\\"));\\n  i += 12; n++;\\n}\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Find audio localStorage key context in engine\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/terminal/call-17c01153-b79c-4692-91d8-18b6b6e97bea-29.log\",\"total_bytes\":842}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"d37852d4-cb53-4423-b2ab-0b2ae87299e1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_16","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The audio key is `pixel-engine:audio` with default master settings.\n\nDefault audio levels are master 0.8, sfx 1, music 0.6, and muted false. On boot, if the key is absent, music volume will be set to 0.1. A test will verify this by loading a fresh profile. The codebase will be written efficiently with a comprehensive but not infinitely split structure.\n\nThe file layout is now mapped out. Core utilities will live in `src/core/` alongside the seeded RNG and save system. Simulation logic splits into scoring, tools, operations, strains, bot AI, codec, and campaign modules. Content definitions for operations, scenes, dialogue, about text, and cast will go under `src/content/`. Voice audio handling will be in `src/audio/voice.js`.\n\nThe file layout now includes audio songs and SFX, view and UI modules, input devices, network session, game entry point, test runner, build tool, and authoring docs. The simulation will be organized into a few large well-structured files. The RNG implementation is being drafted using a 31-bit multiply-add generator.\n\nThe RNG implementation is now complete with float, int, state, and set methods. The simulation will store its RNG state so clones can restore it. The createOp function is being drafted to accept seed, seats, difficulty, and configuration parameters.\n\ncreateOp now assigns each seat a position, tool, and initial stats. Seat gifts alternate between quiet and look for consults. Story operations override this with a fixed surgeon gift.\n\nStory operations now define gift unlock rules. Op 1 and 2 start with no gifts. Op 3 unlocks quiet mid-op. Op 6 grants second look. Op 9 grants both. The simulation tracks locked and unlocked gifts separately.\n\nThe step function is being drafted. It skips updates when the phase is won or lost. Quiet mode scales the simulation delta by composure and a quiet scale factor.\n\nHistory will be recorded at 10 Hz of real time for the second look. Cursors move in real time regardless of quiet mode. During code blue, the simulation freezes while paddles and the 12-second clock continue in real time.\n\nGifts are handled before tool application and strain ticks. During quiet mode, vitals do not drain. Tool effects apply to each seat using the scaled simulation delta. Strain ticks advance at the same rate. Vitals drain normally unless quiet or god mode is active; bleeders add extra drain capped at two per second total.\n\nVital drain now subtracts the combined base and bleed rates multiplied by the scaled simulation delta. The shared clock decrements by that same scaled amount.\n\nMulti-table operations will track vitals per table in a `sim.tables` array, while single-table operations keep vitals as a single number on the simulation object. The HUD reads from the active table, but every table drains independently.\n\nStage completion, failure conditions, and events are being checked next.\n\nThe drain tool implementation is being drafted. It checks pool proximity and reduces pool radius by the drain rate multiplied by the scaled simulation delta. Pools below 0.2 radius are removed and reveal hidden content. When the seat is not on a pool, a dry-time counter increments using simulation time so quiet mode slows the waste. After 0.6 seconds of dry tissue, the seat becomes bruised unless it was already bruised this hold.\n\nThe drain tool now spawns a sloppy nick when bruising occurs and resets the dry-time counter on valid pool contact. Forceps logic is being drafted: holding within 0.45 of a grabbable entity initiates a pull along a path, with deviation above one unit causing tears and drops; release completes the dish.\n\nForceps release logic is being drafted. The seat’s position is projected onto the pull path to measure deviation; if it exceeds one unit, the entity tears. Progress along the path updates the pulled distance, but a speed limit snaps the entity back by half a unit if exceeded. Once the pulled distance reaches the target length, the entity becomes free.\n\nForceps release logic is now complete. A free entity completes and rates based on deviation history. A tethered entity snaps back and lacerates. The seat drops its hold.\n\nPull paths are stored as polylines starting at the hook position. Bots move along these points.\n\nForceps rating thresholds are set to ≤0.12 for CLEAN, ≤0.3 for GOOD, and SLOPPY otherwise.\n\nScalpel tool logic is being drafted. An incision entity stores a path polyline, progress index, and deviation samples. The player must start within 0.5 of the path start and advance through points while held. Mean deviation is tracked; exceeding 0.8 spawns a pool once per excursion and registers as a mistake. Releasing the tool resumes the simulation. Completion rates by mean deviation.\n\nSuture tool logic is also being drafted. A laceration becomes a line between two points with slots every 0.45 units. The player must cross the wound line with an amplitude of at least 0.25 within 0.4 of each slot.\n\nSuture amplitude is the perpendicular swing across the wound line. The implementation tracks which side of the line the tip is on and counts the distance traveled on the previous side when the side flips. A stitch is tied if that amplitude is at least 0.25 and the tip is within 0.4 of the next slot. If the crossing is too far from a slot, the stitch is marked SLOPPY and must be redone.\n\nSuture rhythm is now defined: stitches within 0.2 to 0.7 seconds of the previous one rate CLEAN; outside that window but still near a slot, GOOD; far off timing, SLOPPY. Pitch rises per stitch for audio feedback.\n\nLaser tool logic is being drafted. Targets accumulate heat while the seat stays within 0.45 units. Completion occurs at full heat and rates CLEAN if stray time was low. Stray time increments when the seat holds the tool without a valid target.\n\nLaser stray-time thresholds are finalized: CLEAN under 0.1 seconds, GOOD under 0.3, and SLOPPY otherwise.\n\nScanner tool logic is drafted. While held, hidden entities within 2.2 units become marked and remain visible. It is unrated.\n\nGel tool logic is drafted. A mask grid of cells is painted by a brush radius of 0.8. Completion occurs at 92 percent coverage. Nicks beneath the mask heal after one second of coverage. Rating will be based on completion speed.\n\nGel tool rating thresholds are finalized: CLEAN under four seconds, GOOD under eight, and SLOPPY otherwise.\n\nSyringe logic is drafted. The seat holds the tool for 0.45 seconds to draw a dose, then releases inside a ring to heal eighteen vitals and consume one dose. Releasing outside wastes the dose and rates SLOPPY. The quick syringe key performs the same action.\n\nDressing logic is drafted. The seat drags along a marked line while held within 0.5 units to complete the operation stage.\n\nBone fragment placement uses forceps with rotation input. Snapping is clean within ±5°, good within ±15°, and the fragment stays otherwise.\n\nStage completion checks are drafted. A stage clears when all entities of specified kinds are done on the active table, or when a required flag is set.\n\nStage clearing now spawns the next stage's entities. Operation definitions will stay data-driven and compact. Helper functions for hooks, pools, cuts, wounds, and gel masks are being drafted. A `hookPath` function is being written to generate curved pull paths from a starting point using arc curvature.\n\nHook path points are now generated using sine and cosine offsets along the arc. Positions must stay within safe bounds: |x| ≤ 6, z in [-3.6, 3.0], and x > 5 with z > 2.4 must be avoided.\n\nA bot intent function is being drafted. It picks a goal for the seat based on skill level and moves toward it at a speed scaled by cursor speed and skill, adding noise.\n\nThe bot input protocol is being defined. The simulation step will apply movement toward target coordinates at a speed scaled by cursor speed and any speed multiplier, unless the input carries a snap flag. Exact placement is reserved for tool tests and mouse input; seeking is for bot navigation.\n\nMouse input raycasts to the surface to place the tip. Bot movement adds a deterministic sine noise offset scaled by skill, and reaction delays before tool changes scale with skill as well. During code blue, the bot checks whether the needle is within the zone; below 0.3 skill it fails, otherwise it charges and uses the side.\n\nCode blue bot logic is being refined. At skill 0.55 or above, the bot releases the charge once the needle enters the zone; below that it charges again. Skill 0.4 may still trigger code blue on operation four despite it being optional, while operation three likely does not. The zone width is 0.2 units, and the needle sweeps across it in 1.2 seconds, giving a zone window of roughly 0.12 seconds—about seven frames at sixty frames per second.\n\nQuiet mode is being added for the bot. It activates when vitals are below forty, the mode is unlocked, and composure exceeds thirty. Operations one and two do not unlock it; operation three unlocks it at seventy percent completion. Using quiet stops vitals drain, which helps skill zero point seven on later operations. At full skill level it may also preserve vitals for score. The trade-off is that quiet drains composure and slows simulation time by a quarter, making the clock drain slower while the cursor moves at full speed.\n\nQuiet mode would let a skill one bot finish operations with massive time left, inflating its score. Both skill zero point seven and skill one use it, so ranking still works if both behave similarly. Composure drains eighteen percent per second in real time, so a seat starting at forty can hold quiet for about two seconds before needing clean actions to refill it.\n\nQuiet mode is bursty rather than infinite. The bot will use it only when vitals fall below forty-five or during tight timed windows like pore and sob, keeping composure available for other moments.\n\nA Second Look bot is being added: it presses the gift key once whenever a `needRewind` flag is set.\n\nA flaw in the Second Look rewind logic was spotted: if rewinding restores exact state, the hidden clot burst never happens, so unclamping would burst again and create an infinite loop. The plan resolves this by making the first unclamp burst unavoidable, forcing the prompt to use the Second Look.\n\nMeta flags like `clotSeen` and `secondLookUsed` will be excluded from the rewind snapshot. After rewinding, the hidden clot burst is already marked as seen, so the next unclamp succeeds without looping.\n\nThe 5% score penalty for Second Look usage will persist across rewinds. A `lookUses` counter will be kept outside the restored snapshot so it survives rewind.\n\nHistory snapshots will clone entities, vitals, mistakes, clock, stage, score, streak, doses, flags, strain state, and table vitals. Cursor positions, the look counter, the history buffer itself, and composure will be excluded from the snapshot.\n\nA hash of the restorable state plus seat positions will be computed to ensure deterministic simulation runs. Dev mode sliders modify the live config object, so changing a slider alters subsequent steps; resetting copies the default values back.\n\nA test confirmed that the vitals drain multiplier slider works correctly. The final score formula adds five points per second of time left and ten points per full vital, then applies a five percent penalty for each Second Look used. The rank function maps scores to S, A, B, or C based on percentage of par, with an optional cap to promote an S to an A.\n\nPar values will be hardcoded after running a stable bot, then verified by the test. The rank function caps S or A ranks at B when the cap is B. For multi-table operations, the vitals bonus will sum all table vitals rather than averaging or using the minimum.\n\nOperation three fails if any patient dies. The bot must keep all three tables above zero vitals. Independent drain across three tables gives roughly 220 seconds before collapse, while the operation clock is 360 seconds. Ignoring one table will kill it.\n\nThe bot will round-robin tables, prioritizing the lowest-vital table that still has tasks. An idle seat with no input causes vitals to drain and pools to bleed until code blue or the clock expires. The simulation exits early on failure to avoid running all 350,000 steps.\n\nIdle simulation runs are estimated at roughly one hundred thousand steps across all eleven operations. Skill testing will total about two hundred sixty-four thousand steps, which should finish in a few seconds if each step is lean. Deep state cloning will be avoided every frame; snapshots will only be made when a Second Look gift is triggered or during tests.\n\nConsult pile-up timing is being mapped to a 180-second window. Pools and hooks appear at zero seconds. Lacerations arrive at thirty. Polyps at sixty. Later waves at ninety. An introit at forty seconds restores one health point. The hub round lasts four minutes. The fight should be winnable in two to three minutes. Boss fights are marked as introit, lacrimosa, dies, and libera. Skill 0.7 needs twenty percent clock for non-boss fights.\n\nBoss operations are now fixed at five, seven, eight, and nine. Pile-up, trimmed Introit, Lacrimosa, Dies Irae, and the early procedures are non-boss. The hub round cycles these four session ops with a four-minute limit. A shorter `sessionCut` version will isolate each boss fight to an 180-second clock for the host.\n\nThe host and client networking architecture is being designed. The game boot sequence will detect an active AIHub session, verify WebGPU support, and either run a shared-world session or fall back to standalone mode. The server will initialize AIHub without starting the engine directly; instead it will drive the simulation loop using `setInterval` and `AIHub.time()`. Clients will start their own engines, display the card until the host signals start, then begin playing.\n\nThe host loop will step the simulation in one-sixtieth-second chunks to catch up, capped at thirty frames per second. Clients will transmit input every one-fifteenth of a second even when idle. The host will broadcast snapshots at twelve frames per second, keyframes at one per second, and events. A token bucket allows twenty tokens per second with a burst of forty. Standings will group all present players by score. The simulation will use AIHub.rng('deal') instead of Math.random.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b6ff1f5d-5f6f-4e7c-9471-10ce087d0199-30","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pass|problem|__hubCheck|ready\\(|finish\\(|send\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/vendor/harness.html","head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b6ff1f5d-5f6f-4e7c-9471-10ce087d0199-31","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function check|problems|pass =|shared-world|both ways|server","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/vendor/round.js","head_limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":582,"output_tokens":7208,"cache_read_input_tokens":98560,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"85ad9dfb-76f1-46e4-859b-6fd2c45f01f8"}
