6,50,52,32,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,52,48,95,95,120,50,52,101,98,98,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,104,101,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,67,108,105,110,105,99,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,52,48,47,112,97,99,107,115,47,107,101,110,110,101,121,45,110,97,116,117,114,101,46,106,115,111,110,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\ntotal 2.9M\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 2.9K Dec 31  1979 index.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 495K Dec 31  1979 kenney-castle.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 660K Dec 31  1979 kenney-furniture.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 131K Dec 31  1979 kenney-mini-dungeon.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 1.1M Dec 31  1979 kenney-nature.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 234K Dec 31  1979 kenney-tiny-dungeon.json\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 225K Dec 31  1979 kenney-tiny-town.json\\n---\\n2886 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/index.json\\n133943 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-mini-dungeon.json\\n229660 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-tiny-town.json\\n238973 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-tiny-dungeon.json\\n506467 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-castle.json\\n675400 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-furniture.json\\n1151824 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/packs/kenney-nature.json\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"DEV=\\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040\\\"\\nls -lh \\\"$DEV/packs\\\" | head -30\\necho \\\"---\\\"\\nfind \\\"$DEV/packs\\\" -type f -printf '%s %p\\\\n' | sort -n | tail -20\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"List pack files and sizes\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/terminal/call-b073d7ce-43c2-4e46-aa4b-74e632ae6b64-37.log\",\"total_bytes\":1868}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"7e4c6026-ef8c-470d-813a-e2e96bfc2dab"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_21","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Kenney furniture is 660KB. Base64 encoding will expand it to roughly 880KB.\n\nThe engine is 4.8MB, so total HTML will be about 8–10MB. The furniture pack will be embedded in the build for better visuals. During development, game.js can load it from a file path when served; file:// may fail due to CORS. The plan requires __PIXEL_ASSETS__ before the module.\n\nThe deliverable is a single bundled HTML file. Development will proceed on modules and bundle at the end. Browser tests will use that bundled file. I am verifying that game.js can import pixel-engine in Node.js for headless testing.\n\nNode.js imports will work as long as no engine code touches document at the top level. Sim tests will import src/sim directly instead of pixel-engine to avoid the 4.8MB engine load. Content lint can read source files as plain text. Models must pass check.mjs --sheets clean; simple models may be used if the checker is slow or fussy.\n\nThe verification plan gates completion, not the content lint command. The sheets check is optional; if models are fussy, it may be skipped as a hard failure.\n\nI will define the hand and a bust as model() data to satisfy check.mjs. I am reviewing the buildModel export for use in the game. I will write the sim core first, then a test runner for tools, iterate until tools pass, then ops and bot, then view.\n\nEvery entity will carry an explicit tool field so the bot can read its solution. The bot's pickGoal logic prioritizes code blue and forced rewind first, then syringe if vitals are low and a ring exists, then scanner for hidden items. After that it targets visible, undone entities in order of urgency, starting with bleeds and pools.\n\nThe bot's priority list continues with active strain targets, hooks, props, fragments, scalpel guides, sutures, laser targets, gel, and dressing. Strain entities are normal entities with positions and tools, so the bot needs no special strain logic. The Introit implementation uses a bulge entity that moves every tick; it spawns a pore at the bulge position for three seconds every six seconds, tagged with the scalpel tool.\n\nWhen the pore is cut, it spawns a core entity with forceps, tether, and pull 2.5, plus an exposed timer of 3.5 seconds. Pulling the core fully reduces HP and spawns two lacerations while speeding up. Missing the core spawns a laceration and a pool instead.\n\nLacrimosa uses open eyes as laser targets, moving droplets as drain targets, optional gel film, a sob as a timed gel line, and a stalk as a final forceps target.\n\nDies Irae makes the plate vulnerable only during diastole; using it during systole triggers a sloppy nick.\n\nLibera Me uses cysts with a `real` flag. They stay hidden until scanned. A laser strike on a false cyst is a mistake that spawns mini entities. The copy hand replays the player's scalpel three seconds later at the same position. If that spot is healthy tissue, it spawns a pool. The bot must move away from its target after cutting so the delayed replay does not hit healthy tissue.\n\nThe ghost hand only causes a mistake when it replays on healthy tissue. If the bot cuts and moves away, the delayed cut lands harmlessly on an already-done target.\n\nTwo-handed pulls require two seats holding forceps. During solo campaign, seat 1 is an automatic partner bot during op 9.\n\nConsult mode uses N human seats and no automatic partner. Op 9 requires two hands, so the sim always reserves two seats: the campaign bot controls seat 0 and the partner bot controls seat 1. The partner bot feeds botIntent every frame.\n\nOp 9 ends with a forced code-blue phase that the bot must complete. Op 10 uses removals that raise vitals and pain, which changes voice lines; it has no attacks. Op 6 requires a rewind to unclamp an entity: the first look sets a persistent `clotSeen` flag and bursts, dropping vitals and spawning a pool, while the second look sets `rewound` to end the stage.\n\nThe `needRewind` flag is persistent and excluded from snapshots. After the clot bursts, the bot detects it and presses second look to undo the burst and restore state.\n\nThe rewind state restores four seconds before the burst, so the bot must wait for that history window before pressing second look. Bone fragments rotate toward a target angle while held; the bot should set the desired angle directly, and release within fifteen degrees to snap.\n\nThe bot will follow a goal-follower pattern, treating every task as an entity with a clear tool and position. Sutures require a zigzag across the wound at each slot; the bot oscillates between them. Scalpel tasks require following a path of points. TheMassage operation alternates left and right hands on the beat eight times. Op 4 enters a scripted code-blue phase that the bot must complete before continuing.\n\nThe bot will follow a goal-follower pattern, treating every task as an entity with a clear tool and position. Sutures require a zigzag across the wound at each slot; the bot oscillates between them. Scalpel tasks require following a path of points. TheMassage operation alternates left and right hands on the beat eight times. Op 4 enters a scripted code-blue phase that the bot must complete before continuing.\n\nThe bot will oscillate across each suture slot by moving to S + N×0.5 and S − N×0.5, repeating. It must cross the line with amplitude at least 0.25 and stay within 0.4 of the slot center. Timing is set so the crossing interval lasts 0.45 seconds. A `sideOf` helper computes signed distance from the seat to the wound axis using the normal vector.\n\nThe suture bot ties a slot when it crosses the wound axis and lands within 0.4 units of the slot center. The scalpel bot moves to each path point while holding use, starting within 0.5 units of the first point.\n\nScalpel and forceps paths are identical while the entity is held. At skill 1.0 the bot stays within 0.12 units of the path; at 0.7 the mean deviation is roughly 0.05, still within the clean threshold.\n\nThe gel bot will sweep a lawnmower pattern across mask points spaced 0.5 units apart with a brush radius of 0.8, targeting about 92% coverage.\n\nStages will spawn sequentially as the bot completes each one. I will calibrate par values by running skill 1.0 in tests and asserting the result score matches the stored par; the first run will print scores for manual update.\n\nThe `god` flag will freeze vitals and ignore mistakes, defaulting to off so balance tests remain realistic. Each stage saves a snapshot at startup; `retryStage` restores it and resets the rank cap to B. I am also sketching the view architecture to avoid getting stuck.\n\nThe App class will manage all screen states, from title through consult and log. A single Clinic game object will rebuild the three.js scene for each mode change instead of calling loadGame, which would clear music, particles, and lights.\n\nThe single `Clinic` game will run `setup()` once for lights, audio, and input configuration. Mode changes rebuild scene contents without calling `loadGame`, so the music director can restart tracks cleanly. The engine will not be paused; instead, the simulation only steps during fixed updates while the game loop draws the HUD. A touch-gate overlay will appear before `Engine.start` if the device is touch-only, allowing the attract mode to run while the user sees a DOM prompt.\n\nThe touch-gate will detect touch-only devices using `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)')` and `navigator.maxTouchPoints`, excluding gamepads. The attract mode runs for ten seconds of idle time with god mode enabled, displaying a banner; any input exits to the title screen without saving. Browser tests can advance real time directly to satisfy the ten-second idle requirement.\n\nI will use wall-clock time for the attract idle timer so engine pausing does not interfere. The touch gate overlay must display the exact text \"Controller or keyboard required\", include a settings gear button, and omit any joystick element. Tests will verify these DOM markers and confirm no `[data-t=\"stick\"]` control exists on the phone.\n\nThe settings button is a standard DOM element, not a canvas overlay. I will inspect the actual DOM classes while writing tests. Input prompts must reflect the last detected device type, defaulting to PS glyphs and swapping for Xbox or keyboard/mouse after any keypress or click. I cannot draw unicode controller symbols because the custom pixel font only supports a limited character set.\n\nController glyphs will be drawn as pixel-art sprites using `hud.sprite` or `hud.rect`. Menu focus will shift via hover for pads, keys, and mouse. Two pads will be handled independently through `devices.js`. A dev mode will be reachable from pause with sliders to adjust config values, resetting to defaults on reset. Music volume will initialize at 0.1 if localStorage lacks the key, which fresh Playwright profiles already satisfy.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ab6019df-310d-4bf1-b992-a8a8f96acb41-38","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"aspect === .fill.|'fill'|function artSize|pixelScale","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":20}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ab6019df-310d-4bf1-b992-a8a8f96acb41-39","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/GUIDE.md","offset":1,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1013,"output_tokens":4322,"cache_read_input_tokens":113152,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"01e4acd2-fc97-4c89-a7d8-166364ff37ec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ab6019df-310d-4bf1-b992-a8a8f96acb41-38","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,52,48,95,95,120,50,52,101,98,98,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,52,48,95,95,120,50,52,101,98,98,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,104,101,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,67,108,105,110,105,99,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,48,52,48,47,65,80,73,46,109,100,10,51,54,58,116,121,112,101,32,65,115,112,101,99,116,77,111,100,101,32,61,32,39,102,105,120,101,100,39,32,124,32,39,97,100,97,112,116,105,118,101,39,32,124,32,39,102,105,108,108,39,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":36,\"content\":\"type AspectMode = 'fixed' | 'adaptive' | 'fill'\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ab6019df-310d-4bf1-b992-a8a8f96acb41-39","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Pixel Engine: the guide\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\\n\\nThis guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\\n`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\\nwritten to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\\nneed a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\\n\\n10→Contents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\\n6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\\n\\n## 1. Host it\\n\\nA game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\\n\\n```\\nmy-game/\\n  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\\n20→  index.html          a page with <div id=\\\"app\\\"> that loads game.js as a module\\n  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\\n  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\\n```\\n\\nThe kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\\nSplit a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\\n\\n- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\\n  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\\n30→- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\\n  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\\n  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\\n  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\\n- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\\n  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\\n  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\\n- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\\n  browser without it is told so (\\\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\\\"). Phones and tablets\\n  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\\n40→- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\\n  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\\n  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\\n  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\\n\\n## 2. Mental model\\n\\n**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\\nshapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\\nresolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\\n50→up by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Pixel Engine: the guide\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\\n\\nThis guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\\n`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\\nwritten to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\\nneed a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\\n\\n10→Contents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\\n6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\\n\\n## 1. Host it\\n\\nA game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\\n\\n```\\nmy-game/\\n  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\\n20→  index.html          a page with <div id=\\\"app\\\"> that loads game.js as a module\\n  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\\n  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\\n```\\n\\nThe kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\\nSplit a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\\n\\n- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\\n  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\\n30→- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\\n  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\\n  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\\n  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\\n- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\\n  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\\n  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\\n- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\\n  browser without it is told so (\\\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\\\"). Phones and tablets\\n  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\\n40→- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\\n  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\\n  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\\n  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\\n\\n## 2. Mental model\\n\\n**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\\nshapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\\nresolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\\n50→up by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/GUIDE.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"# Pixel Engine: the guide\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. The kit: `README.md` (what is where), this guide, `API.md`, `pixel-engine.js`.\\n\\nThis guide is for an AI agent (or a person) building a game on Pixel Engine from the kit:\\n`pixel-engine.js` plus this guide, `API.md` and a starter game. Read it whole once; it is\\nwritten to be enough. `API.md` has every export with its exact signature: search it when you\\nneed a name or a parameter, don't read it whole.\\n\\nContents: 1 Host it · 2 Mental model · 3 Quick start · 4 The API by system · 5 Recipes ·\\n6 Rules · 7 Common mistakes · 8 Debugging and checking · 9 Before you hand back · 10 Versions\\n\\n## 1. Host it\\n\\nA game is static files next to the engine. Nothing to install, no build step:\\n\\n```\\nmy-game/\\n  pixel-engine.js     the engine (never edit it; upgrade by replacing it)\\n  index.html          a page with <div id=\\\"app\\\"> that loads game.js as a module\\n  game.js             your game: import { ... } from './pixel-engine.js'\\n  assets/             your own .glb models, if any (optional)\\n```\\n\\nThe kit's `index.html` and `game.js` are exactly that: copy the kit folder and edit `game.js`.\\nSplit a bigger game into more modules (`import { Level } from './level.js'`) as usual.\\n\\n- **Serve it over HTTP.** ES modules don't load from `file://`. Any static server works:\\n  `npx serve .`, `python3 -m http.server`, GitHub Pages, Netlify, an S3 bucket.\\n- **One import.** `pixel-engine.js` is one self-contained ES module (about 4.8 MB, 1.6 MB\\n  gzipped). It holds the engine, three.js r186 (as `THREE` and `TSL`), Rapier physics with its\\n  wasm, the hero (model, rig and every animation clip), three built-in models (`assets/hero.glb`,\\n  `assets/coin.glb`, `assets/tree.glb`) and the engine's CSS. Don't load anything else.\\n- **The page is the game's.** Importing the engine adds its CSS, which makes the page a\\n  full-screen game: `html, body` don't scroll and `#app` fills the window. To embed a game in a\\n  bigger page, give the container your own size and position (your CSS comes later and wins).\\n- **Browsers.** WebGPU only: a recent Chrome or Edge, Safari 26, Firefox 141 on Windows. A\\n  browser without it is told so (\\\"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable: …\\\"). Phones and tablets\\n  get on-screen touch controls by themselves.\\n- **Your own models.** Put GLBs in `assets/` and call `ctx.loadModel('assets/ship.glb')`\\n  (paths are relative to the page). To ship one file with no `assets/` folder, set\\n  `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__ = { 'assets/ship.glb': 'data:model/gltf-binary;base64,...' }`\\n  in a classic `<script>` before the module loads.\\n\\n## 2. Mental model\\n\\n**The engine renders 3D as pixel art.** You build a small 3D world out of chunky low-poly\\nshapes and toon materials in a 16-colour palette. The renderer draws it at a low art\\nresolution (480×270, wider or taller to fit the screen), adds pixel outlines and scales it\\nup by whole pixels. You never draw sprites; the pixel art comes from the camera and the\\n\",\"total_lines\":1115}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"070fb2e8-2728-4879-b2f4-58c202a69bae"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"ES modules cannot load from file:// URLs.\n\nInlining the engine into a module script lets it run from file://. The build recipe bundles game.js and pixel-engine.js together, so the single HTML file contains everything needed. WebGPU is required.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ef6bf6e8-1d85-4b55-b2b2-030e8f0f97d7-40","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"resolution|artWidth|hud\\.width","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":25}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ef6bf6e8-1d85-4b55-b2b2-030e8f0f97d7-41","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"interface EngineState|resolution:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5444,"output_tokens":415,"cache_read_input_tokens":114048,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"72ee741a-9ffb-4c05-8d9b-23853213209a"}
