FILTERS) applied in Pixel mode, in order.\\n- `look?: Look`: A full look: pixel art or clean rendering and filters per layer (characters & objects, environment) plus whole-scene filters (render/look.ts). Wins over `filters` and `edges`.\\n- `seed?: number`: Seed for `ctx.random`: each level gets its own stream from this and the game's name, so the same seed replays the same game. Default 1. `?seed=` in the URL.\\n- `record?: number`: Frames of input the engine keeps for each level (`recording()`). Default `MAX_FRAMES`, an hour at 60 fps (up to some 20 MB of JSON with the mouse moving all the time); 0 records nothing.\\n- `debugUI?: boolean`: Debug panel. Default: on in dev (`vite`) or with ?debug=1, off in production builds.\\n- `debugKeys?: DebugKeysOption`: Engine hotkeys (P Pixel/Raw, R resolution, ` debug panel, [ ] looks, M mute): developer tools. Default: on with the debug panel (dev, ?debug=1), off in a shipped game, whose players have the same options in the settings. `true` / `false`: always on / off; an…\\n- `settings?: { button?: TouchCorner | boolean; key?: string | false }`: The player's settings (`engine.settings`): look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate, sound and debug info, in a panel the ⚙ button opens. `button`: its corner (default, or true: 'top-right'; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves), or false for n…\\n- `touch?: boolean`: On-screen joystick + buttons. Default: auto (coarse pointer), or ?touch=1 / ?touch=0.\\n- `touchButtons?: readonly TouchButton[]`: On-screen action buttons. Default: the platformer's (DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS).\\n- `touchBar?: boolean`: The touch top bar of the engine hotkeys (ⓘ debug panel, P, R, ◐ looks, ♪ mute), on a touch screen while they are on (see `debugKeys`). Default true; false hides it even then, unless the debug panel is on (`?debug=1`, dev builds).\\n- `background?: number`\\n\\n### FPS_OPTIONS\\n\\n```ts\\nconst FPS_OPTIONS: readonly number[]\\n```\\n\\n### class FrameLimiter\\n\\nFrame cap for `requestAnimationFrame` loops. Returns whether the frame at `now` (ms)\\nshould run, and advances the schedule so the long-run rate is `maxFps` even when the\\ndisplay rate isn't a multiple of it (90 Hz, 144 Hz) and timestamps jitter.\\n\\n- `new FrameLimiter(maxFps: number)`\\n- `maxFps: number`\\n- `shouldRun(now: number): boolean`\\n\\n### frameView()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction frameView(viewport: Viewport, base: Resolution, aspect: AspectMode, current: Resolution): { framing: Framing; resolution: Resolution }\\n```\\n\\nThe framing and art resolution of a view (PixelRenderer's canvas, or a HeadlessView's viewport):\\n`computeFraming`, and the art size as a resolution. Identity is kept where nothing changed: the\\n`current` object while the art size stays, else `base` itself when the art size equals it (a\\nfixed or 16:9 view reports the preset it was given), else a new one.\\n\\n### interface Framing\\n\\n- `readonly artWidth: number`: Art (internal) resolution actually rendered. Equals `internal` in fixed mode.\\n- `readonly artHeight: number`\\n- `readonly scale: number`: Device pixels per art pixel. Integer whenever `integer` is true.\\n- `readonly integer: boolean`: True when every art pixel maps to an integer block of device pixels.\\n- `readonly canvasWidth: number`: Canvas backing-store size in device pixels.\\n- `readonly canvasHeight: number`\\n- `readonly cssWidth: number`: Canvas CSS size and offset inside the viewport, in CSS pixels.\\n- `readonly cssHeight: number`\\n- `readonly offsetX: number`\\n- `readonly offsetY: number`\\n\\n### interface Game\\n\\nA game is a plain object. The engine owns rendering, physics stepping, camera,\\nlighting and debug UI; the game only builds its world and reacts to input.\\n\",\"total_lines\":5143}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-9bbc9926-f97c-4b54-ae28-7e4a6c883528-43","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"420→```ts\\nfunction rebindDebugKeys(keys: DebugKeyMap, changes: DebugKeyChanges): DebugKeyMap\\n```\\n\\nRebind the hotkeys that are on and leave the ones that are off off: what a game with its own\\nuse for a key does at runtime (`engine.debugKeys = rebindDebugKeys(engine.debugKeys, {\\nresolution: 'F7' })`), so a shipped game, which has no hotkeys, gets none back.\\n\\n### interface Resolution\\n\\n430→Pure layout math for pixel-perfect presentation. No DOM, no three.js: unit-tested.\\n\\nThe canvas backing store is sized to `art × scale` device pixels with an integer\\n`scale`, and the scene renders at exactly the art resolution (pixelSize = scale). Every\\nart pixel therefore covers an integer block of device pixels, and the canvas is centered\\ninside the viewport. Fixed/adaptive framing letterboxes; fill framing crops only the\\nexcess device pixels at the edges of the last art pixel.\\n\\nThree aspect modes:\\n- `fixed`: the art resolution is exactly `internal` (16:9); other screen shapes letterbox.\\n440→- `adaptive`: the art *height* stays `internal.height` and the art width follows the\\n  screen's aspect…\\n\\n- `readonly width: number`\\n- `readonly height: number`\\n\\n### RESOLUTIONS\\n\\n```ts\\nconst RESOLUTIONS: { readonly default: { readonly width: 480; readonly height: 270; }; readonly compare: { readonly width: 320; readonly height: 180; }; }\",\"content_concise\":\"420→```ts\\nfunction rebindDebugKeys(keys: DebugKeyMap, changes: DebugKeyChanges): DebugKeyMap\\n```\\n\\nRebind the hotkeys that are on and leave the ones that are off off: what a game with its own\\nuse for a key does at runtime (`engine.debugKeys = rebindDebugKeys(engine.debugKeys, {\\nresolution: 'F7' })`), so a shipped game, which has no hotkeys, gets none back.\\n\\n### interface Resolution\\n\\n430→Pure layout math for pixel-perfect presentation. No DOM, no three.js: unit-tested.\\n\\nThe canvas backing store is sized to `art × scale` device pixels with an integer\\n`scale`, and the scene renders at exactly the art resolution (pixelSize = scale). Every\\nart pixel therefore covers an integer block of device pixels, and the canvas is centered\\ninside the viewport. Fixed/adaptive framing letterboxes; fill framing crops only the\\nexcess device pixels at the edges of the last art pixel.\\n\\nThree aspect modes:\\n- `fixed`: the art resolution is exactly `internal` (16:9); other screen shapes letterbox.\\n440→- `adaptive`: the art *height* stays `internal.height` and the art width follows the\\n  screen's aspect…\\n\\n- `readonly width: number`\\n- `readonly height: number`\\n\\n### RESOLUTIONS\\n\\n```ts\\nconst RESOLUTIONS: { readonly default: { readonly width: 480; readonly height: 270; }; readonly compare: { readonly width: 320; readonly height: 180; }; }\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md\",\"offset\":420,\"limit\":30,\"raw_output\":\"```ts\\nfunction rebindDebugKeys(keys: DebugKeyMap, changes: DebugKeyChanges): DebugKeyMap\\n```\\n\\nRebind the hotkeys that are on and leave the ones that are off off: what a game with its own\\nuse for a key does at runtime (`engine.debugKeys = rebindDebugKeys(engine.debugKeys, {\\nresolution: 'F7' })`), so a shipped game, which has no hotkeys, gets none back.\\n\\n### interface Resolution\\n\\nPure layout math for pixel-perfect presentation. No DOM, no three.js: unit-tested.\\n\\nThe canvas backing store is sized to `art × scale` device pixels with an integer\\n`scale`, and the scene renders at exactly the art resolution (pixelSize = scale). Every\\nart pixel therefore covers an integer block of device pixels, and the canvas is centered\\ninside the viewport. Fixed/adaptive framing letterboxes; fill framing crops only the\\nexcess device pixels at the edges of the last art pixel.\\n\\nThree aspect modes:\\n- `fixed`: the art resolution is exactly `internal` (16:9); other screen shapes letterbox.\\n- `adaptive`: the art *height* stays `internal.height` and the art width follows the\\n  screen's aspect…\\n\\n- `readonly width: number`\\n- `readonly height: number`\\n\\n### RESOLUTIONS\\n\\n```ts\\nconst RESOLUTIONS: { readonly default: { readonly width: 480; readonly height: 270; }; readonly compare: { readonly width: 320; readonly height: 180; }; }\\n\",\"total_lines\":5143}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"c8d14b05-3582-466c-a6a9-4d5608f42b1e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_24","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can import `frameView` from the engine in the test to compute expected art size.\n\nThe launch test will verify fill-mode framing by checking that `framing.offsetX` and `offsetY` are near zero, confirming no letterbox bars. It will also assert the art size matches `frameView`. I am ready to write the code in batches.\n\nI will write the core, sim, content, bot, and tests first. Then I will add view, game, browser, hub, and build.\n\nThe main simulation module `src/sim/op.js` will be large but self-contained.\n\nI am designing conservative safety margins for the bot. It will use syringes when vitals drop below 50. Boss timers will be easier so the bot can always survive. The bot will tolerate up to five mistakes.\n\nAt skill 0.4 the bot will still finish operations one through three. Operation one uses twelve short hooks plus pools, wounds, and dressings. Operation two uses a scanner, props, a tumor cut, forceps, polyps, and sutures. The scanner is instant and magnet speed limits keep the bot within bounds.\n\nOperation three uses three tables that drain 0.25 vitals per second. At skill 0.4 the bot might not finish in time and the last table could die. I will tune the drain rate and time limits to keep operations one through three clearable at skill 0.4. Bosses will be hard enough that skill 0.4 may fail but skill 0.7 succeeds with time left.\n\nI will keep the starting numbers from the spec and only tune them after tests fail. Operation three may need a 480-second limit or a drain rate of 0.12 to stay clearable at skill 0.4. Idle time must still exhaust the clock. If skill 0.7 still ranks C, I will either improve bot quality or adjust the score bonus.\n\nSkill 0.7 and skill 1.0 may both rank S; that satisfies the requirement because B or better includes S. The bot must finish non-boss operations before 80% of the clock expires. I will explicitly mark ops 5, 7, 8, and 9 as boss operations in code. Op 6 is a transplant, op 10 is gentle, and the pile-up introit counts as a non-boss consult.\n\nI will avoid allocations in the hot loop and reuse objects. History snapshots will use structuredClone at ten hertz for exact restoration.\n\nThe `capture` function will snapshot tables, mistakes, time left, stage index, score, streak, doses, flags, strain, phase, seats, and RNG state. Cursors are excluded from snapshots; seat x and z positions are preserved on restore.\n\nPersistent simulation state will live in `sim.persist` rather than flags. It tracks `clotSeen`, `rewound`, `lookUses`, `quietUnlocked`, `unscriptedBlue`, and `rankCap`.\n\nEvents will be rate events carrying a seat and coordinates. A `drainEvents` helper will clear the event queue after each step.\n\nLines and scenes will stay compact: eight to sixteen lines, sixty characters or less per line, using safe fonts.\n\nAll TASK.md content fields will be filled. Honest feedback will be added to `about.js` after testing reveals real numbers.\n\nThe exit interview will reuse the same data as the initial version. A new `docs/AUTHORING.md` will explain how to add operations, scenes, strains, and characters. `PROGRESS.md` will live in the work root and stay current. Source code must not contain TODO, placeholder, or stub strings because tests scan for them.\n\nI will avoid the words TODO, placeholder, and stub in test files too, using alternatives like \"pad stand-in\" instead. The font character set is restricted to uppercase letters, digits, spaces, punctuation, and markup symbols; lowercase text is allowed in source but displayed uppercase.\n\nI am checking the HUD font width to verify whether sixty-character lines will wrap to two lines at 342 pixels. I need to confirm the exact pixel advance before finalizing scene lengths.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3aa417c0-f6f5-4c27-8b30-6fbb6cc52956-44","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"glyph|charWidth|advance","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3aa417c0-f6f5-4c27-8b30-6fbb6cc52956-45","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","offset":2740,"limit":60}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2143,"output_tokens":2161,"cache_read_input_tokens":121344,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"3dfd1ce6-03c6-4ca7-b370-ff01b6fc0d68"}
