rame 1920\\\", frameView({width:1920,height:1080,dpr:1}, {width:480,height:270}, \\\"fill\\\", {width:480,height:270}));\\nconsole.log(\\\"frame 960\\\", frameView({width:960,height:540,dpr:1}, {width:480,height:270}, \\\"fill\\\", {width:480,height:270}));\\nconsole.log(\\\"frame 1280\\\", frameView({width:1280,height:720,dpr:1}, {width:480,height:270}, \\\"fill\\\", {width:480,height:270}));\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Measure HUD glyph width and fill framing\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3%2Fwork/01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4/terminal/call-1ca23e87-eb77-4337-9b1e-634210246616-46.log\",\"total_bytes\":1777}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"228ee11b-dbcc-4b3f-bcb5-9c916378d038"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_26","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Checking character width calculations. Ten characters at 6 pixels each equal 59 pixels.\n\nStory lines will be capped at 60 characters to fit within the 342-pixel width limit. Lines longer than 57 characters wrap to two lines, and anything beyond 114 characters wraps to three. The wrap function will use the 5+1 pixel formula. Ready to write all the code.\n\nPlanning the file structure. Core modules will be written by hand for reliability. Sketching rng.js and config.js with seed-based RNG and live configuration state.\n\nConfig.js will expose resetConfig and setConfig alongside the live config object. Ten slider keys are mapped to difficulty multipliers applied at operation creation, covering vitals drain, bleed drain, mistake penalties, time limits, cursor speed, steady factor, aim assist, tool tolerance, and drain rate.\n\nThe remaining slider keys are mapped to difficulty multipliers. These cover laser burn time, syringe dose, composure gain, quiet mechanics, Second Look costs and duration, strain speed and HP, Dies Irae BPM, Code Blue zone width, and co-op scaling per seat.\n\nA compact entity structure is being sketched with id, kind, x, z, table, radius, done, and hidden fields.\n\nThe entity schema is expanding to cover tooling, mechanics, and state tracking. Path arrays and progress indices will drive scalpel, forceps, and dressing movement. Suture fields include target coordinates, slot tracking, side, amplitude, and tie timing. Laser entities carry heat levels. Gel entities store cell arrays. Syringes track ring status. Fragments use angular rotation and targeting. Generic fields cover health, required tools, window states, real or cyst status, hand count, held-by references, and timers.\n\nEntity fields are being added for bleed status, vital and pain damage on completion, nick and bruise states, and strain part links.\n\nStage definitions will include spawn lists, until conditions such as pool or hook completion, flag checks, time limits, onEnter actions like tool unlocks and quiet mode, and timed wave spawns.\n\nThe createOp flow begins by copying stage definitions and initializing seats.\n\nOperation initialization will scale timeLeft, vitals, and drain by difficulty and config multipliers. Tools will be unlocked from the stage definition, but the syringe will likely remain locked until vitals dip below fifty or the final stage, since the bot may keep vitals high.\n\nBoth skills will use the syringe when vitals fall below fifty-five to keep dose consumption balanced and prevent score gaps.\n\nThe operation begins at stage zero with a history ring, then enters the play phase. The step function accepts inputs as an object keyed by seat ID; missing seats receive no input. If the phase is already won or lost, the function returns immediately. Simulation time delta is multiplied by a devSpeed factor, defaulting to one.\n\nThe step function will return immediately if the simulation is paused. The bot input generator is being sketched with cursor speed scaled by skill level and added noise, plus special handlers for code blue and massage phases before selecting a goal.\n\nBot input now adds per-seat noise to the goal coordinates using sine and cosine offsets. The chooseGoal function is being written as the core decision engine. For suture tasks, the next step is computing the target slot position and its normal vector.\n\nSuture timing is being tuned to the CLEAN window. The bot will alternate target sides every 0.45 seconds, crossing the 0.9-unit gap in about 0.1 seconds at full skill speed. Noise and slot tolerance are kept within safe bounds. The scalpel goal is to move along its path.\n\nPath following will be handled by the simulation based on tip position rather than bot-specific logic. The scalpel goal advances to the next path point once the tip is within 0.3 units of the current one. Forceps will move to an entity, grab it with use true, follow its path, and drop when free. Gel tasks target the nearest unpainted cell with use true. Drain tasks go to the pool center with use true. Laser tasks wait near the target during diastole windows without using, then approach and use true otherwise.\n\nScanner tasks will target the centroid of hidden entities and use true. Fragments require grabbing, setting the target angle, and dropping when close. Dressing follows the same path logic as scalpel. The bot must explicitly switch tables by setting input.table, since tools only affect the active table while other tables still drain vitals. A partner bot is being planned for operation nine.\n\ncreateOp will add a partner seat when partner is true, and the campaign will drive all seats through the bot. The partner goal will target left-side entities or the tether during two-hand pulls. Entities will be tagged with side labels so each seat prefers its own side and shared tools. A Dies Irae diastole timer is being added: the beat advances at the configured BPM, systole lasts the first 35 percent of each cycle, and diastole begins after that.\n\nUsing a tool during systole on the heart will trigger a sloppy nick and block progression. The Code Blue state machine tracks a needle sweep, a 12-second timer, and charging flags per seat. The needle oscillates up and down every 1.2 seconds. Charging is detected when any seat presses both triggers or use plus alt.\n\nCode Blue scoring now counts needle hits inside the blue zone and misses subtract three seconds. Two hits restore vitals to twenty-five and return the phase to play, unless the stage is scripted and caps the rank at A. A second zero-vital collapse kills the bot immediately because Code Blue is a one-time save. Intern difficulty allows two saves.\n\nIntern difficulty grants two Code Blue lives; all others get one. Mistakes now trigger an automatic loss once the configured maximum is reached, though dev mode disables this with a zero value. The bot will count forceps tears and scalpel cuts on healthy tissue as mistakes, while sloppy suture stitches are excluded.\n\nMistake events are now explicitly defined: forceps tears, scalpel cuts on healthy tissue above 0.8, laser burns on healthy tissue exceeding half a second, and false cyst laser hits. Syringe releases outside the ring, drain bruises, and sloppy suture stitches are classified as SLOPPY instead of mistakes. A bad Code Blue attempt is treated as a time penalty rather than a mistake.\n\nClean ratings will be assigned to every successful hook, stitch, and cut. A score streak counter will track consecutive clean actions. Final score and rank are computed only after all stages complete and the phase becomes won. A stable FNV-1a hash of key simulation numbers will be exported so identical seeds and inputs produce deterministic results for test runs. A binary snapshot encoder is being drafted to serialize up to sixty active entities.\n\nThe binary snapshot format packs stage, vitals, time, and score into a header, then encodes each entity as an ID hash, kind, scaled x, z, and angle. A round-trip test will verify that decoded positions stay within 0.02 units of the original and that total size stays under 1100 bytes. Keyframes will be JSON snapshots of the capture output when they remain under 16 KB; fifty entities at roughly two hundred bytes each yield about ten kilobytes, which fits comfortably.\n\nThe keyframe encoder will use TextEncoder to turn JSON snapshots into bytes. Peak entity count will be estimated by concatenating all spawns across every stage and encoding that hypothetical maximum state. A campaign runner is being drafted to iterate through scenes, instantiate each operation with the given seed and skill, and run the bot against it.\n\nThe campaign runner will record each operation's rank and final score into the save object, mark it unlocked, and return the completed save. Tests will verify this by reading the memory store that writeSave uses when localStorage is unavailable. Scene data requires no simulation; the campaign simply records which beats are seen. A runSim helper is being added to wrap the step loop with a default sixty-second timeout.\n\nThe runSim helper now steps the simulation until the phase is no longer play, codeblue, or massage, capped at sixty seconds. Idle input returns an empty object. A factory pattern with auto-incrementing IDs is being introduced for operation content, starting with pool and hook generators.\n\nHooks are generated with a path curve and pull distance. Hidden hooks sit under pools and reveal only after the pool is drained.\n\nOperation two uses magnets that snap at high speed. The bot will cap its movement to eighty percent of each magnet's speed limit when pulling one.\n\nOperation six has a cold-time bar that counts down from 120 seconds during transplant. The bot should finish before it expires.\n\nA visibility filter will hide entities that are marked done, hidden but unmarked, or tied to a pool that is still active. When a pool completes, any entity tagged with hiddenBy that pool will be revealed automatically.\n\nGel will heal nicks after one second by marking them done. Stages that do not explicitly require nicks to be cleared will ignore them as problems.\n\nNicks spawned by mistakes will not drain vitals, so they cannot snowball. The bot can optionally gel them. Dressing is the final stage; completing it ends the operation and triggers a twelve-hook gag.\n\nThe twelve-hook gag will be handled as a single stage that spawns a bandage and the hook line during dressing, then waits for both to finish. The bot must also handle \"until kinds\" checks when no entities of that kind have spawned yet.\n\nA stage is considered complete only when every entity it spawned is done. If a stage requests no entities of a kind, it clears immediately because none were required to exist. Waves append their spawned IDs to the stage's list so they are tracked for completion.\n\nStrain logic will live in `src/sim/strains.js` and export `tickStrain` and `startStrain`. Each stage can declare a strain such as `introit`, which spawns entities and sets a completion flag. Operation eight enters a massage phase after the plates, counting beats through eight alternations before the final cut. Operation five will run an optional open stage, then the introit strain, followed by the eggs laser, suture, gel, and dressing stages.\n\nThe view layer will use a canvas tissue mesh with a heightAt function, a hand model built from buildModel, and simple tool meshes parented to the hand. HUD.js will draw vitals, clock, composure, comm, wheel, and prompts. menu.js will manage the menu state machine, and game.js will host the Clinic class. Screenshots will feature a dark room with a box table, cylinder lamp, point light, and title EKG line; during surgery, the colored tissue plane, drapes as boxes, visible hand, and HUD will be rendered.\n\nThe camera will drift each frame. Mouse aim will raycast against the tissue heightAt function. The hand model uses floats so it can float above the floor. In story mode the field hides and a bust group appears with simple box figures. SFX register during setup. Songs are stored as data and playMusic runs on the title screen. Voice renders as PCM, wraps into a WAV, and loads via audio.load.\n\nA `renderSyllable` helper will generate 22050 Hz Float32 voice samples for Node tests. It synthesizes glottal pulses and formants. A `lineAudio` function mixes syllables into full lines. Tests verify the output is not silent and finite.\n\nThe settings screen will list options. Its music slider controls `audio.setVolume`. The value is stored in `app.settings` and the localStorage game key. The engine audio volume stays separate from the UI mirror.\n\nFresh profiles default music to 0.1 and persist any change. Dev sliders appear in pause mode and adjust each DEFAULTS key with left/right controls plus a reset button. After ten seconds of idle time the game enters an attract demo that cycles through operations with a god-mode bot on operation one, displaying a banner that exits to the title on any input without saving. A DOM overlay div `#touch-gate` will hold the demo text, while the engine settings button stays separate.\n\nThe couch consult menu will let up to four local seats join without network calls. The session path in game.js must precede the engine initialization.\n\nsession.js will not be modified. The built HTML will include the necessary head tags. The harness and its bridge and round scripts will be copied into dist/ next to game.html so the harness can serve them from the same directory. Tests will run against that dist folder. URL flags will support operation, stage, difficulty, seed, bot mode, god mode, attract mode, dev mode, and scene selection.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-6c52197f-f4f5-4049-9024-5cf30fec34b4-47","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"errors|gpuErrors","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","head_limit":20}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-6c52197f-f4f5-4049-9024-5cf30fec34b4-48","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md","offset":140,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1166,"output_tokens":5973,"cache_read_input_tokens":127104,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"ef73f1b9-8ada-497a-838d-eae437f645e9"}
