0,108,101,115,115,86,105,101,119,96,32,116,104,97,116,32,100,114,97,119,115,32,110,111,116,104,105,110,103,44,32,105,110,32,78,111,100,101,46,32,78,111,32,68,79,77,44,32,110,111,32,71,80,85,44,32,110,111,32,108,105,115,116,101,110,101,114,115,44,32,110,111,32,65,117,100,105,111,67,111,110,116,101,120,116,32,40,96,97,117,100,105,111,46,99,111,117,110,116,115,96,32,115,116,105,108,108,32,99,111,117,110,116,115,41,44,32,110,111,32,112,97,110,101,108,115,59,32,101,114,114,111,114,115,32,103,111,32,116,111,32,96,101,110,103,105,110,226,128,166,10,49,51,55,58,45,32,96,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,101,114,114,111,114,115,58,32,115,116,114,105,110,103,91,93,96,58,32,69,114,114,111,114,115,32,116,104,114,111,119,110,32,98,121,32,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,104,111,111,107,115,32,119,104,105,108,101,32,116,104,101,32,114,101,110,100,101,114,32,108,111,111,112,32,114,97,110,44,32,101,97,99,104,32,109,101,115,115,97,103,101,32,111,110,99,101,44,32,111,108,100,101,115,116,32,102,105,114,115,116,32,40,97,116,32,109,111,115,116,32,50,48,41,46,32,84,104,101,121,32,97,114,101,32,108,111,103,103,101,100,32,116,111,32,116,104,101,32,99,111,110,115,111,108,101,32,97,110,100,32,115,104,111,119,110,32,105,110,32,97,32,98,111,120,32,111,110,32,115,99,114,101,101,110,59,32,116,104,101,32,102,114,97,109,101,32,107,101,101,112,115,32,114,101,110,100,101,114,105,110,103,32,115,111,32,116,104,101,32,112,105,99,116,117,114,101,32,115,116,97,121,115,32,117,112,46,32,70,105,120,32,116,104,101,32,102,105,114,115,116,32,111,110,101,32,102,105,114,115,116,46,32,40,96,115,116,101,112,40,41,96,32,116,104,114,111,119,226,128,166,10,51,55,48,58,45,32,96,100,101,98,117,103,73,110,102,111,63,58,32,98,111,111,108,101,97,110,96,58,32,84,104,101,32,100,101,98,117,103,32,112,97,110,101,108,58,32,102,114,97,109,101,32,114,97,116,101,44,32,99,97,109,101,114,97,44,32,71,80,85,32,101,114,114,111,114,115,32,97,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,115,116,97,116,117,115,46,10,49,48,54,54,58,45,32,96,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,103,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,115,58,32,71,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,82,101,99,111,114,100,91,93,96,10,52,51,53,56,58,45,32,96,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,103,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,115,58,32,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,71,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,82,101,99,111,114,100,91,93,96,10,53,48,51,57,58,45,32,96,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,103,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,115,58,32,114,101,97,100,111,110,108,121,32,71,112,117,69,114,114,111,114,82,101,99,111,114,100,91,93,96,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":6,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":136,\"content\":\"- `static headless(game: Game, options: HeadlessOptions = {}): Promise<Engine>`: The engine without a page (docs/engine/headless.md): the same boot, game code and frames as `start`, with a `HeadlessView` that draws nothing, in Node. No DOM, no GPU, no listeners, no AudioContext (`audio.counts` still counts), no panels; errors go to `engin…\"},{\"line_number\":137,\"content\":\"- `readonly errors: string[]`: Errors thrown by the game's hooks while the render loop ran, each message once, oldest first (at most 20). They are logged to the console and shown in a box on screen; the frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throw…\"},{\"line_number\":370,\"content\":\"- `debugInfo?: boolean`: The debug panel: frame rate, camera, GPU errors and the game's status.\"},{\"line_number\":1066,\"content\":\"- `readonly gpuErrors: GpuErrorRecord[]`\"},{\"line_number\":4358,\"content\":\"- `readonly gpuErrors: readonly GpuErrorRecord[]`\"},{\"line_number\":5039,\"content\":\"- `readonly gpuErrors: readonly GpuErrorRecord[]`\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6c52197f-f4f5-4049-9024-5cf30fec34b4-48","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"140→- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\\n150→- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\\n- `frame: number`\\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\\n- `time: number`\\n- `readonly renderer: View`\\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\\n- `camera: CameraRig`\\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\\n160→- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\\n170→- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\\n180→- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\",\"content_concise\":\"140→- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\\n150→- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\\n- `frame: number`\\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\\n- `time: number`\\n- `readonly renderer: View`\\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\\n- `camera: CameraRig`\\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\\n160→- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\\n170→- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\\n180→- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/API.md\",\"offset\":140,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"- `readonly ambient: AmbientLight`\\n- `readonly context: GameContext`\\n- `readonly audio: AudioManager`\\n- `readonly particles: Particles`\\n- `readonly hud: Hud`\\n- `readonly tweens: Tweens`: Tweens on game time (`ctx.tweens`).\\n- `readonly shake: CameraShake`: Screen shake (trauma 0..1): `engine.shake.add(0.4)`. Added to the camera after the rig placed it, on real time; ortho cameras move by whole art pixels. Reset on level unload.\\n- `debug: DebugUI | null`\\n- `touch: TouchControls | null`\\n- `readonly settings: Settings`: The player's settings: look, pixel art, pixel size, quality, frame rate and debug info (plain data, saved per game), applied over the game's own choices; `open()` shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button, `EngineOptions.settings`). Sound is `audio`'s.\\n- `debugKeys: DebugKeyMap`: Active engine hotkeys (from EngineOptions.debugKeys; none until the engine starts).\\n- `manual: boolean`: Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`. Frame-exact and independent of how fast the browser renders (filmstrips, replays).\\n- `frame: number`\\n- `fps: number`: Rendered frames per second (measured over ~0.5 s).\\n- `readonly qualityOption: QualityOption`: Requested quality (`auto` adapts); `quality` is the level in use.\\n- `time: number`\\n- `readonly renderer: View`\\n- `readonly physics: Physics`\\n- `camera: CameraRig`\\n- `readonly scene: Scene`\\n- `get gpu: PixelRenderer | null`: The page's renderer, for direct GPU work (readbacks, the canvas, compute): null headless (`Engine.headless`), where nothing is drawn. Gameplay reads `renderer`, which both have.\\n- `get game: Game`: The running game. Replace it with `loadGame()`.\\n- `get paused: boolean`: Freeze simulation, animation, particles and game time (rendering continues). For tooling, capture and pause menus. Queued key presses are dropped while paused.\\n- `get lights: LightPool`: The dynamic light pool (render/lights.ts), engine-owned: its lights stay in the scene across levels, so loading another level never changes the light count (no recompiles). Created on first use; sized by quality.\\n- `get timeScale: number`: Game speed: 1 normal, 0.25 slow motion, 2 double, 0 frozen (a menu: key presses made meanwhile are dropped, like a pause). Scales game time, physics, animation, particles, tweens and the camera follow; not transitions, shake or the render loop. Back to 1 when…\\n- `hitstop(seconds: number): void`: Freeze the game for `seconds` of real time: impact frames that sell a hit. Rendering, screen shake and transitions keep going; overlapping hitstops keep the longest. Key presses made during a hitstop still count afterwards (a buffered jump).\\n- `get screen: ScreenFx`: Transitions, flashes and shockwaves (render/screenFx.ts): `await engine.screen.cover('iris')`.\\n- `get sunDir: Vector3`: The direction sunlight comes from (unit, pointing at the sun).\\n- `setSunDirection(dir: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number]): void`: Point the sun (and its shadows): a direction toward the sun, normalised here. A level's time of day or a sun dial; reset to the engine default when the level unloads. Below the horizon the sun is kept just above it (lower `sun.intensity` for night instead).\\n- `get quality: QualityLevel`: The quality level in use (see `EngineOptions.quality`).\\n- `useQuality(option: QualityOption): void`: Quality by option: a level, or `auto`: the device's level (low on phones and tablets, else medium), lowered once if a few seconds of frames are slow (measured once, then remembered).\\n- `get debugShown: boolean`: Whether the debug panel is showing.\\n- `showDebug(on: boolean): void`: Show or hide the debug panel (created the first time it shows, filled at once: the loop fills it only while the game runs).\\n- `setQuality(level: QualityLevel): void`\\n- `get maxFps: number`: Frame cap of the render loop (0 = display rate).\\n- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\\n- `toggleMode(): RenderMode`\\n- `toggleResolution(): Resolution`\\n- `get availableFilters: readonly string[]`: Every filter id the engine ships (see render/filters.ts).\\n- `get filters: readonly string[]`\\n- `setFilters(ids: readonly string[]): void`\\n- `get look: Look`: The current look (a copy): per-layer pixel art and filters, whole-scene filters.\\n- `get lookPresets: Readonly<Record<string, Look>>`: Every named look (LOOK_PRESETS): `engine.setLook(engine.lookPresets.pixel_heroes)`.\\n- `setLook(look: Look): void`: Apply a look (render/look.ts); `LOOK_PRESETS` has named ones.\\n- `cycleLook(step: 1 | -1): string`: Cycle through LOOK_PRESETS ([ and ] keys).\\n- `state(): { version: string; game: string; seed: number; ready: boolean; paused: boolean; time: number; timeScale: number; hitstop: number; screen: ScreenFxState; backend: BackendName; ... 19 more ...; status: string; }`: Snapshot for tests, tooling and agents.\\n- `step(n = 1, dt = 1 / 60): void`: Tooling: switch to manual time and advance `n` frames of `dt` seconds (simulation, animation, camera). Nothing is drawn; call `renderer.capture()` to see the result.\\n- `loadGame(game: Game, options: { camera?: CameraConfig } = {}): Promise<void>`: Unload the running game and start `game` in the same engine (renderer, canvas, camera rig, input, audio context and debug UI stay).\\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop the loop and free everything: game, scene, physics, audio, input listeners, DOM, GPU. With a `loadGame()` in flight, the world and GPU resources are freed once its `setup()` has finished (it never runs against a freed physics world or renderer).\\n- `get seed: number`: The seed every level's `ctx.random` comes from (`EngineOptions.seed`, `?seed=`; a replay sets it).\\n\",\"total_lines\":5143}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"e8c854cd-9020-4faa-b6f8-84e9ae191150"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_27","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I understand the goal and am ready to begin.\n\nI'll write the simulation and tests first, then update PROGRESS.md and the plan checklist as I go. A dev index.html will load game.js for testing, while the final deliverable is a single bundled HTML file.\n\nThe introit strain is being designed as a simple state machine. It moves along a spline around the safe zone, spawning a pore every six seconds. If the pore is not completed, it spawns a laceration and pool instead. Once completed, it spawns a core with forceps, tether, and pull mechanics.\n\nThe introit core mechanics are now defined. Successful pulls reduce health, spawn two lacerations, and increase speed by 25%. Timeouts trigger a snap laceration instead. When health reaches zero, the strain dies and spawns six egg laser targets. Final stats scale by config.strainHp and config.strainSpeed.\n\nThe lacrimosa strain is being designed with five eyes, each with three hit points. Eyes open sequentially rather than simultaneously to keep the bot-friendly. Each open eye becomes a laser target.\n\nLacrimosa eye mechanics are now defined. Each eye opens for eight seconds and heals if left untouched. Droplets spawn while the eye is open, moving toward the tissue at 1.5 units per second. They drain within 0.7 seconds; reaching the tissue or z < -3.2 causes a nick and a vital loss.\n\nAt half total health, the eyes crawl slowly and droplets drift toward the tip. The last eye performs Sob three times before forceps can pull its stalk. The bot must finish four eyes first, then manage the final eye's repeated sob attacks.\n\nThe lacrimosa eye mechanics are simplified. When the last eye reaches zero health, it enters a Sob phase if fewer than three have been performed. Each Sob spawns a gel line for six seconds; failure costs twenty-five vitals. After three Sobs, the eye dies and its stalk appears.\n\nDies Irae is now defined as seven plates. Each plate spawns a scalpel during diastole. Cutting it triggers a short forceps lift. Lifting it spawns a laser eye that burns for about 0.7 seconds. Completing a plate increases the heart rate by six beats per minute. Once the rate reaches one hundred twenty, fibrillation begins.\n\nDies Irae will trigger a scripted code blue after seven plates, followed by an eighth beat and a final scalpel. Wrath flares will occasionally spawn pools. The BPM syringe reduces heart rate by twelve beats per minute while healing. At seventy-two beats per minute, the diastole window is about half a second; at one hundred eight, it shrinks to roughly three tenths of a second. A short scalpel action takes only five hundredths of a second, so both remain well within the available window. The bot must avoid holding use during systole; if a target entity appears outside the diastole phase, the bot will stay near the start and wait.\n\nLibera Me stage one is now defined. Eight real cysts are hidden among four false ones. The scanner reveals which are genuine and which are fake. Lasering a false cyst counts as a mistake and does not advance the stage. Lasering a real cyst completes it. Once all eight real cysts are destroyed, vitals snap to forty-one and the stage clears. The monitor displays ninety-nine while the lie persists. True vitals drain normally but remain hidden from the display. The stage should finish quickly because true vitals barely move.\n\nLibera Me stage two is now defined. It runs three concurrent subsystems: one introit with one hit point, two eyes with one hit point each, and three plates. Entities are tagged left or right to split the work between partners. The bot will prioritize timed actions like the introit pore over sequential plate sequences.\n\nLibera Me stage three is now defined as a ghost copy. The ghost records the bot's tip and use history, then replays scalpel cuts at old positions after a delay. If a replayed cut lands more than 0.8 units from any valid target, it spawns a nick and mistake. Completed targets will remain ghost-safe for four seconds so the replay does not punish the bot.\n\nThe ghost copy mechanics are confirmed safe. Done target zones remain ghost-safe, so replayed cuts on healthy tissue trigger punishment.\n\nOperation 10 is now defined. The spine requires four slow extractions with forceps at a speed limit of 0.8. Each extraction adds eight vitals and one pain. Afterward comes suturing, gel application, and dressing. Drain is 0.05 over 240 seconds.\n\nOperation 4 bone is now defined. Six fragments must be grabbed, rotated, and released. Two fragments are hidden under pools. Each fragment carries a random angle and a target angle. Afterward comes gel seams.\n\nOperation 4 bone now includes an artery bleeder suture with fast drain, a scripted code blue, and final suturing. Fragment rotation will snap to the target angle immediately when the bot is in range, dropping with a five-degree clean threshold or a fifteen-degree snap threshold.\n\nOperation 6 is now defined as a seven-stage sequence. It opens the scalpel, places two clamps by holding within 0.45 units for 0.4 seconds, cuts the vessels, lifts the kidney with forceps, fetches the donor from the ice tray, sutures three times, and removes the clamps in order.\n\nSkill noise on fragment rotation is also set: at skill 0.7 the error is three degrees, at 0.4 it is six degrees, and at full skill it is zero.\n\nOperation 6 now includes a rewound-clot safety net. The first unclamp triggers a dramatic burst that drops vitals by twenty and spawns a pool; the bot can rewind to undo it. After success the kidney flag turns pink, the scanner shows the sample, and dressing follows.\n\nOperation 2 is being drafted as a scalpel midline cut with pools along the path, hidden scanner props, short lance cuts followed by forceps, and a magnet speed limit for the lances.\n\nOperation 2 now includes a closed scalpel path around the tumor, forceps lift, five laser polyps, and final drain with suturing, gel, and dressing.\n\nOperation 3 is being drafted as three tables with local coordinate spaces. The camera will whip pan between them. Tasks are batched across stages, and quiet mode unlocks at seventy percent completion.\n\nOperation 3 now includes a quiet mode that unlocks at seventy percent completion. Losing any table triggers a code blue for that table and fails the operation, since all tables drain from the start.\n\nConsult is defined as a twenty-hundred-second pile-up with waves at twenty-five-second intervals, a small introit near the end, and a one-seat drain for solo balance.\n\nPar is fixed in the operation definition; the test will assert that par equals the final score.\n\nA calibration script will be run. Retry-from-stage and rank cap tests are being added to the verification plan alongside existing checks for tools, Quiet mode, Second Look restore, Code Blue behavior, slider state, deterministic hashing, codec round-trip, and Resident balance.\n\nThe frame loop is now ordered. Inputs and cursor movement run at real speed. Tool switching and table changes follow. Gifts are handled next, with look restoring state and returning early. Code blue and massage steps run before applying tools. Strain ticks, vitals, and clock advance in simulation time. Stage logic, fail and win checks, history sampling, and events complete the cycle. Quiet mode drains composure in real time while cursors move at full speed; the clock uses simulation time.\n\nQuiet mode ends when composure reaches zero. Gift activation requires at least twenty percent composure to start and continues until the button is released or composure drops below that threshold; it cannot be restarted without refilling composure first. Second look is a press-and-release gift, unlike quiet which is held. In operation nine seat zero uses quiet and seat one uses look.\n\nOperation nine now includes a tag input that lets the human switch which seat is controlled by the player. The other seat remains bot-controlled. Campaign mode keeps both seats as bots, so no tag is needed there.\n\nSecond look will restore state only when the gift button is newly pressed, composure covers the cost, the phase is not code blue, and the history buffer is long enough. Quiet mode will be blocked during code blue. The implementation will prioritize correctness and iterate with tests. Files will be organized into op.js for the main logic, content/ops.js for operation definitions, and a separate debug runner at tests/cal.mjs; the main runner will print only failures.\n\nCalibration will run as a separate command during development. The simulation seed is fixed at one for balance, and the bot remains deterministic because its randomness derives only from simulation state and skill rather than extra sources. Empty input leaves the cursor idle and prevents accidental tool activation. Forceps will target entities within a 0.45-unit radius, with exact arrival at full skill.\n\nPath pulls measure distance along the curve from the start point. Completion requires reaching the full path length. Deviation from the closest segment exceeding one unit tears the entity, spawns a nick, releases the grab, and counts a mistake.\n\nPath pulls will be rated CLEAN if mean deviation is 0.12 or less, GOOD up to 0.3, and SLOPPY otherwise.\n\nScalpel progress requires visiting points in order. The bot advances only when the tip is within 0.25 of the current target point. Deviation above 0.8 spawns a pool once and blocks advancement until the tip returns. Progress does not begin until the tip has been within 0.5 of the start point while the use button is held.\n\nSuture slots are spaced every 0.45 units along the wound length, with a minimum of two. Curve paths will stay inside a safe zone bounded by x ±6 and z between −3.6 and 3.0, avoiding the bottom-right corner. A new `src/sim/api.js` will re-export the public test API for unified imports. The 6-second knock timer uses simulation time, so quiet mode correctly slows it down. If vitals are already zero, entering code blue happens before quiet mode can take effect.\n\nQuiet mode prevents code blue by halting vitals decay. The final stage triggers an immediate win and score even if the clock is low. Rank par will use integer math: time bonus and vitals are floored before scoring, since floating-point drift could shift a one-point difference between runs.\n\nSecond look costs five percent of the final score. Skill 1.0 on operation six is forced to use it once, so the par already includes that penalty. Quiet mode does not give a skill advantage because both skill levels use it identically.\n\nQuiet mode will be used only when vitals fall below thirty-five. This prevents score differences from varying vitals decay rather than action quality. The drain and speed parameters are being tuned so both skill levels can keep vitals above that threshold. For operation one, the action count is estimated at two pools, twelve hooks, six stitches, gel, and dressing. Drain success is not rated; it is only penalized as sloppy on dry.\n\nDrain completion will rate CLEAN when no bruise appears. The operation base score is roughly 2100 points, with streak bonuses pushing the ceiling near 2940. Skill 1.0 should reach about 4740 total, while skill 0.7 should land around 4175, yielding an 88 percent S rank.\n\nOperations with few actions and long time limits will see the time bonus dominate, keeping both skill levels close to each other. If skill 1.0 finishes much faster, the ratio should still hold up.\n\nFor skill 0.4 fail-bosses, the introit pore window will be three seconds while the core path remains long. Skill 0.4 speed and delay may cause misses that accumulate mistakes or vitals loss. The requirement is only for operations one through three, not for boss failure. Tests will verify this and parameters will be tuned.\n\nThe idle fast path will still step at 60 Hz to keep code blue timing accurate. The first plan checklist item covers boot from file:// and requires the view, so I will write the simulation core first, then the view, and check that box only after it is truly done.\n\nI will write a single large simulation file and a single large game file to reduce tool calls. I am also drafting the suture crossing logic in my head to avoid bugs.\n\nSuture crossing logic now tracks the signed deviation from the curve and the largest amplitude reached. A slot is crossed only when the cursor is within 0.4 times the tool tolerance of the slot position. The action rates CLEAN if the time interval between ties is between 0.2 and 0.7 seconds, otherwise GOOD.\n\nSuture crossing now rates SLOPPY when the tie interval is outside 0.1 to 1.1 seconds. A missed slot triggers a SLOPPY rating and blocks advancement until the stitch is retried. The amplitude tracker resets after each cross, and the side sign updates correctly.\n\nThe suture side tracker initializes to zero, so the first frame does not count as a cross. A simulated oscillation from +0.45 to −0.45 correctly triggers a slot tie once the amplitude reaches 0.25 and the cursor nears the slot. The scalpel step function is being drafted to require the tip to be within half the tool tolerance of the start point before path progression begins.\n\nScalpel deviation is tracked as the distance to the current target point. A deviation above 0.8 spawns a pool and counts a mistake, but only once per segment; the flag resets when the tip returns closer than 0.8. Advancement to the next path point requires the tip to be within 0.28 of that point. Once the full path is completed, the action rates CLEAN if the mean deviation is 0.12 or less, GOOD up to 0.3, and SLOPPY otherwise.\n\nPath points will be spaced 0.15 units apart with a 0.3-unit advancement threshold so the bot reliably catches each point even at high speed. The seat movement function is now being drafted.\n\nThe seat movement function clamps coordinates to the room bounds when placing. It seeks directly to a target point when the seek flag is set. Otherwise it moves with cursor speed scaled by a 2.2 power of the stick magnitude, applying the steady factor only when steady mode is active.\n\nThe seat movement function now interpolates toward a seek target at a speed capped by the cursor speed multiplied by the time step. Stick aim assist will reduce movement speed to sixty percent when the cursor is near a valid target, but only for human input; the bot uses seek instead of stick. The view passes mouse clicks as place commands with coordinates derived from raycasting.\n\nSteady mode is unnecessary for the bot at skill 1.0. I will install Playwright 1.63.0 in the dev folder so browser tests can run in parallel while I write code. The test runner will use explicit relative imports instead of setting package.json to ESM.\n\nThe kit currently lacks a package.json, so Node cannot import the existing .js files as ES modules. I will create one with type: module so the test runner can import them, while leaving the HTML engine untouched.\n\nI will write PROGRESS.md and create the simulation core in a single file. Operation par values will start at zero; a calibration run will compute the true baseline, and the test will print mismatches so I can update them. I am also verifying gel coverage.\n\nGel coverage will use a 3×3 grid of cells spaced 0.5 units apart, with a brush radius of 0.8. The action finishes when 92 percent of the area is painted. Nicks heal automatically if the cursor stays within 0.8 units of a painted cell for one second.\n\nFor laser stray, the bot accumulates heat while aiming at a target within 0.45 units. If it misses all targets for more than half a second, it triggers a burn mistake.\n\nLaser heat ends the action once it reaches one. The rating depends on stray accumulated during that target; zero stray yields CLEAN.\n\nScanner marks hidden entities within 2.2 units.\n\nSyringe draws a ring over time while held. Releasing after at least 0.45 seconds injects a dose if the cursor is inside the ring; success rates CLEAN, failure rates SLOPPY.\n\nThe injection ring radius is set to 0.8 units. A seat that releases the syringe before 0.45 seconds of drawing counts as an early release with no dose consumed. Multiple seats can operate different entities, and each grabbed object is held by a single seat. Drain scaling will apply only to human seats, not to bot partners, so solo operations remain unaffected.\n\nThe design is now complete. I will write the simulation core and game view in a single large file, then split them into focused modules: config, RNG, meta, save, op, bot, codec, and strains.\n\nThe module layout now includes campaign, content, audio, play, tests, tools, and docs. I will write the simulation files first. To avoid a circular import between strains.js and op.js, tick functions will be defined in strains.js and called by op.js without importing back from it.\n\nThe module dependency graph is now fixed: strains.js will not import op.js, so no circular cycle exists. bot.js will remain standalone and only read the simulation state. campaign will import bot, op, and content. The content module will export a TOOLS list and an OPS list. Operations will be named op1 through op10 plus consult, and scenes will be named ep1 through ep10 plus ending.\n\nStory text will be ASCII-safe and kept under sixty characters per line. Character voices are the lines themselves. The cast list goes in story.js. Forbidden names and placeholder words like TODO or stub are avoided. Wide shots may use simple boxy surgeons if they read clearly, but the plan favors custom rigs for HERO_CLIPS.\n\nA custom rig figure with joint names will be included so animations work, though static poses still show silhouettes if the rig is buggy. The build order is: working simulation and tests first, then the visible surgery scene with tissue, hand, and HUD, followed by title, menus, bust, results, story scenes that advance, audio, and finally the hub. Writing will use a compact style while npm install runs in the background.\n\nThe dev folder path is set. Files will be written in batches, starting with config, RNG, meta, and save before the main simulation module. Hash stability will exclude the events array, history buffer, and input edge flags so rating popups do not alter the hash.\n\nThe simulation hash will include entities, vitals, score, time, RNG state, seat positions, and phase. A determinism test will run the same seed twice to confirm identical hashes. A slider test will verify that doubling the vitals drain multiplier roughly doubles the one-second vitals drop.\n\nThe simulation will hold a live reference to the global config object so that slider changes affect running operations. Difficulty multipliers will be applied at read time.\n\nThe drain formula will multiply the operation base drain by the difficulty multiplier and the live config vitalsDrainMul each frame. A second-look test is being drafted to verify that snapshotting cursors, manually altering vitals or mistakes, pressing look, and stepping forward yields a state matching four seconds ago with unchanged cursor positions.\n\nThe second-look test will use a ring buffer of eighty samples captured every tenth of a second. On rewind, the system selects the stored snapshot nearest to four seconds ago.\n\nThe second-look test will step five seconds before rewinding to ensure the history buffer is full. Exact vitals equality between capture and restore is guaranteed because floating-point values are stored and restored precisely. The cursor position is not saved in snapshots, so the test will assert that the seat coordinates remain unchanged after rewind. To enable the look gift for any operation, the test will either configure the operation with a look gift or use a debug flag to unlock it.\n\ncreateOp will accept minimal custom definitions for tool unit tests, not just full content operations. A drain-success test will spawn a bleeding pool and verify the drain tool completes its stage.\n\nDrain tests will verify a pool shrinks from 1.6 to 0.2 units in about one second, and that using drain away from a pool produces a bruise and SLOPPY rating. A `within` helper will check seat proximity to entities. Forceps tests will confirm a straight path yields CLEAN, while a path that veers off by 1.5 units triggers a nick and mistake.\n\nA fifth mistake-fail test and a clock-fail test are being added. The code-blue test will set vitals to 0.01 and step once with drain to trigger the phase change. It will also verify that the needle position over time is known so the shock can be released in the correct zone.\n\nA timeout test will step without input for thirteen seconds and assert the operation is lost. A quiet test will enable quiet mode, step one second with a gift, and verify that timeLeft drops by about 0.25 while vitals stay flat and composure falls by roughly eighteen. Composure drain will only apply when quiet is active.\n\nRank bounds tests will verify the exact score thresholds for each letter grade. A pristine condition will be added so a perfect score with zero mistakes yields an S rating. Playwright will be installed in parallel. The tissue painter and game view will be written only after the simulation tests pass, to avoid debugging both systems at once.\n\nBot goal selection will use a fixed priority list for entity kinds, from pore to organ. Special cases are handled before the general entity search: code blue, rewind requests, syringe sequences when vitals are low and doses remain, scanner use on hidden unmarked objects, and massage phases.\n\nThe bot will hold the syringe at the ring for half a second before releasing it. It will also wait near an entity that requires diastole if the current phase is not diastole.\n\nThe simulation switches the active table before applying tool inputs, so a same-frame switch and use is valid. A `botAll` helper will generate inputs for every non-human seat in a single pass. In tests all seats are bots. Op 9 places seat 0 on the right side and seat 1 on the left.\n\nchooseGoal will restrict each seat to its assigned side, preferring the closer entity when both are available. Single-seat operations will target only seat 0. Two-hand entities require both seats to grab opposite ends; the simulation tracks heldBy within 0.45 units and counts both seats as holding once the required hand count is met.\n\nA two-hand entity will complete in 1.2 seconds once both seats hold it within 0.5 units and pull together, rated CLEAN. The step function will ignore unknown input fields. A devSpeed setting will let the view accelerate frames, though tests will not use it. The operation JSON will be logged to the console for debugging. A meta title will be added. The main op.js module is being composed now.\n\nGel cells will be stored as a bitset and packed by the codec. Snapshot size is estimated at under 1100 bytes for peak operations, including gel cells and entity paths. If JSON keyframes grow too large, only progress indices will be kept to stay within the 16KB limit.\n\nThe test suite will verify two distinct round-trip paths: a lossless JSON keyframe restore and a lossy fixed-point snapshot. A compact `check` helper will tally passes and failures, print only failing cases with an optional extra message, and exit with code 1 if any test fails.\n\nThe test runner will print only failures and a single pass/fail count line. Browser tests require the built HTML and will be skipped if it is missing. A filter argument can exclude them, and this behavior will be documented in the runner source.\n\nThe test runner will run simulation and content checks by default. Browser and hub tests will be excluded unless explicitly requested, since the verification requires only the core suite to pass.\n\nThe verification will be run as a single test command that also launches the game twice, saves screenshots, and captures browser output. Hub harness output, package hashes, screenshots, and audio will all be gathered in one pass.\n\nThe default test command will run only the fast simulation, content, and audio checks. Browser, hub, launch, and all tests will be available as optional filters. This prevents the full suite from timing out or running out of memory.\n\nThe full campaign playthrough will take roughly 30 to 60 seconds. The test runner can fast-forward idle play phases with a larger time step, then switch back to fine steps once vitals drop low enough for code blue.\n\nIdle simulation will substep at 30 Hz to keep the campaign fast without skipping code-blue timing.\n\nBot simulation will use a 1/60-second step to preserve suture rhythm and scalpel precision. Idle phases will step at 0.1 seconds for speed, but timers must be written to tolerate large dt without crashing.\n\nPath point spacing is set to 0.25 and catch radius to 0.4 so the 1/60-second step never skips a point. A substep cap for idle fast-forward was rejected because it would still be slow; instead, timers for drain, clock, and code blue will be written to tolerate large time steps.\n\nThe step function will clamp dt to 0.25 seconds and return immediately if the operation is already won or lost, preventing vitals from dropping too fast and avoiding double code-blue triggers. The idle test will loop until the operation ends. Stage completion logic will also be hardened against overflow.\n\nThe scalpel spawn pool will be limited to one per excursion to prevent cascading mistakes. Forceps will enforce a speed limit: if the pull rate exceeds the entity’s cap, it snaps back by 0.4 units instead of tearing. During a code-blue phase change, tool inputs that frame the operation will be ignored.\n\nA grab requires the bot to be within 0.45 units, use the forceps tool, and not already hold an object. When a pulled entity reaches full length it is freed; releasing it then completes the operation with a rating. If the bot releases before the entity is free, the tether snaps back and spawns a laceration, which counts as a mistake.\n\nThe bot will track whether an entity is grabbed by checking `e.grabbedBy === seat.id`. A `linePath` helper will generate path points from the entity outward, with the start point at the entity position.\n\nThe `linePath` helper generates evenly spaced points along a line from the entity origin outward, with endpoints clamped to bounds and hooks pulled toward +z or a safe direction based on position. A new `arcPath` helper will produce curved paths using an angle parameter.\n\nThe arcPath helper will use a small curve and short length to keep the zone intact. A `pathMetrics` function is being added to compute the closest point on a polyline for rating deviation.\n\nThe `pathMetrics` helper now computes the closest point on a polyline segment using projection and clamping. Pulled progress will only advance forward, snapping back on tether snaps. If deviation exceeds 1 unit, the entity tears and resets its grab state without completing.\n\nA minimal boot script will be added to satisfy the first checkbox. The simulation code will be kept compact but readable.\n\nFor operations 9 and 10, a lie flag will hide real vitals from the monitor display. A shownVitals helper will return 99 when lying is active.\n\nFollow-up lines by rank will be stored on patient.followUp.S/A/B/C. All four ranks will be written short.\n\nA `lines.js` reaction pool will supply short HUD comm lines during surgery. The `enterStage` function spawns stage entities and tracks their IDs, but it skips the strain timer when a stage has no spawn IDs.\n\nThe `enterStage` function now unlocks tools, sets the quiet flag, and assigns stage-specific speech. Tools that are not unlocked do nothing when used; the bot only picks unlocked tools. The syringe is unlocked from the start so the bot can use it immediately. A teaching line about the syringe may still appear later, but the tool itself will be functional.\n\nThe syringe will unlock when vitals fall below 50 or at stage index four. The scanner, scalpel, and laser remain locked until their operations include them in the tool list. A dressing tool will be contextual: it is not on the wheel but becomes available when a dressing entity is the goal.\n\nA `makeEntity` helper will fill missing fields from entity specs, defaulting position to zero, radius to 0.5, and path to null. Paddles are code-blue inputs rather than tools.\n\nmakeEntity now initializes every entity field from its spec, including free, grabbedBy, speedLimit, tether, x2, z2, slots, sideSign, amp, lastTie, heat, cells, ang, targetAng, hp, window, real, hands, bleed, vitalOnDone, painOnDone, timer, and side.\n\nmakeEntity now initializes nick, cutPunish, stray, heldFor, gelTime, and coverT. It normalizes wound and suture kinds to 'wound' and builds slots for them. Gel, mask, and sob entities become 'gel' or 'sob' and receive cells. Incision and scalpel entities default to an incision kind and generate a linePath if none is provided.\n\nA long list of entity kinds is defined, from pool and hook to organ and unclamp. Tool dispatch groups them by kind: drain targets include pool and droplets; forceps lift hook, prop, magnet, fragment, debris, core, stalk, extraction, and organ; scalpel cuts incision, pore, plate, and tumor; suture repairs wound and bleeder; laser treats polyp, egg, eye, and cyst; gel covers gel and sob.\n\nA `KIND_TOOL` map now assigns every entity kind to its corresponding tool. Scanner targets hidden entities, dressing targets dressing, clamp and unclamp are hold tools, and ring is the syringe.\n\nPlate is assigned to the scalpel tool but switches phases: cut, lift, or burn. A `sim.byId` map will be updated only when entities spawn rather than every frame. The `stageClear` helper checks stage completion against flags, strain status, or spawned entity counts.\n\nStrain stage clears when the strain flag is set, not when entities are spawned. The strain itself only sets that flag; the next stage spawns the eggs.\n\nMissed knock lacerations spawn into the current stage and must be sutured before the strain ends. The suture stage should clear when all wounds from that stage are done, including existing ones at stage start, not only newly spawned ones.\n\nThe `stageClear` helper now checks `!e.optional` when counting entities for a `kinds`-based stage. The introit is simplified so the strain itself spawns optional lacerations on each HP loss, and a later suture stage spawns its own scripted wounds.\n\nThe suture stage will wait for kinds wound and spawn nothing. If the strain leaves no wounds, the stage clears after half a second.\n\nThe suture stage will explicitly spawn two scripted wounds in its spawn data. This makes the stage deterministic and independent of fight outcomes. Missed-knock lacerations still add extra wounds, but all must be sutured before the stage clears.\n\nThe suture stage will spawn three wounds total: one per HP loss and one closing wound. The simulation runner will include a MAX_STEPS safety limit to prevent infinite loops. A bug where the bot could not complete an entity, such as the scalpel failing to reach its last point, will be fixed.\n\nDebug logging will be gated behind `process.env.SIM_DEBUG` so tests remain clean. The bot will also handle plate phases, with a short path and a diastole window.\n\nPlate completion will advance through phases rather than marking the entity done immediately. After the cut phase finishes, the plate switches to lift and resets its path; after lift, it switches to burn with zero heat. The scalpel, forceps, and laser will each special-case plate to handle these phase transitions.\n\nFor the eye, the laser burn time is 1.2 seconds instead of 0.7, and the eye takes one damage when the burn completes.\n\nDroplet entities will drift downward each tick during the strain phase. They spawn near the patient and move toward the nearest seat tip, disappearing once they pass a drain or fall below a depth threshold. This movement logic will live inside `stepEntities`.\n\nGel film entities will last ten seconds and kill droplets that touch them. The bot may place this film, though draining droplets without it is still possible.\n\nSob entities have a six-second timer. If coverage stays below eighty percent when the timer expires, vitals drop by twenty-five and the sob resets so the bot must repeat it; otherwise it is done. The strain stage counts completed sobs.\n\nThe sob line will be short—about six cells along a line—so painting at high speed finishes in under two seconds.\n\nA strain HP multiplier will also be set.\n\nIntroit health is three times the strain HP multiplier. A `heightAt` function will compute raised surface heights for the view layer and mouse picking. It will be tested as a pure unit test.\n\nThe `heightAt` function now models the heart dome with a Gaussian peak near 2.1 and a thump from the pulse, while other arenas use a gentle exponential curve. A surface-aiming helper will be added to `src/sim/aim.js` and tested against this height logic.\n\nThe surface-aiming helper will be exported from `src/sim/aim.js` so tests can import it without the engine. A Node-side fill-mode test using `frameView` may verify the formula against the plan table, though the browser launch test remains the primary verification. Writing is starting now with `package.json`, core modules, `src/sim/aim.js`, `codec.js`, `op.js`, `bot.js`, `strains.js`, and `src/content/ops.js`.\n\nThe file list now includes `src/content/story.js` and `src/audio/voice.js`. Tests will be written first for the tools only so bugs can be fixed iteratively. `op.js` is expected to reach roughly nine hundred lines, so it will be composed in one complete pass. A `score.js` module may be extracted from `op.js` to keep the operation code lean. The total estimated size is about three thousand lines across several files. Final score calculation correctly uses remaining time when a stage ends.\n\nA snapshot of the stage state will be saved on entry so the bot can retry a failed attempt. The `lose` function will set the phase to lost and record the reason. The time limit multiplier will scale the initial timeLeft rather than the clock drain rate.\n\nThe clock drains at one-quarter speed during quiet phases. Tables lose vitals from base drain, bleed count, and extra damage scaled by difficulty and coop, but only when not in quiet or god mode; a table crashes if its vitals reach zero.\n\nThe simulation always stores vitals in a `tables` array, even for a single table, and mirrors the active table’s value into `sim.vitals`. Scoring sums all tables’ vitals. A table that reaches zero vitals triggers a code blue pause; if another table also fails, the check repeats. The intern has two total saves across all tables, not per table. Op 3 starts with initial vitals of seventy, sixty, and fifty-five and uses a drain rate of zero point two five.\n\nGod mode will freeze vitals, mistakes, and the clock so attract mode can run without failure. A zero maxMistakes setting allows unlimited mistakes while still counting them. Mistakes deduct a fixed penalty from table vitals. The simulation logic is now complete and ready to be written.\n\nContent operations will be built with helper functions in ops.js for readability. Par values will start at one and be updated after calibration. A small Node calibration script will run before the final test to print scores.\n\nThe calibration script will print a JSON line and run under a `cal` filter, so it does not interfere with the default failure-only output. Entity snapshots will use structuredClone because they contain only plain data. History capture copies only data fields to avoid infinite loops. Look restore may correctly restore the code-blue phase from an old snapshot.\n\nSnapshot restoration will preserve entities, table vitals, mistakes, timeLeft, stage index, score, streak, doses, flags, phase, seats scores and holding, RNG state, unlocked content, speech, stage timer, beat, and BPM. It will skip the persist object, seat positions, composure, history, real time, and look usage so costs remain sticky. The static stages array and entity IDs ensure restored snapshots stay consistent.\n\nThe quiet flag will be forced to false on snapshot restore so the simulation cannot get stuck in a quiet state. Seat tool and holding state will be restored from snapshots, but seat positions will not. The capture and restore logic will live inside op.js. package.json uses module type, which should work with the engine's check.mjs. Game.js will replace the starter file.\n\nEntity IDs will use an incrementing `sim.nextId` counter. Content entities will be prefixed with the stage ID to keep them unique within each operation. When a pool completes, any entity hidden by that pool ID will be revealed. The scanner's interaction with hidden entities is still being weighed.\n\nThe scanner will reveal hidden entities within range, even those hidden under pools. Pools still need draining to fully expose them. The bot will skip grabbing a hook that is hidden under a pool and drain the pool first.\n\nThe bot will skip hidden but unmarked hooks, so it drains pools first in op 1. Communication lines will be stored as string keys in the simulation and resolved by the view layer; spaces indicate literal text. The test RNG will be seeded early to enable rapid iteration.\n\nThe first stitch will be clean because the suture interval defaults to 0.45 seconds when no prior tie exists. The bot switches sides every 0.45 simulated seconds, and its timing is driven by sim.t rather than real time.\n\nQuiet mode will be disabled during suture to prevent the bot cursor from moving too fast while its phase logic stays slow.\n\nQuiet mode only activates when vitals drop below thirty-five. During quiet, the cursor moves at full speed while the simulation suture checks still use sim positions updated at full speed. Because bot phase is driven by sim.t, which advances slowly during quiet, the bot stays on one side longer in real time and crossings become less frequent.\n\nThe suture interval between crossings stays within the 0.2–0.7 second window even after accounting for travel time. At skill 0.4 the bot moves at speed ~6.7, and the 0.13-second travel still fits comfortably inside that range.\n\nNoise amplitude is safely below the 0.4-second suture slot window at all skill levels. The scalpel mean-deviation metric is risky because it samples every frame while the bot travels between points; the average distance to the current target can hover near the CLEAN threshold.\n\nThe scalpel deviation metric will now measure distance to the path polyline instead of the current target point. This keeps mean deviation near zero while the bot travels between waypoints. The path index advances when the bot is close enough to the next point or has passed it by enough.\n\nScalpel and forceps will use the same path-deviation metric. The scalpel start gate requires the bot to reach within 0.5 units of the first waypoint before it begins tracking progress or deviation. Deviation is only penalized after the bot has started. The entity will store its total path length at creation.\n\nThe strains introit movement is feasible. The pore spawns at the bulge position, and the bot can cross the field in about one second to cut the short scalpel path in roughly 0.2 seconds. At skill 0.7 the core exposure window of 3.5 seconds and a 2.5-unit pull at speed 10 take only about 0.25 seconds plus approach time. At skill 0.4 the same pull takes roughly 0.4 seconds. Both speeds fit comfortably inside the available windows, so the bot may clear the introit as well. Dies irae systole punishment is next.\n\nUsing a tool during systole on a windowed entity will add a SLOPPY rating and a nick, not a full mistake. The bot will avoid doing this, so skill 1 remains CLEAN. The plate action takes roughly 21 seconds across seven plates plus waits, leaving plenty of time in the 420-second window.\n\nFibrillation will be triggered by a scripted code-blue stage after the plates, independent of BPM. The BPM mechanic still exists but cannot reach 120 early because the maximum is 114. Using the syringe to lower BPM is acceptable. The massage phase requires eight alternations.\n\nThe massage phase will use a deterministic rhythm game. Eight beats will occur at 60/bpm intervals. The bot presses the expected side when `sim.massageBeat` matches. A correct press within 0.15 seconds of the beat counts toward the eight alternations. Wrong presses are ignored and do not fail. The bot will alternate sides deterministically.\n\nThe final cut after massage will be implemented as a stage. The test file will be comprehensive and the story will use concise lines. The exit interview structure will live in about.js so the game and JSON share one source; a small script will generate exit-interview.json from about data at the end. A test will verify that about fields match TASK questions in order.\n\nAbout answers will be drafted honestly and revised after testing. Placeholder numbers are banned; real counts will be filled in at the end. The content test only checks that fields exist and are non-empty, not that they are perfect. The sourceInformation section will list the date, source title, and plan file.\n\nThe about.json draft now includes genre, perspective, and technical fields. It lists the Grok CLI headless Linux environment, model xAI/grok-4.7 with reasoning effort xhigh, and usage notes. The testingFeedback, engineFeedback, and promptFeedback arrays each hold four strings. The aiHub section names The Second Opinion Clinic as a Simulation game.\n\nThe `aiHub` section will also list the series, player count as `1–4`, controls, and tags. The en-dash in `1–4` is required by the plan and task example, though the font may not render it.\n\nThe HUD will render the player count as \"1-4\" to avoid a broken en-dash in the font. The JSON will still store `1–4` exactly.\n\nThe font lint only scans story and communication lines, so the en-dash in `aiHub.players` is safe. Tagline options include \"The truth, stat.\" and \"Keep the patient. Keep your nerve.\" The title screen will display both the title and tagline. The arena list covers forearm, stomach, ferry, bone, liver, kidney, lung, heart, chest, and spine; Op 3 uses the ferry arena with three tables.\n\nI will write the core files and simulation in this turn, then run a unit test. I decided against using a subagent to write the view to avoid integration issues. I also need to ensure `until: 'kinds'` correctly excludes nicks from wound counts.\n\nBleeder is a fast two-slot wound with bleed enabled. Bleeding stops once the wound is done and only pools while the wound remains active. Droplet does not block stages. Entities marked done are removed from bot goals. Fragment snapping now snaps to target angle within fifteen degrees and rates CLEAN or GOOD based on a five-degree threshold.\n\nFragment release now sets the angle before clearing the grab flag, so the bot rates CLEAN if the fragment lands within five degrees. Skill noise is added to the target angle while holding; at skill 0.7 the deviation stays within five degrees, and at skill 1.0 the angle is exact. Forceps hold requires holding for 0.35 seconds within 0.45 units. Unclamp triggers a clot if the bot fails to rewind first.\n\nUnclamp #2 fails if the prior unclamp is incomplete; the goal picker enforces this order. The kidney organ triggers a pink flag once all unclamps are done, which the view reads.\n\nThe next stage after unclamps will set the pink flag on entry. A 120-second cold timer starts when the bot places the kidney and counts down each step; failure to complete sutures in time triggers a loss. The donor organ is fetched by pulling it from the tray to the bed with forceps.\n\nThe HTML build script will include hub meta tags for session, players, mode, duration, WebGPU requirement, and goal. A docs/AUTHORING.md file is planned for later.\n\nThe hub meta tags now include a game type, source title, and control instructions. The goal text is 46 characters long. The howto line is 64 characters, well under the 80-character limit.\n\nThe session duration is set to four minutes. A simple test runner will be added and extended later. Calibration tests will verify that each operation's stored par matches the bot's final result, with pars updated after the first run. Tests for skill levels 0.4 and 0.7 are planned, and execution speed will be monitored.\n\nI will avoid allocating arrays inside the inner loop to keep frame costs low. Creating small input objects each frame is acceptable, and pathMetrics requires no allocations. A new audio-boot.js module will handle music volume defaults, with a unit test mocking localStorage and the real test running in a fresh browser profile.\n\nThe boot function only sets music volume to 0.1 when the storage key is absent, so it will not overwrite an existing engine default.\n\nThe engine may write the default audio volume before the boot script runs. If it does, the key already exists and the setup check will skip overriding to 0.1. The boot script must therefore run before any engine initialization that persists defaults.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-53946aaf-b31f-413e-ab3c-81fc938a4cd9-49","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"node -e '\nconst fs = require(\"fs\");\nconst p = \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/pixel-engine.js\";\nconst s = fs.readFileSync(p, \"utf8\");\nconst i = s.indexOf(\"pixel-engine:audio\");\nconsole.log(s.slice(i, i+1800));\n'","description":"Read engine audio persistence logic"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7886,"output_tokens":23491,"cache_read_input_tokens":128256,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"3df6aeba-0554-43b5-9566-b2586d72022a"}
