,82,69,69,44,32,116,111,111,110,77,97,116,101,114,105,97,108,44,32,119,105,116,104,85,114,108,79,112,116,105,111,110,115,32,125,32,102,114,111,109,32,39,46,47,112,105,120,101,108,45,101,110,103,105,110,101,46,106,115,39,59,10,53,54,48,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,99,111,110,115,116,32,98,111,100,121,32,61,32,110,101,119,32,77,101,115,104,40,110,101,119,32,66,111,120,71,101,111,109,101,116,114,121,40,119,44,32,104,44,32,52,41,44,32,116,111,111,110,77,97,116,101,114,105,97,108,40,112,97,108,101,116,116,101,46,115,97,110,100,41,41,59,10,53,54,50,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,99,111,110,115,116,32,103,114,97,115,115,32,61,32,110,101,119,32,77,101,115,104,40,110,101,119,32,66,111,120,71,101,111,109,101,116,114,121,40,119,32,43,32,48,46,49,44,32,48,46,51,44,32,52,46,49,41,44,32,116,111,111,110,77,97,116,101,114,105,97,108,40,112,97,108,101,116,116,101,46,103,114,101,101,110,41,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":15,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/CHANGELOG.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":494,\"content\":\"  with `toonMaterial(0xffffff, { map: textureMap(tex) })`. `ModelOptions.textures` (a\"},{\"line_number\":523,\"content\":\"  - `textureMap` (a cached, shared, nearest-filtered `DataTexture` for `toonMaterial(0xffffff,\"},{\"line_number\":582,\"content\":\"- `glowMaterial(color)`: the unlit counterpart of `toonMaterial`, shared and cached per colour,\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/ASSETS.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":162,\"content\":\"- **Textures:** `toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('bricks') })` on any mesh (the\"},{\"line_number\":205,\"content\":\"import { addModelColliders, buildModel, DEFAULT_TEXEL, Engine, hudSprite, PALETTE, TEXTURE_LIBRARY, textureMap, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\"},{\"line_number\":240,\"content\":\"    const floor = new Mesh(ground, toonMaterial(PALETTE.white, { map: textureMap('cobbles') }));\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/GUIDE.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":91,\"content\":\"import { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\"},{\"line_number\":98,\"content\":\"  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\"},{\"line_number\":229,\"content\":\"- **Materials:** `toonMaterial(color)` is a cached 3-band toon material. Use palette\"},{\"line_number\":232,\"content\":\"- **Meshes:** plain three.js (`new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(c))`).\"},{\"line_number\":253,\"content\":\"collider data and light, and `textureMap('cobbles')` a map for `toonMaterial`. `ASSETS.md` is their\"},{\"line_number\":327,\"content\":\"ctx.scene.add(new SoftMesh(flag, toonMaterial(PALETTE.red)));  // flag.step(dt) per fixed step, mesh.sync() per frame\"},{\"line_number\":524,\"content\":\"import { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PALETTE, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\"},{\"line_number\":560,\"content\":\"      const body = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, 4), toonMaterial(palette.sand));\"},{\"line_number\":562,\"content\":\"      const grass = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w + 0.1, 0.3, 4.1), toonMaterial(palette.green));\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"914f79cb-ca69-48d8-99b4-a152f22f7972"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_157","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll use THREE and toonMaterial from pixel-engine.js.\n\nHeadless Node.js may lack a document object, so canvas-based textures could fail. I need to verify whether Engine.headless provides a fake document or if setup must avoid canvas entirely.\n\nCanvas texture creation will be guarded by a `typeof document !== 'undefined'` check so headless Node.js does not crash. The tissue plane will be rotated -90 degrees on the X axis to lie flat on the ground, matching the 16 by 9 field dimensions.\n\nEntities will be pooled as colored boxes. Each seat gets a glove mesh. Story busts are primitive spheres and boxes colored per character; HERO_MODEL is hidden. The scene needs ambient, directional, and point lights to match the summary's lighting values. The camera uses a fixed preset at position [0, 9.6, 3.1] aimed at [0, 0, 0.1] with FOV 44.\n\nThe camera will stay fixed each frame by updating its position and lookAt target. Mouse aiming will use rayAt to cast a ray from the pointer into aimOnSurface. Tool cycling will respond to wheel events, Tab will be blocked, and context menus will be suppressed. During each fixed update, input will branch: mouse over the arena aims via raycast; otherwise stick or WASD keys drive movement.\n\nGamepad controls map to specific buttons and axes. Tool selection uses the left trigger, mouse left click, or the Cross button. Gifts fire with Space or the right trigger. Steady mode uses Shift or the left trigger. Dropping items uses K or Circle. Pause uses Escape or Start. Table toggling uses Tab, E, or the D-pad.\n\nA gamepadButtons object prevents duplicate key events from firing. Each connected pad maps to its seat index, while keyboard and mouse input goes to seat zero.\n\nIn single-player consult mode, seat zero is human and the other seats are bots. The attract demo runs with god mode enabled and bot skill set to one, skipping save writes. Settings persist in localStorage.\n\nIn setup, if neither the pixel-engine audio key nor soc:settings exists, music volume is set to 0.1. The engine only persists that value after an explicit setVolume call.\n\nDev sliders will be built from the DEFAULTS object keys. Story flow runs through title, menu, scene one, operations, results, scene two, credits, and back to title; progress unlocks the log. Campaign tests clear the save before playback.\n\nThe campaign test runs every scene and operation with a fixed seed and skill one, recording each result in the save. It then marks the log, credits, and quiet flags as complete before returning the save. The content test asserts that all scene IDs, story operations, and both the log and credits are present.\n\nRunning all ten operations at skill one takes roughly fifteen seconds, so the default suite will last about one to two minutes. Font linting checks beat line lengths and character sets. The About fields must answer every TASK question and appear in both about.js and exit-interview.json; the content test imports about.js to verify its keys. Feedback counts will be filled after the browser tests run, but the content test can confirm they are non-empty and marked as added on import.\n\nTesting found real issues: two syringe assertion bugs, an op5 suture stall, a holding ID bug, magnet snap, missing firstClot, and copy-hand timing. I will tally these after the suite finishes.\n\nI need to read check.mjs to confirm createGame expectations before rewriting game.js. The current coin garden pattern will be replaced with the new scene.\n\nFiles will be written in batches. game.js will import app.js, which imports THREE from pixel-engine for mesh creation.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-332f29fd-52fb-4199-bbcb-d82caeff08ed-130","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/game.js","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-332f29fd-52fb-4199-bbcb-d82caeff08ed-131","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/check.mjs","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":923,"output_tokens":1832,"cache_read_input_tokens":166144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"90266767-d913-4d82-a87e-e44c8cdf1038"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-332f29fd-52fb-4199-bbcb-d82caeff08ed-130","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\\n\\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\\n\\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\\n10→  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\\n  return mesh;\\n}\\n\\nclass CoinGarden {\\n  name = 'Coin Garden';\\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\\n  coins = [];\\n  collected = 0;\\n20→  won = false;\\n  target = new Vector3();\\n  ground = new Vector3();\\n\\n  async setup(ctx) {\\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\\n    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\\n    scene.add(\\n      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\\n        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\\n30→        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\\n        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\\n        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\\n      ]),\\n    );\\n    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\\n    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\\n      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\\n      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n40→      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\",\"content_concise\":\"1→// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\\n\\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\\n\\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\\n10→  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\\n  return mesh;\\n}\\n\\nclass CoinGarden {\\n  name = 'Coin Garden';\\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\\n  coins = [];\\n  collected = 0;\\n20→  won = false;\\n  target = new Vector3();\\n  ground = new Vector3();\\n\\n  async setup(ctx) {\\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\\n    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\\n    scene.add(\\n      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\\n        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\\n30→        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\\n        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\\n        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\\n      ]),\\n    );\\n    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\\n    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\\n      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\\n      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n40→      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/game.js\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\\n\\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\\n\\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\\n  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\\n  return mesh;\\n}\\n\\nclass CoinGarden {\\n  name = 'Coin Garden';\\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\\n  coins = [];\\n  collected = 0;\\n  won = false;\\n  target = new Vector3();\\n  ground = new Vector3();\\n\\n  async setup(ctx) {\\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\\n    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\\n    scene.add(\\n      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\\n        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\\n        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\\n        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\\n        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\\n      ]),\\n    );\\n    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\\n    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\\n      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\\n      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\\n      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\\n\",\"total_lines\":103}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-332f29fd-52fb-4199-bbcb-d82caeff08ed-131","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env node\\n// check.mjs: play a Pixel Engine game in a real browser and report what happened.\\n// Ships in the engine kit; run it before you hand a game back.\\n//\\n//   node check.mjs                       index.html in the current folder\\n//   node check.mjs examples/looks.html   any page (served from the folder that holds pixel-engine.js)\\n//   node check.mjs \\\"index.html?look=noir&camera=side\\\"   with URL flags\\n//   node check.mjs http://localhost:5173/\\n//   node check.mjs --sheets models.js    sheets of your own models and textures (no page played)\\n10→//   node check.mjs --headless game.js    play the game in Node, no browser: steps, errors, a replay\\n//\\n// Options:\\n//   --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyW*30   hold each key for that many frames, in order (default: 90 idle frames)\\n//   --cameras                        also shoot the iso, topdown, side and third camera presets\\n//   --looks pixel_heroes,noir        also shoot these looks (names from engine.lookPresets)\\n//   --out check                      folder for the PNGs and report.json (default: ./check)\\n//   --size 960x540                   viewport in CSS pixels\\n//   --sheets models.js,crate,cobbles draw sheets instead of playing a page (ASSETS.md): every\\n//                                    ModelDef and TextureDef a module exports (by name, in an\\n20→//                                    array or object, in a ModelCatalog / TextureCatalog, or added\\n//                                    to the library), and library ids. A PNG each and report.json\\n//                                    in --out (default ./check/sheets); exit 1 on any problem\\n//   --headless [game.js]             play a game module in Node with Engine.headless instead of a\\n//                                    page (no browser, no pictures): it imports the module, makes\\n//                                    the game its `createGame()` export returns, plays the --keys\\n//                                    frames, then replays that run's recording in a fresh game. It\\n//                                    writes report.json (state, errors, fingerprint) to --out; exit\\n//                                    1 on an error or a replay that ends somewhere else\\n//   --help                           this text\\n30→//\\n// It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and, except for --headless, Playwright (`npm i -D playwright` then\\n// `npx playwright install chromium`; a global install, `npm i -g playwright`, works too), or\\n// `playwright-core` with CHROMIUM_PATH set to a Chromium (its headless shell,\\n// `chrome-linux/headless_shell`, is the lightest): it plays WebGPU on SwiftShader with no\\n// display. Exit code 0 means the game started, ran the frames with no errors (page, console,\\n// engine or GPU) and drew a picture (with --sheets: every sheet was drawn and no\\n// def has a problem; warnings are fine); 1 means it didn't; 2 means the tool couldn't run (bad\\n// arguments, no page, no browser, or a browser without WebGPU, which no game can start in).\\nimport { execSync } from 'node:child_process';\\n40→import { createServer } from 'node:http';\\nimport { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, isAbsolute, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\nimport * as zlib from 'node:zlib';\\n\\nconst usage = () => readFileSync(new URL(import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\\\\nimport ')[0].replace(/^#!.*\\\\n/, '').replace(/^\\\\/\\\\/ ?/gm, '');\\nconst fail = (message) => {\\n  console.error(`check.mjs: ${message}\\\\n\\\\n${usage()}`);\\n50→  process.exit(2);\\n};\\nif (typeof zlib.crc32 !== 'function') fail(`needs Node 20.15+ or 22.2+ (this is ${process.version})`);\\n\\n// --name value flags, --name flags, then one positional page\\nconst VALUE = new Set(['keys', 'looks', 'out', 'size', 'sheets']);\\nconst FLAG = new Set(['cameras', 'help', 'headless']);\\nconst opts = {};\\nconst positional = [];\\nfor (let i = 2; i < process.argv.length; i++) {\\n60→  const a = process.argv[i];\\n  const name = a.slice(2);\\n  if (!a.startsWith('--')) positional.push(a);\\n  else if (VALUE.has(name)) {\\n    const v = process.argv[++i];\\n    if (v === undefined || v.startsWith('--')) fail(`${a} needs a value`);\\n    opts[name] = v;\\n  } else if (FLAG.has(name)) opts[name] = true;\\n  else fail(`unknown option ${a}`);\\n}\\n70→if (opts.help) {\\n  console.log(usage());\\n  process.exit(0);\\n}\\nif (positional.length > 1) fail(`one page at a time (got ${positional.join(' ')})`);\\n// --sheets \\\"\\\" is still --sheets (drawSheets says it needs something to draw), never a page run\\nconst sheets = opts.sheets !== undefined;\\nif (sheets && positional.length) fail(`--sheets draws sheets instead of playing a page: play ${positional[0]} in a run of its own`);\\nconst headless = opts.headless === true;\\nif (headless && (sheets || opts.cameras || opts.looks)) fail('--headless plays the game in Node, with no pictures: --sheets, --cameras and --looks need the browser');\\n80→const target = positional[0] ?? (headless ? 'game.js' : 'index.html');\",\"content_concise\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env node\\n// check.mjs: play a Pixel Engine game in a real browser and report what happened.\\n// Ships in the engine kit; run it before you hand a game back.\\n//\\n//   node check.mjs                       index.html in the current folder\\n//   node check.mjs examples/looks.html   any page (served from the folder that holds pixel-engine.js)\\n//   node check.mjs \\\"index.html?look=noir&camera=side\\\"   with URL flags\\n//   node check.mjs http://localhost:5173/\\n//   node check.mjs --sheets models.js    sheets of your own models and textures (no page played)\\n10→//   node check.mjs --headless game.js    play the game in Node, no browser: steps, errors, a replay\\n//\\n// Options:\\n//   --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyW*30   hold each key for that many frames, in order (default: 90 idle frames)\\n//   --cameras                        also shoot the iso, topdown, side and third camera presets\\n//   --looks pixel_heroes,noir        also shoot these looks (names from engine.lookPresets)\\n//   --out check                      folder for the PNGs and report.json (default: ./check)\\n//   --size 960x540                   viewport in CSS pixels\\n//   --sheets models.js,crate,cobbles draw sheets instead of playing a page (ASSETS.md): every\\n//                                    ModelDef and TextureDef a module exports (by name, in an\\n20→//                                    array or object, in a ModelCatalog / TextureCatalog, or added\\n//                                    to the library), and library ids. A PNG each and report.json\\n//                                    in --out (default ./check/sheets); exit 1 on any problem\\n//   --headless [game.js]             play a game module in Node with Engine.headless instead of a\\n//                                    page (no browser, no pictures): it imports the module, makes\\n//                                    the game its `createGame()` export returns, plays the --keys\\n//                                    frames, then replays that run's recording in a fresh game. It\\n//                                    writes report.json (state, errors, fingerprint) to --out; exit\\n//                                    1 on an error or a replay that ends somewhere else\\n//   --help                           this text\\n30→//\\n// It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and, except for --headless, Playwright (`npm i -D playwright` then\\n// `npx playwright install chromium`; a global install, `npm i -g playwright`, works too), or\\n// `playwright-core` with CHROMIUM_PATH set to a Chromium (its headless shell,\\n// `chrome-linux/headless_shell`, is the lightest): it plays WebGPU on SwiftShader with no\\n// display. Exit code 0 means the game started, ran the frames with no errors (page, console,\\n// engine or GPU) and drew a picture (with --sheets: every sheet was drawn and no\\n// def has a problem; warnings are fine); 1 means it didn't; 2 means the tool couldn't run (bad\\n// arguments, no page, no browser, or a browser without WebGPU, which no game can start in).\\nimport { execSync } from 'node:child_process';\\n40→import { createServer } from 'node:http';\\nimport { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, isAbsolute, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\nimport * as zlib from 'node:zlib';\\n\\nconst usage = () => readFileSync(new URL(import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\\\\nimport ')[0].replace(/^#!.*\\\\n/, '').replace(/^\\\\/\\\\/ ?/gm, '');\\nconst fail = (message) => {\\n  console.error(`check.mjs: ${message}\\\\n\\\\n${usage()}`);\\n50→  process.exit(2);\\n};\\nif (typeof zlib.crc32 !== 'function') fail(`needs Node 20.15+ or 22.2+ (this is ${process.version})`);\\n\\n// --name value flags, --name flags, then one positional page\\nconst VALUE = new Set(['keys', 'looks', 'out', 'size', 'sheets']);\\nconst FLAG = new Set(['cameras', 'help', 'headless']);\\nconst opts = {};\\nconst positional = [];\\nfor (let i = 2; i < process.argv.length; i++) {\\n60→  const a = process.argv[i];\\n  const name = a.slice(2);\\n  if (!a.startsWith('--')) positional.push(a);\\n  else if (VALUE.has(name)) {\\n    const v = process.argv[++i];\\n    if (v === undefined || v.startsWith('--')) fail(`${a} needs a value`);\\n    opts[name] = v;\\n  } else if (FLAG.has(name)) opts[name] = true;\\n  else fail(`unknown option ${a}`);\\n}\\n70→if (opts.help) {\\n  console.log(usage());\\n  process.exit(0);\\n}\\nif (positional.length > 1) fail(`one page at a time (got ${positional.join(' ')})`);\\n// --sheets \\\"\\\" is still --sheets (drawSheets says it needs something to draw), never a page run\\nconst sheets = opts.sheets !== undefined;\\nif (sheets && positional.length) fail(`--sheets draws sheets instead of playing a page: play ${positional[0]} in a run of its own`);\\nconst headless = opts.headless === true;\\nif (headless && (sheets || opts.cameras || opts.looks)) fail('--headless plays the game in Node, with no pictures: --sheets, --cameras and --looks need the browser');\\n80→const target = positional[0] ?? (headless ? 'game.js' : 'index.html');\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/check.mjs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"#!/usr/bin/env node\\n// check.mjs: play a Pixel Engine game in a real browser and report what happened.\\n// Ships in the engine kit; run it before you hand a game back.\\n//\\n//   node check.mjs                       index.html in the current folder\\n//   node check.mjs examples/looks.html   any page (served from the folder that holds pixel-engine.js)\\n//   node check.mjs \\\"index.html?look=noir&camera=side\\\"   with URL flags\\n//   node check.mjs http://localhost:5173/\\n//   node check.mjs --sheets models.js    sheets of your own models and textures (no page played)\\n//   node check.mjs --headless game.js    play the game in Node, no browser: steps, errors, a replay\\n//\\n// Options:\\n//   --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyW*30   hold each key for that many frames, in order (default: 90 idle frames)\\n//   --cameras                        also shoot the iso, topdown, side and third camera presets\\n//   --looks pixel_heroes,noir        also shoot these looks (names from engine.lookPresets)\\n//   --out check                      folder for the PNGs and report.json (default: ./check)\\n//   --size 960x540                   viewport in CSS pixels\\n//   --sheets models.js,crate,cobbles draw sheets instead of playing a page (ASSETS.md): every\\n//                                    ModelDef and TextureDef a module exports (by name, in an\\n//                                    array or object, in a ModelCatalog / TextureCatalog, or added\\n//                                    to the library), and library ids. A PNG each and report.json\\n//                                    in --out (default ./check/sheets); exit 1 on any problem\\n//   --headless [game.js]             play a game module in Node with Engine.headless instead of a\\n//                                    page (no browser, no pictures): it imports the module, makes\\n//                                    the game its `createGame()` export returns, plays the --keys\\n//                                    frames, then replays that run's recording in a fresh game. It\\n//                                    writes report.json (state, errors, fingerprint) to --out; exit\\n//                                    1 on an error or a replay that ends somewhere else\\n//   --help                           this text\\n//\\n// It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and, except for --headless, Playwright (`npm i -D playwright` then\\n// `npx playwright install chromium`; a global install, `npm i -g playwright`, works too), or\\n// `playwright-core` with CHROMIUM_PATH set to a Chromium (its headless shell,\\n// `chrome-linux/headless_shell`, is the lightest): it plays WebGPU on SwiftShader with no\\n// display. Exit code 0 means the game started, ran the frames with no errors (page, console,\\n// engine or GPU) and drew a picture (with --sheets: every sheet was drawn and no\\n// def has a problem; warnings are fine); 1 means it didn't; 2 means the tool couldn't run (bad\\n// arguments, no page, no browser, or a browser without WebGPU, which no game can start in).\\nimport { execSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { createServer } from 'node:http';\\nimport { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module';\\nimport { basename, dirname, extname, isAbsolute, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\\nimport * as zlib from 'node:zlib';\\n\\nconst usage = () => readFileSync(new URL(import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\\\\nimport ')[0].replace(/^#!.*\\\\n/, '').replace(/^\\\\/\\\\/ ?/gm, '');\\nconst fail = (message) => {\\n  console.error(`check.mjs: ${message}\\\\n\\\\n${usage()}`);\\n  process.exit(2);\\n};\\nif (typeof zlib.crc32 !== 'function') fail(`needs Node 20.15+ or 22.2+ (this is ${process.version})`);\\n\\n// --name value flags, --name flags, then one positional page\\nconst VALUE = new Set(['keys', 'looks', 'out', 'size', 'sheets']);\\nconst FLAG = new Set(['cameras', 'help', 'headless']);\\nconst opts = {};\\nconst positional = [];\\nfor (let i = 2; i < process.argv.length; i++) {\\n  const a = process.argv[i];\\n  const name = a.slice(2);\\n  if (!a.startsWith('--')) positional.push(a);\\n  else if (VALUE.has(name)) {\\n    const v = process.argv[++i];\\n    if (v === undefined || v.startsWith('--')) fail(`${a} needs a value`);\\n    opts[name] = v;\\n  } else if (FLAG.has(name)) opts[name] = true;\\n  else fail(`unknown option ${a}`);\\n}\\nif (opts.help) {\\n  console.log(usage());\\n  process.exit(0);\\n}\\nif (positional.length > 1) fail(`one page at a time (got ${positional.join(' ')})`);\\n// --sheets \\\"\\\" is still --sheets (drawSheets says it needs something to draw), never a page run\\nconst sheets = opts.sheets !== undefined;\\nif (sheets && positional.length) fail(`--sheets draws sheets instead of playing a page: play ${positional[0]} in a run of its own`);\\nconst headless = opts.headless === true;\\nif (headless && (sheets || opts.cameras || opts.looks)) fail('--headless plays the game in Node, with no pictures: --sheets, --cameras and --looks need the browser');\\nconst target = positional[0] ?? (headless ? 'game.js' : 'index.html');\\n\",\"total_lines\":586}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"22383742-1b5d-489f-8eeb-e71a992032a0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_158","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"createGame is required. Headless mode will handle the plays.\n\nTitle screen key presses will be ignored. Engine.headless handles missing WebGPU in Node. I'll write the content files carefully. The settings key is soc:settings. In game.js, AIHub connects before Engine.start when info.server is present.\n\nThe boot function returns early in Node. It shows a touch gate on touch-only devices. AIHub connects with shared-world mode and seats 1 and 4. It fails if WebGPU is unavailable. When info.server is true it marks AIHub ready and exits. Otherwise it creates the game and assigns sessionInfo.\n\nEngine.start runs with touch disabled and settings for buttons and keys. AIHub.ready() is called after startup. The host loop will use setInterval inside Clinic.setup rather than fixedUpdate.\n\nClients send input at 15 Hz even when idle and render from snapshots. The host simulates using AIHub.time() as its step clock. Session mode begins in card state until AIHub.time() reaches zero. Cards are drawn there with Enter and Cross to answer ready questions and Triangle to answer more. A start event triggers a countdown. The host accumulates AIHub.time() into simulation time and steps the world with collected inputs.\n\nClients send input messages at 15 Hz even when idle. The host publishes snapshots at 12 Hz, keyframes at 1 Hz, and events at most 4 Hz. Hosts store inputs by seat; clients apply snapshots. Player IDs come from info.players, with seats assigned by sorting those IDs. The deal seed is generated with AIHub.rng('deal') to match createOp's integer requirement. The active operation index cycles through SESSION_OPS modulo info.round. The host calls finish() when an operation ends or the time runs out.\n\nHost-leave events will not trigger finish. The end state stops music and loops and halts all sends. Clients will send a small hello during the card phase to keep traffic flowing, then switch to 15 Hz idle input once play begins.\n\nInput messages will begin immediately when ready() is called, including during the card and countdown phases. The host will send a small state update at 12 Hz during the card phase so both directions have traffic. Each peer maintains its own token bucket, so clients send at 15 Hz while the host sends at most 17 messages per second.\n\nClients do not send snapshots. The host applies inputs and steps the simulation. Clients render decoded state and move their own cursor locally for responsiveness. The view overlays seat positions from snapshots with a local override for the current seat. The host bot must play the operation to win before time-up so the harness sees a completed game.\n\nSession operations last about 200 seconds, well under the four-minute hub limit. The host bot can win an operation in roughly 20 seconds and finish with standings. Each client sends its own input, and the host bot must not override human input. The harness likely simulates players or simply verifies the game runs to completion.\n\nThe auto=1 harness auto-readies players and expects the round to finish. If all clients stay idle, the operation may lose on time or vitals before the four-minute limit; the host must still finish with standings that include every present player. The host must also finish within a grace period when the time-up event fires.\n\nThe server copy must still run the simulation even though it never calls Engine.start. When the host leaves, the remaining clients retire that seat and the hub voids the match rather than calling finish.\n\nThe server can be the host. It runs the simulation headlessly via setInterval without starting the engine, while clients handle rendering. Standings group players by score descending; if all scores are equal, every player is placed in a single group.\n\nThe standings function groups player IDs by score and sorts descending. It returns an array of score groups. Every present player appears exactly once. Left players are omitted. The finish payload carries scores, a won flag, and per-player stats with names and text limited to sixteen characters.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5880494c-7150-4c23-8bdc-47b849722824-132","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/vendor/README.md","limit":100}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3844,"output_tokens":2168,"cache_read_input_tokens":167040,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"d7d05dcc-c22a-4463-a9d7-f467b10b8d34"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-5880494c-7150-4c23-8bdc-47b849722824-132","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n10→\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n20→> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n30→|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n40→- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n50→\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n60→   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n70→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n80→\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n10→\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n20→> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n30→|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n40→- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n50→\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n60→   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n70→<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n80→\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n90→[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n100→  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/vendor/README.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":100,\"raw_output\":\"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n\",\"total_lines\":410}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"4df39d0a-9ee8-443a-9aa9-bf18cebda6c3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_159","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have enough information. I will write the code now.\n\nI'm structuring the story into compact scenes. Each character gets a distinct voice pitch. I'm also drafting a pure JavaScript synthesizer to generate syllables.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9b13c1ee-474e-4479-bd3f-d2d773e72a1a-133","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/audio/AudioManager.d.ts","offset":1,"limit":140}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3333,"output_tokens":399,"cache_read_input_tokens":170880,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"47c50cf6-b07b-4da8-9ae1-52940ce794c7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-9b13c1ee-474e-4479-bd3f-d2d773e72a1a-133","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import { type Song } from './music';\\nimport { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\nexport type VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music';\\nexport interface AudioSettings {\\n    master: number;\\n    sfx: number;\\n    music: number;\\n    muted: boolean;\\n}\\n10→export interface PlayOptions {\\n    /** 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume. */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo. */\\n    pitch?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface VoiceSettings {\\n    /** 0..1 (default 1). */\\n20→    volume?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n    /** 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down. */\\n    muffle?: number;\\n    /** Pitch shift in semitones. */\\n    pitch?: number;\\n}\\n/** A looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall. */\\nexport interface Voice {\\n30→    readonly name: string;\\n    /** The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them). */\\n    readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>;\\n    /** Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started. */\\n    readonly sounding: boolean;\\n    readonly stopped: boolean;\\n    /** Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks). */\\n    set(s: VoiceSettings): void;\\n    stop(): void;\\n}\\n40→/** Low-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud. */\\nexport declare const muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number;\\n/**\\n * Web Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\\n * sequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\\n * persisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\\n *\\n * Browsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\\n * first key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\\n * (headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\\n50→ * was played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\\n */\\nexport declare class AudioManager {\\n    /** Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded. */\\n    readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>;\\n    /** The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests. */\\n    readonly log: {\\n        name: string;\\n        at: number;\\n    }[];\\n60→    /** How many times each sound was requested. */\\n    readonly counts: Record<string, number>;\\n    context: AudioContext | null;\\n    private master;\\n    private buses;\\n    private readonly settings;\\n    private readonly buffers;\\n    private readonly files;\\n    private readonly pendingFiles;\\n    private unlockListeners;\\n70→    private readonly lifetime;\\n    private song;\\n    private songName;\\n    private readonly musicSources;\\n    private readonly voices;\\n    /** Files being fetched or decoded (a loop waiting for one is not missing its sound). */\\n    private readonly loading;\\n    private disposed;\\n    constructor(target?: EventTarget | null);\\n    /** Web Audio exists in this environment. */\\n80→    static get supported(): boolean;\\n    /** True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input). */\\n    get unlocked(): boolean;\\n    /** Current volumes and mute (persisted). */\\n    get volume(): Readonly<AudioSettings>;\\n    get muted(): boolean;\\n    /** Name of the playing song ('' when none). */\\n    get playing(): string;\\n    /**\\n     * Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call\\n90→     * it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\\n     */\\n    unlock(): void;\\n    private target;\\n    /** Listen for the next user input (until the context is running). */\\n    private armUnlock;\\n    private disarmUnlock;\\n    /** Resume a suspended / interrupted context; stop listening for input once it runs. */\\n    private resume;\\n    /** Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef). */\\n100→    register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a\\n     * SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\\n     */\\n    play(sound: string | SoundDef, options?: PlayOptions): boolean;\\n    /**\\n     * Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch:\\n     * with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with\\n     * no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.\\n110→     * Stop it when you are done (a level's `dispose`).\\n     */\\n    loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings?: VoiceSettings): Voice;\\n    /** Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests). */\\n    get loops(): readonly Voice[];\\n    /** Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads). */\\n    stopLoops(): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works\\n     * before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\\n120→     */\\n    load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>;\\n    /** Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music. */\\n    playMusic(song: Song | null, name?: string): void;\\n    stopMusic(): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the\\n     * recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\\n     */\\n    persist: boolean;\\n130→    setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void;\\n    setMuted(muted: boolean): void;\\n    toggleMute(): boolean;\\n    /** The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset). */\\n    resetVolumes(): void;\\n    /** Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song. */\\n    update(): void;\\n    /** Stop everything and close the AudioContext. */\\n    dispose(): void;\\n    private start;\\n140→    private bufferFor;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import { type Song } from './music';\\nimport { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\nexport type VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music';\\nexport interface AudioSettings {\\n    master: number;\\n    sfx: number;\\n    music: number;\\n    muted: boolean;\\n}\\n10→export interface PlayOptions {\\n    /** 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume. */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo. */\\n    pitch?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface VoiceSettings {\\n    /** 0..1 (default 1). */\\n20→    volume?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n    /** 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down. */\\n    muffle?: number;\\n    /** Pitch shift in semitones. */\\n    pitch?: number;\\n}\\n/** A looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall. */\\nexport interface Voice {\\n30→    readonly name: string;\\n    /** The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them). */\\n    readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>;\\n    /** Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started. */\\n    readonly sounding: boolean;\\n    readonly stopped: boolean;\\n    /** Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks). */\\n    set(s: VoiceSettings): void;\\n    stop(): void;\\n}\\n40→/** Low-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud. */\\nexport declare const muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number;\\n/**\\n * Web Audio for games: procedural retro sound effects (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\\n * sequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\\n * persisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\\n *\\n * Browsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\\n * first key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\\n * (headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\\n50→ * was played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\\n */\\nexport declare class AudioManager {\\n    /** Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded. */\\n    readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>;\\n    /** The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests. */\\n    readonly log: {\\n        name: string;\\n        at: number;\\n    }[];\\n60→    /** How many times each sound was requested. */\\n    readonly counts: Record<string, number>;\\n    context: AudioContext | null;\\n    private master;\\n    private buses;\\n    private readonly settings;\\n    private readonly buffers;\\n    private readonly files;\\n    private readonly pendingFiles;\\n    private unlockListeners;\\n70→    private readonly lifetime;\\n    private song;\\n    private songName;\\n    private readonly musicSources;\\n    private readonly voices;\\n    /** Files being fetched or decoded (a loop waiting for one is not missing its sound). */\\n    private readonly loading;\\n    private disposed;\\n    constructor(target?: EventTarget | null);\\n    /** Web Audio exists in this environment. */\\n80→    static get supported(): boolean;\\n    /** True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input). */\\n    get unlocked(): boolean;\\n    /** Current volumes and mute (persisted). */\\n    get volume(): Readonly<AudioSettings>;\\n    get muted(): boolean;\\n    /** Name of the playing song ('' when none). */\\n    get playing(): string;\\n    /**\\n     * Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call\\n90→     * it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\\n     */\\n    unlock(): void;\\n    private target;\\n    /** Listen for the next user input (until the context is running). */\\n    private armUnlock;\\n    private disarmUnlock;\\n    /** Resume a suspended / interrupted context; stop listening for input once it runs. */\\n    private resume;\\n    /** Add or replace a sound effect (data, see SoundDef). */\\n100→    register(name: string, def: SoundDef): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Play a sound effect by name (built-in SFX, `register`ed or `load`ed) or directly from a\\n     * SoundDef. Returns false when nothing could be heard (locked, muted, no Web Audio).\\n     */\\n    play(sound: string | SoundDef, options?: PlayOptions): boolean;\\n    /**\\n     * Start a sound looping, as a voice you steer while it plays (volume, pan, muffle, pitch:\\n     * with `spatialize` it sits at a place in the world). Before the first user input, or with\\n     * no Web Audio, the voice keeps its settings silently and starts once audio unlocks.\\n110→     * Stop it when you are done (a level's `dispose`).\\n     */\\n    loop(sound: string | SoundDef, settings?: VoiceSettings): Voice;\\n    /** Voices playing or waiting to (tooling and tests). */\\n    get loops(): readonly Voice[];\\n    /** Stop every looping voice (the engine does this when a level unloads). */\\n    stopLoops(): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Load an audio file (wav/ogg/mp3…) under `name`; `play(name)` then plays it. Works\\n     * before the first user input too: the file is decoded once audio unlocks.\\n120→     */\\n    load(name: string, url: string): Promise<void>;\\n    /** Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music. */\\n    playMusic(song: Song | null, name?: string): void;\\n    stopMusic(): void;\\n    /**\\n     * Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the\\n     * recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\\n     */\\n    persist: boolean;\\n130→    setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void;\\n    setMuted(muted: boolean): void;\\n    toggleMute(): boolean;\\n    /** The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset). */\\n    resetVolumes(): void;\\n    /** Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song. */\\n    update(): void;\\n    /** Stop everything and close the AudioContext. */\\n    dispose(): void;\\n    private start;\\n140→    private bufferFor;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/audio/AudioManager.d.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":140,\"raw_output\":\"import { type Song } from './music';\\nimport { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\nexport type VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music';\\nexport interface AudioSettings {\\n    master: number;\\n    sfx: number;\\n    music: number;\\n    muted: boolean;\\n}\\nexport interface PlayOptions {\\n    /** 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume. */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo. */\\n    pitch?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n}\\nexport interface VoiceSettings {\\n    /** 0..1 (default 1). */\\n    volume?: number;\\n    /** Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right). */\\n    pan?: number;\\n    /** 0 (