cts (`SFX`, data), a small chiptune\\n * sequencer (`Song`, data), optional audio files, and master / sfx / music volume + mute\\n * persisted in localStorage. Available as `ctx.audio`.\\n *\\n * Browsers only allow sound after a user gesture, so the AudioContext is created on the\\n * first key / pointer / touch input. Before that, and wherever Web Audio does not exist\\n * (headless tests, old browsers), every call is a silent no-op that still records what\\n * was played in `log`, so games behave identically and tests can assert on sounds.\\n */\\nexport declare class AudioManager {\\n    /** Sound registry: the built-in SFX plus anything registered or loaded. */\\n    readonly sounds: Map<string, SoundDef>;\\n    /** The most recent plays (name and game-independent wall time), newest last; tooling/tests. */\\n    readonly log: {\\n        name: string;\\n        at: number;\\n    }[];\\n    /** How many times each sound was requested. */\\n    readonly counts: Record<string, number>;\\n    context: AudioContext | null;\\n    private master;\\n    private buses;\\n    private readonly settings;\\n    private readonly buffers;\\n    private readonly files;\\n    private readonly pendingFiles;\\n    private unlockListeners;\\n    private readonly lifetime;\\n    private song;\\n    private songName;\\n    private readonly musicSources;\\n    private readonly voices;\\n    /** Files being fetched or decoded (a loop waiting for one is not missing its sound). */\\n    private readonly loading;\\n    private disposed;\\n    constructor(target?: EventTarget | null);\\n    /** Web Audio exists in this environment. */\\n    static get supported(): boolean;\\n    /** True once the AudioContext is running (after the first user input). */\\n    get unlocked(): boolean;\\n    /** Current volumes and mute (persisted). */\\n    get volume(): Readonly<AudioSettings>;\\n    get muted(): boolean;\\n    /** Name of the playing song ('' when none). */\\n    get playing(): string;\\n    /**\\n     * Create / resume the AudioContext. Called automatically on the first user input; call\\n     * it yourself from a click handler if you start sound from your own UI.\\n\",\"total_lines\":144}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"b9dfcaa5-0e59-4a48-86dd-11e8a0feadbe"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_190","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The relevant audio volume value is `ctx.audio.volume.music`.\n\nA fresh profile defaults music volume to 0.1 if the key is absent. The headless fingerprint excludes idle time and uses a stable hash of mode, focus, and simulation state. Headless tests must run without a document.\n\nThe setup code must guard localStorage access. In Node it is undefined, so the catch block prevents errors and still sets the default music volume.\n\nCanvas textures will be skipped in headless mode. Tissue will use a solid toon color instead. The engine may guard localStorage writes during volume changes. I will write clinic.js as one large file with smaller helpers rather than many tiny modules.\n\nEleven files are planned: audio director, network session, stage meshes, HUD, game class, entry point, build tool, authoring guide, and tests. Story scene length is confirmed at 8–16 lines. The HUD charset covers letters, digits, and punctuation with a sanitize function for en-dashes. Menu items are being drafted.\n\nThe menu now lists five items: ON CALL, OPERATING LOG, CONSULT, SETTINGS, and ABOUT.\n\nON CALL opens a subfocus with New Shift and Continue options. New Shift clears the save and starts scene zero in story mode. Continue loads an existing save. Difficulty is selected as Intern, Resident, or Attending before the campaign begins.\n\nThe playable campaign flow runs through story scenes line by line. After the final line it either shows a brief or moves to credits.\n\nThe operating log runs until the shift ends, then shows results and advances to the next scene or credits. Credits write the save and return to the title.\n\nConsult mode has four seats. The keyboard occupies seat zero, pads join by index, and a circle leaves. Once seat one confirms start, the player picks an operation from the pile or cleared list while excluding op3 and op9. The game begins with consult mode enabled and the joined count passed as seats.\n\nA two-pad test may use direct query parameters such as ?seats=2&op=op1 instead of the consult menu. That path still uses couch mode with two independent tips and no network.\n\nSettings items include Music, Sound, Voices, Glyphs, Aim Assist, Cursor Speed, Rumble, Difficulty, and Reset. Left and right controls adjust values. Glyphs toggles between PS and Xbox layouts. The music slider writes to both the audio engine and local settings. Settings are only persisted when the player confirms changes inside the menu itself.\n\nThe pause screen offers Resume, Retry Stage, Restart, Settings, Dev Mode, and Quit. It opens with the Escape key during an operation but not during a session.\n\nDev mode has five tabs: balance, cheats, jump, actions, and view. L1 and R1 or Q and E switch between them.\n\nDev mode balance sliders use one row per setting with left and right nudges, and a final reset row. Cheats toggle god mode, composure, freeze, mistakes, and unlock via devCheat. Jump loads operations from a list. Actions trigger win, fail, complete, blue, or spawn pool. View toggles hitboxes, bot skill, and simulation speed.\n\nAttract mode starts after ten seconds of idle time on the title screen, menu, operating log, settings, about, or consult lobby. Story scenes, operations, pause, results, credits, cards, and sessions are excluded.\n\nThe attract reel runs op1 with god mode and bot skill 1, displaying a DEMO banner. It loops by restarting the operation after a win or 25 seconds of simulation time. The cycle rotates through op1, op2, op5, and the pile. Any input exits to the title screen while swallowing that input and skipping save writes.\n\nA touch-only gate shows a DOM overlay during attract mode. It disappears once a keyboard or pad connects.\n\nWhen session arguments are present, the game starts in card mode without a title screen. AIHub events are wired during setup, and ready() is called inside the ready hook. The host skips simulation stepping in fixedUpdate because the host uses its own interval.\n\nClients will not step the simulation in fixedUpdate. They apply snapshots and move a local cursor that the view reads for the local seat.\n\nThe host player starts the engine but also steps the simulation via setInterval. fixedUpdate must skip stepping to avoid double-stepping, and a sessionHost flag will track this state.\n\nThe server does not start the engine at all. It only steps the simulation via setInterval, and runServer runs before Engine is created.\n\nThe session loop will live in `src/net/session.js` so both the server and the clinic host can share it. `attachSession` will accept an AIHub and a set of hooks, returning a stop function, a local input setter, and a note function. The server path will call `runServer`, which creates a session world seeded from `AIHub.rng('deal')`, picks an operation from `SESSION_OPS` by round index, sets seats to the player count, enables consult mode, and wires up the input map.\n\nThe host will step the simulation for its own seat and any idle clients. Clients send idle input starting at zero, so the host applies those values. If the operation does not finish in six seconds, time-up ends it with standings. The harness test calls timeUp when the round is not finished, so finishing on time-up is the required path.\n\nThe traffic check runs before timeUp. It waits roughly 4.5 seconds after the round starts, then verifies bidirectional message exchange. Clients send at 15 Hz and the host sends snapshots at 12 Hz, so idle simulation still produces traffic. The host must keep stepping and sending snapshots during this window. Traffic checks occur only during active play, not while cards are being drawn.\n\nThe host must call finish exactly once on the time-up event to avoid double-finishing. When a player leaves, the present list updates and the last remaining player is removed if three or fewer remain; standings are computed from AIHub.players at that moment. Scores come from seat scores in the simulation, defaulting to zero. Stats entries use an id, a name, and a text field limited to sixteen characters. Awards include titles and details such as \"STEADIEST HAND\" for fewest mistakes.\n\nAwards are limited to present players and capped at three. Titles must be twenty-four characters or fewer, and details forty. A win is detected when the simulation phase equals 'won'. The server world sends input from players and snapshots back to them, finishing on time-up once it has sent messages. Clients apply decoded snapshots into a local simulation seeded identically so entity IDs match; they preserve their own cursor position from local input while restoring other seats from the decoded state. The message format carries seat index, x, z, tool, use, gift, steady, and snapshot coordinates.\n\nMessages are typed as snapshots, keyframes, events, or pings. Snapshots are unreliable; keyframes and events are reliable. The host is rate-limited to seventeen messages per second, including four card-phase pings. Clients send input at fifteen hertz even when idle. The host applies input by seat index, and the server has no local player. The host will not run a bot so it does not fight the player's arrow-key input.\n\nArrow keys will move the cursor directly without a bot interfering with human seats. Idle input is sent as empty input, so the operation may not finish before time-up and that is acceptable. The host interval can throttle to one second, but catch-up still sends messages; dropping below zero sent messages is fine. The server must not throw when creating operations because the session operation list is valid. The round index cycles through the operation list using modulo, and the seed is generated by flooring a random value from AIHub.rng('deal') scaled to one billion.\n\nSession gameplay must avoid Math.random to keep cursor noise out of the simulation. On end, music stops, loops halt, and the interval stops so no further sends occur. The Options menu is ignored during sessions. Enter on the card screen is handled by both a window listener and input.wasPressed.\n\nA window keydown listener will explicitly call AIHub.answer('ready') for Enter and 'more' for M during the card phase, in case the engine misses those events. Pad buttons such as triangle will be polled inside update() while the card is active. AIHub.ready() should be invoked from game.ready() after setup, without requiring a keypress.\n\nThe server calls ready immediately after starting the loop, and the harness waits for the card screen to appear before sending Enter. The window listener installed during setup will catch that keypress without issue. Engine.start is asynchronous and may take several seconds, so a twenty-second timeout is sufficient. Running four engine copies plus the harness in separate iframes risks exhausting WebGPU memory, but the plan indicates this passed in a prior spike, so it will be attempted.\n\nIf memory runs low, quality will be set to 'low' and debug UI, debug keys, touch, and recording will be disabled. The view will paint a large colored tissue plane from above against a dark blue-grey background, with a lamp mesh, using the specified arena colors.\n\nThe tissue plane is a 16-by-9 PlaneGeometry rotated down and placed at y=0. Entities are small spheres or boxes at heightAt, hands are boxes at seat coordinates, and busts are sphere groups for story mode; the title screen uses a lamp cylinder and a monitor box. During operation mode, mouse aim traces a camera ray from the pointer to the arena surface via rayAt and aimOnSurface, updating the seat position.\n\nThe `act` method steps the simulation for a single seat and syncs meshes. Launch tests rely on this path. A potential conflict with `fixedUpdate` was noted, but idle steps will not undo the drain placed by `act`. The launch URL starts directly in operation mode.\n\nThe launch URL parser is guarded against headless environments where `location` is absent. Glyph sprites will be small pixel patterns drawn with `hud.sprite`, and text labels will sit beside them so tests can read prompts from `__GAME__` instead of the canvas. A `fit` sanitizer normalizes dashes, ellipses, and strips invalid characters, though underscores may be omitted if the font lacks them.\n\nThe about screen will display five pages of feedback and credits, wrapping text at fifty-seven characters. Em-dashes are avoided in favor of regular hyphens to stay within the font set. A PROGRESS.md file will be written whenever the work root is touched, recording the title and current state.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eaeaae65-ab61-47b1-b3f9-347aee0b0a1f-67","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"rayAt\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/camera.d.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eaeaae65-ab61-47b1-b3f9-347aee0b0a1f-68","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/src/sim/op.js","offset":1080,"limit":50}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1048,"output_tokens":4114,"cache_read_input_tokens":117760,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"84bbfe65-4e3b-494f-96eb-f806168f1a47"}
