{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_195","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"God works via opts.god. Reviewing the stage jump URL.\n\nA `?stage=2` devCheat URL is needed after creation. A standalone `standingsOf` helper could be unit-tested in sim tests without a browser. This is optional if the harness passes.\n\nI will unit-test the standings helper in the default suite before running browsers. I am now writing all files carefully, avoiding forbidden strings like TODO and stub in code and comments. AUTHORING.md must document filters and how to add ops, scenes, strains, and characters. game.js must also be clean of forbidden names. The hand-in is the built HTML.\n\nThe dev index.html will keep loading game.js, which the build inlines. The esbuild bundle will include pixel-engine.js at 4.8MB. session.js is prepended verbatim as a global AIHub reference rather than bundled. The build script copies harness, bridge, round, and root HTML into dist/. Head tags and an SVG data URL icon will be embedded directly in the HTML string.\n\nThe favicon will be a hand-drawn 16×16 SVG with a red background and white cross. Meta tags will include a description, theme-color #1a1218, hub:type Game, and hub:source Trauma Center: Second Opinion. The session script must remain the first script after charset.\n\nThe session script will be escaped and placed right after the charset tag. The session file itself will not be minified. pixel-engine.js must remain untouched on disk so the kit matches the zip.\n\nI will run check.mjs after game.js is written. The existing lint test only walks src and docs, so it misses game.js in the dev root. I will add game.js to the walk explicitly to catch any forbidden words before shipping.\n\nThe campaign content test will import play to run all eleven ops at skill 1. Each op finishes quickly in simulation time because the bot uses place teleport. The test already covers skill 1 and 0.7 for every op, so the campaign adds only a small extra delay of roughly twenty to forty seconds. I will add a note about this.\n\nThe story charset test will enforce a strict font character set and cap each line at 57 characters, fitting the 342-pixel width. Scene lengths are limited to eight to sixteen lines.\n\nSOURCE_INFORMATION must contain all required keys with exact usage, the AI_HUB genre, and en-dash players. Songs will be validated in the audio test. parseSong behavior and spacing need verification before writing files. The Clinic class outline is being completed now. Engine calls fixedUpdate before update.\n\nInput sampling happens after physics in update, so it is one frame late. That is acceptable.\n\nMenu input uses wasPressed on the render frame inside update.\n\nThe session host skips fixedUpdate and steps the simulation directly. Clients do not step the authoritative simulation; they send input and move a local cursor for display until a snapshot arrives.\n\nBoth sides create a local simulation on startup with the same seed. They diverge until snapshots arrive.\n\nCard mode will display the title, ready players, goal, how-to, and remaining time. The session enters ready state when the start event fires and the timer reaches zero.\n\nIf the countdown is negative, the card or a steady screen stays active until time becomes non-negative.\n\nThe server runServer loop owns the simulation directly. It steps the shared sim object and sends snapshots without a view. The clinic does not step the authoritative simulation; it only renders snapshots.\n\nThe player host will share one simulation instance between the clinic and the loop via a module-level reference.\n\nThe session state object will hold the simulation, input buffers, and flags for started, ended, sent, and local state. In game.js, the player host creates both the clinic and the session together. It connects to the hub, opens the session, marks itself ready if a server exists, or fails if WebGPU is unavailable.\n\nopenSession starts its interval before the engine loads, so clients can send input during the card phase. The ready() call happens after engine initialization, which keeps the card visible until play begins. Both sides can ping and send input before the game actually starts.\n\nThe host will not send its own inputs to itself. It only receives snapshots from clients and sends them out. Clients must send their inputs to the host. The harness checks that every copy sent and received a non-zero count, so the host’s received tally will reflect client inputs rather than its own snapshots.\n\nI am checking whether the 5% simulated network loss applies to reliable keyframes. If it does not, the reliable snapshot should still arrive and both directions will pass the harness. Even with loss, most messages should survive over four seconds of 12Hz traffic.\n\n150 ms latency is safe because the 4.5 s play window easily absorbs it.\n\nThe openSession interval runs at 20 Hz, but the client will send input only every fourth tick to stay under the 15 Hz cap. Timestamps will track accumulated traffic across all message types.\n\nThe host will step the simulation at 30 Hz using a catch-up loop driven by AIHub.time(). It calculates how many 60 Hz steps to apply since the last tick. Snapshots, keyframes, and events are sent on separate timers to keep total traffic under 15 Hz.\n\nThe host simulation catch-up is capped at 30 steps per tick to prevent throttled intervals from overloading the loop. A token bucket rate limiter will enforce the 20 messages-per-second ceiling by accumulating tokens against wall-clock time, so hidden tabs cannot burst traffic.\n\nThe token bucket will use an 18/s cap to stay safely under the 20/s limit. Input buffers will be keyed by player ID, with the host’s local input updated each frame by the clinic. The simulation is created only when the start event fires, so the seed and player list are finalized before stepping begins; clients need it to apply snapshots. Before that point no simulation exists and no snapshots are sent. During the card phase the host will send ping messages at 4 Hz.\n\nPings and input messages will run during the card phase so both directions show early traffic. The leave handler deletes a player’s input buffer without finishing the game. The time-up handler calls finish(false) on the host. Win or loss after a step triggers finish. The end handler sets the ended flag, clears the timer, and silences hooks.\n\nThe finish function gathers scores from the simulation seats for each player and sorts them into standings. It only runs on the host and ignores duplicate calls.\n\nThe finish function groups tied scores into sub-arrays. It builds a stats array with SCORE and MISTAKES for each player, capped at sixteen characters. It awards STEADIEST HAND to the first player. Finally it calls hub.finish with standings, scores, a win flag, stats, and awards.\n\nA zero-score tie is valid and satisfies checkStandings. The scores object will be a plain object with string keys. A probe function will report scene, menu, world, and input state. WebGPU failure is only relevant when the session starts the engine; the server does not.\n\nA touch-only device detector will use matchMedia and navigator checks to identify touchscreens without a fine pointer or gamepad. If the device is touch-only, a gate overlay will block startup until the user confirms they want to proceed.\n\nThe touch gate overlay will display a centered message and remain transparent to pointer events so the settings button stays clickable. It will dismiss automatically when a keyboard key is pressed or a gamepad connects. The settings button will be positioned at the top-right corner only on touch devices.\n\nThe context menu is suppressed on the app element. Tab is blocked from input and gamepad buttons are cleared. A fresh-audio flag checks localStorage to set initial music volume. The operation can begin before meshes exist because syncing handles null values.\n\nThe ready function exposes an API object through window.__GAME__ that wraps the game instance, simulation, mode switching, configuration, input actions, prompt state, audio volume, and engine. It also calls AIHub.ready when a session exists.\n\nThe goto method supports test-driven navigation. It can return to the title from any mode or enter pause only if a simulation is active.\n\nThe act method steps the simulation for seat 0 by default and returns an entity snapshot. The goto method can also return to the title from the dev mode. Browser tests will be imported by run.mjs when filtered for browser, launch, or hub; they require Playwright and the built HTML. The default test suite skips them for speed, but verification requires running them after build.\n\nThe default test command must include content checks, simulation tests, and audio verification. Launch is a separate step that loads the root HTML twice.\n\nLaunch, browser, hub, and package checks will each have dedicated filters in the test runner. Launch writes scratch files. Browser writes browser.txt. Hub writes hub.txt. Package checks run via a package filter and produce package.txt. The verifier audits committed test outputs.\n\nPackage checks will be folded into the default test suite or a dedicated `package` filter. The script will record zip hashes and JSON output in package.txt, and include an assertion that verifies those hashes match so the verifier can catch mismatches on rerun.\n\nPackage hash checks will be deferred until after the exit interview is written, since they depend on that file. The content test will then verify that the JSON exists in the work root and matches about.js.\n\nA `shots` test filter will generate screenshots at 1920x1080 for the title screen, all ten arenas, the bust view, the HUD, and the results stamp. Each arena will be loaded via a query parameter such as `?op=op1&bot=0`. These images will be produced during the browser or launch run and saved under scratch.\n\nA `?screen=results&op=op1` URL will trigger an instant devCheat win for the results screenshot, avoiding a slow full playthrough. The plan already lists query parameters for operations, stages, difficulty, seed, bots, god mode, attract mode, and scene.\n\nThe screenshot script will trigger an instant devCheat win via page.evaluate before capturing the results image, so no new URL parameter is needed. The planned implementation order is: validate songs, write devices, director, session, stage, HUD, clinic, and game.js, then update AUTHORING and extend tests, run the content, audio, and simulation test suite, build, run headless checks, execute browser, launch, and hub tests, and finally fix any bugs.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-92c1ab87-5806-4ab9-91db-f403f811e13f-74","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function renderSound|export function renderSound|export declare function renderSound","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/audio/synth.d.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-92c1ab87-5806-4ab9-91db-f403f811e13f-75","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/src/audio/voice.js","offset":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":814,"output_tokens":5762,"cache_read_input_tokens":128768,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"7f39b230-bb59-4480-a52f-4fe223149906"}
