{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-51922ebc-f6d9-4d2d-b907-a04f730e731c-76","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\n/** A track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those). */\\nexport type Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>;\\n/**\\n * A chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\\n * where a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\\n * Every track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\\n * note's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\\n */\\n10→export interface Song {\\n    bpm: number;\\n    /** Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4. */\\n    stepsPerBeat?: number;\\n    tracks: Record<string, Instrument>;\\n    patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>;\\n    /** Pattern names in play order. */\\n    order: readonly string[];\\n    /** Loop forever (default true). */\\n    loop?: boolean;\\n20→}\\nexport interface SongNote {\\n    track: string;\\n    /** Step index from the start of the song. */\\n    step: number;\\n    /** Length in steps (1 + following holds). */\\n    steps: number;\\n    freq: number;\\n}\\nexport interface ParsedSong {\\n30→    /** Seconds per step. */\\n    stepTime: number;\\n    /** Total steps in one pass of `order`. */\\n    length: number;\\n    notes: SongNote[];\\n}\\n/** Flatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos. */\\nexport declare function parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong;\\n/** The playable SoundDef for one note of a track. */\\nexport declare function noteSound(instrument: Instrument, freq: number, seconds: number): SoundDef;\\n40→/** The playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps. */\\nexport declare const PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\n/** A track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those). */\\nexport type Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>;\\n/**\\n * A chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\\n * where a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\\n * Every track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\\n * note's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\\n */\\n10→export interface Song {\\n    bpm: number;\\n    /** Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4. */\\n    stepsPerBeat?: number;\\n    tracks: Record<string, Instrument>;\\n    patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>;\\n    /** Pattern names in play order. */\\n    order: readonly string[];\\n    /** Loop forever (default true). */\\n    loop?: boolean;\\n20→}\\nexport interface SongNote {\\n    track: string;\\n    /** Step index from the start of the song. */\\n    step: number;\\n    /** Length in steps (1 + following holds). */\\n    steps: number;\\n    freq: number;\\n}\\nexport interface ParsedSong {\\n30→    /** Seconds per step. */\\n    stepTime: number;\\n    /** Total steps in one pass of `order`. */\\n    length: number;\\n    notes: SongNote[];\\n}\\n/** Flatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos. */\\nexport declare function parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong;\\n/** The playable SoundDef for one note of a track. */\\nexport declare function noteSound(instrument: Instrument, freq: number, seconds: number): SoundDef;\\n40→/** The playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps. */\\nexport declare const PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/audio/music.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"import { type SoundDef } from './synth';\\n/** A track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those). */\\nexport type Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>;\\n/**\\n * A chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\\n * where a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\\n * Every track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\\n * note's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\\n */\\nexport interface Song {\\n    bpm: number;\\n    /** Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4. */\\n    stepsPerBeat?: number;\\n    tracks: Record<string, Instrument>;\\n    patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>;\\n    /** Pattern names in play order. */\\n    order: readonly string[];\\n    /** Loop forever (default true). */\\n    loop?: boolean;\\n}\\nexport interface SongNote {\\n    track: string;\\n    /** Step index from the start of the song. */\\n    step: number;\\n    /** Length in steps (1 + following holds). */\\n    steps: number;\\n    freq: number;\\n}\\nexport interface ParsedSong {\\n    /** Seconds per step. */\\n    stepTime: number;\\n    /** Total steps in one pass of `order`. */\\n    length: number;\\n    notes: SongNote[];\\n}\\n/** Flatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos. */\\nexport declare function parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong;\\n/** The playable SoundDef for one note of a track. */\\nexport declare function noteSound(instrument: Instrument, freq: number, seconds: number): SoundDef;\\n/** The playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps. */\\nexport declare const PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song;\\n\",\"total_lines\":41}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"67df3806-3227-42f7-afa8-0923605b1ccd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_197","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Running parseSong first, then writing all the files.\n\nChecking note counts for each part. The calm lead was 14 notes and needs one more D4 to reach 16.\n\nTense, critical, and boss parts all check out at 16 notes. Boss lead is only 15.\n\nBoss lead is now 16 notes. Sparse lead and bass are also fixed at 16. Fanfare lead is 13 notes and fanfare bass is 14.\n\nFanfare lead and bass are now 16 notes. Sad lead, bass, and REST all check out at 16. All parts are fixed. Writing files in parallel batches.\n\nChecking devCheat spawn arguments. Clinic meshes will be pooled and reused rather than rebuilt. The fill-mode canvas will cover the full viewport without letterboxing. I'll expose the engine to the test for size checks.\n\nThe test will verify the canvas fills the viewport by checking its bounding rectangle against window dimensions. Integer scaling at 16:9 covers the screen without letterbox bars.\n\nI'll record the art dimensions and canvas size in launch.txt. The test will assert the canvas fills the viewport within 2 pixels.\n\nThe tissue fill will use a mid-brightness color so the mean pixel value is clearly above zero on painted frames. The scripted act sequence will be replayed twice to compare entity states and simulation hashes in launch.txt. A few idle frames before the act may differ between runs due to timing.\n\nThe test will read the entity's position before the act, then call act() immediately. Idle drift is unreliable because fixedUpdate timing varies by load time. The agreement criterion is that both runs produce the same aimed entity state after the act.\n\nA race between act() and concurrent fixedUpdate steps makes exact hash comparison unreliable. The test will instead verify that both runs change the same entity in the same direction and satisfy the same checks, such as music level and painted state.\n\nA `hold` flag will pause the simulation clock during the test. The launch URL will include `bot=0`. The test will set `app.simHold = true`, record a hash, act drain 30 times, and record the final hash and entity position.\n\nA `hold` query flag will be added for tests only. It pauses the simulation clock so the launch test can act immediately without idle drift. This flag is not part of the normal `?bot=` family and will be documented in AUTHORING.\n\nThe verifier audits committed tests and saved evidence rather than rebuilding them, so the launch test must be a genuine run. The default page will remain playable without the hold flag.\n\nThe game will check for `hold=1` in the URL to pause the simulation clock. Op1 places the first pool at x=-3.1, z=-1.1 and drains it. The settings screen music slider defaults to 10% volume. Dev sliders will list all default values. Nudge increments are mapped per setting: 0.1 for multipliers, 0.05 for bleed and steady factors, and 1 for integers.\n\nThe nudge step map now covers tool tolerance, drain rate, laser time, syringe dose and draw, composure gain, quiet scale and drain, look cost and duration, strain speed and HP, dies BPM, blue zone, and coop scale. Reset will restore all settings to defaults. The code imports Engine, withUrlOptions, THREE, toonMaterial, and setLookLayer from the pixel engine.\n\nThe import list now includes Mesh, PlaneGeometry, SphereGeometry, BoxGeometry, CylinderGeometry, AmbientLight, DirectionalLight, CanvasTexture, NearestFilter, Vector3, and Group from THREE. A Group will hold the bust meshes. stage.js will handle building and updating those meshes. The fingerprint method combines mode, focus, line, and a simulation hash to identify the current state.\n\nThe title screen fingerprint is stable because the simulation is absent there. The status method will show mode and focus when no simulation exists, or the full simulation state including stage, vitals, time left, and score. A cameraTarget Vector3 will be stored on the instance. HUD text will wrap at 57 characters per line with a two-line limit for communication messages. Glyph sprites are also being prepared.\n\nCrosshair glyph sprites are defined as ASCII arrays. The attract banner will display exactly \"DEMO - PRESS ANY BUTTON\". Touch text must match precisely. The hub's input check for Enter and Space keys needs to handle the title-to-menu transition correctly.\n\nMenu confirm will open the call screen on the first press and start the story on the second. Space on the title screen enters the menu, while ArrowDown navigates there. The test will press Enter to enter the menu, then ArrowDown to move focus.\n\nOnly key presses, clicks, or pad buttons will exit the title screen. Mouse movement alone will not trigger the transition. This prevents pointer jitter from accidentally skipping the attract timer and makes hover tests on the title possible.\n\nMouse movement will reset the attract idle timer only when the delta is significant and the pointer is over the canvas. This prevents browser synthetic mouse events from accidentally skipping the attract sequence.\n\nThe plan explicitly includes mouse movement as input, so the attract idle timer resets on pointer motion. The test must avoid moving the mouse to prevent accidental resets; a tiny delta at startup may reset once, but sustained stillness will start attract. Exiting attract will swallow the current input so it does not also activate the title screen. writeSave will be skipped on boot, and the game will not use fetch or WebSocket.\n\nThe audio test will import pixel-engine.js, which is large but acceptable. The content test campaign calls play() synchronously and may block briefly, but it returns the simulation at the end. Story text uses apostrophes that match the font charset. The about screen wraps its long research text.\n\nThe font charset includes commas, periods, parentheses, and hyphens. The exit-interview JSON will retain the en-dash in the players field. The about screen and credits page will use a hyphen instead.\n\nTesting feedback will be updated only if new bugs appear. The current counts are 7 issues and 4 critical. Session.js does not need to import pixel-engine directly because game.js already loads it through the clinic module.\n\nThe pixel-engine module is safe to import in a browser because it only initializes WebGPU after Engine.start, and it does not touch the document during import. Clinic will import THREE directly from pixel-engine.js inside view/stage.js and clinic.js; multiple imports of the same module are harmless. The dependency graph avoids cycles: session.js imports op.js, ops.js, codec.js, and bot.js, while clinic imports session.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-47a37bcb-3287-477c-9917-45bb4aff2416-77","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"input\\.table|input\\.angle|input\\.gift","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/src/sim/op.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":725,"output_tokens":4611,"cache_read_input_tokens":135552,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"b66c7d5c-ff0d-47d3-ab7d-02239f64236f"}
