{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_395","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Re-examining fixedUpdate.\n\nFound a bug in fixedUpdate. When only keyboard is used, padInputs is empty and non-drive seats receive bot input instead of humanInput. This breaks couch consult with keyboard and pads.\n\nKeyboard and pad inputs both map to seat 0, so only one player can join. This is a design limitation. The two-pad test likely works correctly.\n\nKeyboard and mouse inputs both map to seat 0, so a plugged-in controller overrides the keyboard during solo play. This is a real UX bug for couch and TV setups where the user expects keyboard and mouse to remain available.\n\nThe last-device system tracks mouse input, but fixedUpdate still routes it to padInputs[0] when a controller is connected. Mouse movement therefore cannot move the cursor while a pad is present. This directly violates the \"mouse aims\" requirement.\n\nConfirmed: padInputs[0] always wins for seat 0, even when the last device is mouse. The engine clears gamepadButtons every frame to prevent keyboard key presses from being synthesized.\n\nChecking the engine's input polling order. If the engine reads gamepadButtons before update() clears them, pad buttons might still map to keyboard keys. The timing between engine polling and the frame reset is critical.\n\nThe comment in devices.js confirms pads are read directly and not merged by the engine. The browser test for prompt swapping may already cover the pad-to-keyboard concern.\n\nA clearer bug was spotted in `enterScene`. After setting `this.mode = 'story'`, the subsequent `this.mode === 'attract'` check is unreachable because the mode was just changed, so the attract guard is dead code.\n\nThe dead attract guard in `enterScene` is harmless because attract mode calls `beginOp`, not `enterScene`. REELS are operations, not scenes.\n\nNow checking the HUD COMP position. `drawOp` places it at x=8 with top-right anchor. The engine text anchor handling is minified, so `Hud.d.ts` will be checked instead.\n\nThe screenshot shows COMP at the top right, but the source code places it at x=8 with a top-right anchor. If the engine uses top-left as default, the text would end up off-screen. This suggests the bundled HTML differs from the current source. The root HTML is the deliverable, so the running page must be verified against that bundle.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8e9bc24d-bfbd-467f-be0c-803591b93bb6-58","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/types/engine/hud/Hud.d.ts","limit":120}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8e9bc24d-bfbd-467f-be0c-803591b93bb6-59","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"COMP","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/dist/bundle.js"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8e9bc24d-bfbd-467f-be0c-803591b93bb6-60","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"anchor","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224040__x24ebb3/work/The-Second-Opinion-Clinic-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224040/GUIDE.md","head_limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3873,"output_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":113920,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa39-3d19-7772-a082-6fbf4c1e0ec4","uuid":"ca88f563-504e-4919-8046-4c415daa8d04"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8e9bc24d-bfbd-467f-be0c-803591b93bb6-58","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import type { Framing, Resolution } from '../framing';\\nimport { type PaletteColor } from '../palette';\\n/** Where an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left). */\\nexport type HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right';\\nexport type HudColor = PaletteColor | number;\\nexport interface HudOptions {\\n    /** Default 'white'. */\\n    color?: HudColor;\\n    /** 1-pixel drop shadow color, or false. Default 'ink'. */\\n10→    shadow?: HudColor | false;\\n    /** Integer art-pixel scale (2 = double-size glyphs). Default 1. */\\n    scale?: number;\\n    /** Default 'top-left': x/y are offsets from that screen corner/edge, in art pixels. */\\n    anchor?: HudAnchor;\\n}\\n/**\\n * Pixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\\n * the post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\\n * laid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\\n20→ * (`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\\n *\\n * Retained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\\n * (clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\\n * resolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\\n *\\n *   ctx.hud.clear();\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\\n *   ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\\n */\\n30→export declare class Hud {\\n    private readonly container;\\n    /** The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn). */\\n    canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null;\\n    visible: boolean;\\n    private items;\\n    private drawn;\\n    private size;\\n    private layoutKey;\\n    constructor(container: HTMLElement | null);\\n40→    /** HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution). */\\n    get width(): number;\\n    get height(): number;\\n    /** Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation). */\\n    text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** A filled rectangle (panels, bars). */\\n    rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor?: HudAnchor): void;\\n    /**\\n     * A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped\\n     * through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\\n50→     *\\n     *   hud.sprite(4, 4, ['.yy.', 'ywyy', 'yyyy', '.yy.'], { y: 'sand', w: 'white' });\\n     */\\n    sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options?: HudOptions): void;\\n    /** Remove everything. */\\n    clear(): void;\\n    /** Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`. */\\n    measure(text: string, scale?: number): {\\n        width: number;\\n        height: number;\\n60→    };\\n    /** Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed. */\\n    sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void;\\n    dispose(): void;\\n    private createCanvas;\\n    private paint;\\n    private glyphs;\\n    private place;\\n}\\n/**\\n70→ * Top-left corner, in whole art pixels, of a w×h element placed at offset (x, y) from\\n * `anchor` on a screen of `size` art pixels. Pure (unit-tested).\\n */\\nexport declare function hudPlace(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, anchor: HudAnchor, size: Resolution): [number, number];\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import type { Framing, Resolution } from '../framing';\\nimport { type PaletteColor } from '../palette';\\n/** Where an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left). 